window.CONCALL_DATA = [
  {"name":"Tata Consultancy Services Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"8 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"No revenue or earnings guidance (\"we don't provide any specific revenue or earnings guidance\"). Long-term operating margin aspiration of 26%–28% maintained. The CFO declined to confirm a 25% FY27 exit, saying only \"inching up\".","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue","v":"₹73,188 crore, +1.3% QoQ, +11.2% YoY"},{"m":"Revenue in USD","v":"$7,642 million, +20 bps QoQ, +2.4% YoY"},{"m":"Constant currency growth","v":"+0.5% QoQ, +2.8% YoY"},{"m":"Operating margin","v":"24%, stable QoQ"},{"m":"Net margin","v":"19%"},{"m":"International revenue growth","v":"+1.2% QoQ cc"},{"m":"UK growth","v":"+3.5% QoQ cc"},{"m":"BFSI growth","v":"+2.5% QoQ, +3.9% YoY cc"},{"m":"Manufacturing growth","v":"+3.1% QoQ, +4.2% YoY"},{"m":"Technology and Services growth","v":"+3.1% QoQ, +4.8% YoY"},{"m":"Consumer Business","v":"−0.7% QoQ, −1% YoY"},{"m":"India and Regional Markets","v":"India +6%; Regional Markets +3.6% YoY"},{"m":"TCV","v":"US$9.6 billion, excluding Porsche and Best Buy"},{"m":"Annualised AI revenue","v":"$3.1 billion; over 10% of revenue"},{"m":"Workforce","v":"598,056 associates"},{"m":"LTM IT services attrition","v":"13.3%, down 30 bps QoQ"},{"m":"DSO","v":"74 days, constant QoQ"},{"m":"Cash conversion","v":"102.2% of net income"},{"m":"Invested funds","v":"$5.98 billion"},{"m":"Learning hours","v":"17.1 million, +17% QoQ"}],"takeaways":["25% exit margin not confirmed: Asked three times about exiting FY27 near 25%, the CFO said \"we'll continue to endeavor to inch up on our margins… While it's an uphill task\". Investments for growth remain the near-term priority.","H2 margin headwinds: The CFO expects MHP acquisition dilution of \"about 50 basis points depending on the timing of closure\". Furlough seasonality and continued ecosystem investments are further H2 headwinds.","India project deferral: An analyst cited a roughly 10% India revenue decline. The CEO attributed the decrease to \"a project deferral\", \"not related to any third-party or hardware pass-through\". He expects it to come back in the medium term without giving a date.","Demand environment unchanged: The CEO said demand \"has not materially changed since last quarter\", with discretionary programs \"under scrutiny\". He declined to comment on next quarter but is confident growth momentum continues \"on a medium-term basis\".","Strategic acquisitions and GCC: The Porsche partnership and proposed MHP acquisition await regulatory approvals. The CEO said MHP and the Best Buy GCC are about helping clients become \"AI-first\", not about productivity."],"revenue":["International broad-based growth: International revenue grew 1.2% QoQ in constant currency. The UK grew 3.5%, North America and Continental Europe 0.4% each. The CEO expects North America \"to recover and become healthy soon\".","Vertical spread: BFSI grew 2.5% QoQ, and Manufacturing and Technology and Services 3.1% each. Consumer fell 0.7% on inflation and insourcing, ERU fell 0.5%, Communications was \"broadly flat\", and Life Sciences grew 0.3%.","AI revenue scaling: Annualised AI revenue reached $3.1 billion, over 10% of revenue, and \"coming in at a much higher margin than our company average\". BFSI, Manufacturing and Life Sciences lead AI growth.","AI deflation not complete: In BFSI and Hi-Tech, the CEO said \"I don't want to conclude by saying that the deflation part is complete\", but AI modernization and vendor consolidation give \"confidence that the growth can continue\".","Regional market volatility: The CEO said BSNL \"can provide a cushion for the next couple of quarters\", but regional markets will stay volatile and lumpy. One account fell below the $100 million band on currency movement."],"profit":["Investment-led margin pressure: The CFO said investments in partnerships, M&A initiatives, niche hiring, a higher bench and subcontractors were partly offset by currency gains and operating leverage. Operating margin held at 24%.","Gross margin and subcontracting: The CFO said subcontractor and third-party costs hit gross margin, from \"near-term demand-supply skill mismatches\", with some link to the visa fee hike. Subcontracting is \"definitely one of the levers\" toward 26%.","Lower bonus outlay: The CFO attributed the employee cost decline partly to \"a lower outlay in performance-linked bonus\" given performance against internal targets, alongside workforce and location mix.","Pricing broadly stable: The CFO said there may be \"some front-loading of productivity benefits\", but AI revenue is priced better. Revenue per employee saw \"a slight reduction\", and portfolio pricing is \"broadly stable\".","Cash and capital allocation: Cash conversion was 102.2% of net income, with invested funds of $5.98 billion and DSO steady at 74 days. The capital allocation policy is \"unchanged\", returning substantial free cash flow."],"pipeline":["Order book: TCV was US$9.6 billion, excluding the Porsche and Best Buy deals. The CEO said win rates improved.","Five investment areas: The CFO listed capability acquisitions, an AI, hyperscaler and semiconductor partnership ecosystem, high-growth service areas, talent, and new engines such as data centers, GCCs, mid-market, ServiceNow, public services and sovereign cloud.","Data center timeline: The CFO said data center capacity will be built once contracts come in, with \"about 18 to 24 months before the revenues start kicking in\".","New AI launches: The company launched TCS ADD AgentHub for drug discovery, agentic control planes, an Autonomous Engineering Lab powered by NVIDIA in Bengaluru, and an AI-first lights-out factory lab in Pune.","Hiring to demand: The CHRO said 10,000 graduates were onboarded this year and campus hiring for next year is in final stages. \"We will continue to hire to staff those positions.\" Learning hours rose 17% QoQ to 17.1 million."],"commitments":[{"c":"Long-term margin band","s":"26%–28% \"guiding beacon\"; \"we are going to keep it\"","t":"Long term","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 margin trajectory","s":"\"Inch up\" from 24%; 25% exit not confirmed; \"uphill task\"","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MHP margin dilution","s":"About 50 bps, depending on closure timing","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MHP acquisition","s":"Subject to regulatory approvals","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"India deferred revenue","s":"Expected back; \"We don't want to put a particular date\"","t":"\"medium term\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"BSNL contribution","s":"Cushion for regional markets","t":"\"next couple of quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q3 furloughs","s":"\"Very similar to the previous years\"","t":"Q3 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"North America recovery","s":"Expected \"to recover and become healthy soon\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Data center revenue","s":"Revenue begins 18 to 24 months after contract","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Capital allocation","s":"Unchanged; return substantial free cash flows","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Inflation, energy costs and selective consumer spending in retail, continued telecom pressure, AI-related cybersecurity incidents, and volatile, lumpy regional market deliveries.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 8 Oct 2026; exchange cover letter p1, physical page numbers used). Guidance p3, p15–16, p20–21, p27; K1 p16–17, p20–21; K2 p16; K3 p13–14, p28; K4 p4, p12; K5 p4, p24–25; R1 p3, p23; R2 p3, p11; R3 p4, p19, p22; R4 p13; R5 p14, p17; P1 p5; P2 p13, p15–16; P3 p21; P4 p20; P5 p6; I1 p3, p12; I2 p5–6; I3 p22; I4 p4; I5 p10, p26, p28; C1 p15, p27; C2 p16, p20–21; C3 p16; C4 p4; C5 p14, p28; C6 p17; C7 p17; C8 p23; C9 p22; C10 p6; M1 p4; M2 p4; M3 p5; M4 p5; M5 p6; M6 p3; M7 p3; M8 p10; M9 p10; M10 p11; M11 p11; M12 p3; M13 p3; M14 p4; M15 p9; M16 p10; M17 p6; M18 p6; M19 p6; M20 p10; Risks p8, p11, p17. Analyst-cited figures not used as management data: AI revenue growth of 19% and freshers of 14,000 last quarter."},
  {"name":"HCL Technologies Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"12 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"FY27 CC revenue growth now 2.5%–4%, including 87 bps inorganic; organic midpoint \"broadly unchanged\". Services 3%–4%. EBIT margin 17.5%–18.5% maintained. CEO declined to raise it.","metrics":[{"m":"Total revenue","v":"$3,724 million, +2.3% QoQ, +2.6% YoY CC"},{"m":"Services revenue","v":"$3,442 million, +3% QoQ, +4% YoY"},{"m":"EBIT","v":"$677 million; margin 18.2%, +131 bps QoQ, +71 bps YoY"},{"m":"EBIT margin ex-restructuring","v":"19% vs 17.5% in previous quarter"},{"m":"Net income","v":"$538 million, 14.4% of revenue"},{"m":"LTM normalized diluted EPS","v":"INR70.22, +12.2% YoY; INR67.56 including labor codes"},{"m":"ITBS and ERS","v":"ITBS +2.7% QoQ, +4.4% YoY; ERS +4.1% QoQ, +2.1% YoY"},{"m":"HCLSoftware revenue","v":"$294 million, -5.8% QoQ, -11.2% YoY"},{"m":"HCLSoftware ARR","v":"$1.134 billion, +7.8% YoY; 2.6% excluding Jaspersoft"},{"m":"Advanced AI revenue","v":"$219 million, +28.4% QoQ, +78% YoY; annualized $876 million"},{"m":"New deal bookings","v":"$3.3 billion, +27% YoY, +35% QoQ"},{"m":"Inorganic revenue contribution","v":"$35.6 million; aided sequential growth by 97 bps"},{"m":"Geography growth YoY","v":"USA +3.5%, Europe -0.3%, ROW +11.4%, India +21.8%"},{"m":"Headcount","v":"230,339, net increase of 6,450 QoQ"},{"m":"LTM attrition","v":"12.8%"},{"m":"LTM ROIC","v":"41.2%, +261 bps YoY; Services 49.4%; Software 19.2%"},{"m":"LTM cash flow","v":"FCF $1.78 billion; OCF $1.96 billion; FCF/NI 87%"},{"m":"Cash (30 Sep)","v":"Gross $2.66 billion; net $2.56 billion"},{"m":"DSO including unbilled","v":"84 days, down 2 days QoQ"},{"m":"Dividend","v":"INR12 interim; LTM payout INR60, 88.6% of net income"}],"takeaways":["Guidance not raised despite quarter: Asked why the top end was not raised, the CEO said \"we are in a volatile world, and we don't want to increase our guidance\". For Services, \"we feel confident to be slightly above the midpoint\".","Mega deal revenue deferred: The CEO said the mega deal \"is not going to contribute to any revenue in Q3 or Q4. It is going to come in Q1 of next year.\" He said H2 caution reflects a volatile environment.","Software decline from renewal timing: Fewer multi-year renewals came due. Multi-year subscription revenue fell from 81 million in Q2 FY26 to 39 million. The CFO said the decline \"does not accurately reflect the underlying health\".","Anthropic Premier partnership: The company announced a global Premier partnership with Anthropic, with a dedicated business unit and Center of Excellence. HCLSoftware becomes an Anthropic ISV partner, embedding Claude and Claude Code.","Margins over invest phase: The CEO said 18% to 19% is the \"desired band\". He would not commit to it now, preferring to \"invest to gain more capability and differentiation, rather than optimize margins in this phase\"."],"revenue":["Vertical and client mix: 5 of 7 verticals grew YoY, led by Retail and CPG (14%) and Financial Services (7.1%). YoY, the company added 1 client at $100 million-plus, 8 at $50 million-plus, 10 at $20 million-plus and 33 at $10 million-plus.","Inorganic contribution: HPE Telco Solutions contributed approximately 21 million over two months, adding 62 bps to services sequential growth. Jaspersoft added approximately 15 million for the full quarter.","Top-client ramp-downs played out: Problems at three top-20 clients (two telcos, one manufacturer) were factored into guidance and \"has already played out\". The CEO added \"you cannot be sure\" about telco discretionary spend.","Advanced AI deal structure: The CEO said the two largest Advanced AI deals were single-sourced through partners. Competition comes from boutiques and, in agentic AI, from many service providers. Operating model transformation deals include fixed-price contracts; agentic work is largely T&M.","Software ARR vs revenue: The CEO said ARR grew 2.6% YoY excluding Jaspersoft, despite a 15% revenue decline. The Software President expects many multi-year renewals in FY28 and FY29."],"profit":["EBIT margin bridge: Project Ascend added 73 bps, Advanced AI margin 32 bps, SG&A leverage 50 bps and forex 23 bps. Higher restructuring cost 22 bps and the HPE Telco acquisition 25 bps were headwinds.","Advanced AI margin profile: The CEO said IP and platform gross margins will be \"upwards of 70%\". Advanced AI services margins will be \"at least 7% to 10% higher than traditional services\".","FCF conversion one-offs: The CFO said a BAPA tax payment and a move to an external gratuity trust hit LTM cash. FCF-to-NI should revert to \"maybe 100% plus\" as these roll off.","Software margin recovery: Software margins rose sequentially because Q1's one-off restructuring did not recur, alongside employee cost optimisation and lower amortisation. The CFO said Software costs will be rationalised to match growth.","PERM suspension immaterial: The CEO said the company has filed \"a very small number\" of PERM applications and expects no material impact from the US Department of Labor's suspension."],"pipeline":["Pipeline and demand: The CEO described the pipeline as strong and balanced, with healthy demand late in the quarter. \"The environment has been fluid and very dynamic… so we remain cautious.\"","AI platform and IP: AI Force Agent Store now has 900-plus agents, and the company has built more than 20 industry AI solutions. Agentic AI offerings crossed $100 million per quarter, and AI factory grew 64% QoQ.","Acquisitions: The company completed the acquisition of HPE's Telco Solutions Group and announced its intent to acquire Robotiq.ai, a Croatia-based RPA platform, for HCL UnO.","India infrastructure build-out: Plans include the first AI data center in Bhubaneswar with Sarvam and the Government of Odisha, Global Technology Centers in Bhubaneswar and GIFT City, and an INR185 crore semiconductor lab in Bengaluru.","New units and partnerships: HCLTech Pulse launched for enterprises with $500 million to $5 billion in revenue. The company became an OpenAI Advanced Partner and added offerings with NVIDIA, Google Cloud, ServiceNow and Cohere.","AI talent programs: Forward Deployed Engineer capability is scaling, with a centralized FDE Academy, an MIT applied research program and a Microsoft frontier transformation engineer program."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 company revenue growth","s":"2.5% to 4% CC, includes 87 bps inorganic; organic midpoint \"broadly unchanged\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 Services revenue growth","s":"3% to 4% CC, includes 62 bps inorganic; \"slightly above the midpoint\" expected","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 EBIT margin","s":"17.5% to 18.5%; \"remains\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Anthropic business unit","s":"Dedicated Anthropic business unit and Center of Excellence; HCLSoftware to embed Claude","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Robotiq.ai acquisition","s":"Intent to acquire announced; for HCL UnO orchestration","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Bhubaneswar AI data center","s":"First AI data center, with Sarvam and Government of Odisha","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Mega deal revenue start","s":"No revenue in Q3 or Q4","t":"\"Q1 of next year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FCF-to-NI conversion","s":"Revert to \"maybe 100% plus\" as one-offs roll off LTM","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Software renewals","s":"Multi-year renewals \"will come back\"","t":"FY28 and FY29","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q3 Software step-up","s":"\"Q3 will be a strong quarter due to the Software step-up\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Medium-term margin band","s":"18% to 19% \"desired band\"; declined to commit now","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Interim dividend payment","s":"INR12 per share; record date October 16, 2026","t":"October 26, 2026","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The CEO cited a \"fluid\" and \"volatile\" environment and recalled how last year's Middle East conflict changed conditions overnight. \"Things can change fast.\"","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 12 Oct 2026; physical page numbers). Guidance p12, p15, p20; K1 p20; K2 p24; K3 p13; K4 p10; K5 p25–26; R1 p14; R2 p13; R3 p21; R4 p17, p20; R5 p22–23; F1 p14; F2 p19; F3 p18; F4 p21–22; F5 p5; P1 p11–12; P2 p7–8; P3 p7, p11; P4 p9–11; P5 p10–11; P6 p10–11; C1 p12, p15; C2 p12, p15, p20; C3 p12, p15; C4 p10; C5 p7; C6 p9–10; C7 p24; C8 p18; C9 p23; C10 p20; C11 p26; C12 p15; M1 p12; M2 p12; M3 p5, p14; M4 p14; M5 p14; M6 p15; M7 p12–13; M8 p13; M9 p4, p22; M10 p4; M11 p5; M12 p13; M13 p13–14; M14–M15 p5; M16–M19 p14–15; M20 p15; Risks p20."},
  {"name":"Heritage Foods Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"No numeric revenue or margin guidance. The CEO declined to forecast milk prices (\"speculative, I do not wish to make any comment\"). Ice cream target INR500 crores revenue in 6–7 years.","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated revenue","v":"INR13,196 million, +19% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR740 million; margin 5.6%, +98 bps QoQ"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR309 million"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR26,577 million, +18% YoY"},{"m":"Milk procurement","v":"17.28 lakh LPD, +7% YoY; H1 17,69,000 LPD, +4%"},{"m":"Landed procurement cost","v":"INR47.73 per liter, +11% YoY"},{"m":"Milk sales","v":"12.35 lakh LPD, +2% YoY; ASP INR60.34 per liter, +6%"},{"m":"VAP ex ghee and butter","v":"INR4,510 million, +32%; 35.4% of revenue vs 31.3%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 VAP ex ghee and butter","v":"INR10,146 million, +36%; 39.6% of revenue vs 33.7%"},{"m":"VAP incl. ghee and butter","v":"INR5,408 million, +31%; 42.5% of revenue vs 37.9%"},{"m":"VAP volume growth","v":"Lassi 89%, buttermilk 56%, ice cream 44%, paneer 21%, curd 15%"},{"m":"Weighted price increase","v":"About 8%; milk 6%, value-added 9% to 10%"},{"m":"Core dairy growth split","v":"49% from price, 51% from volume"},{"m":"Heritage Nutrivet revenue","v":"INR853 million, +47% YoY; PBT INR37 million"},{"m":"H1 FY27 Heritage Nutrivet","v":"INR1,581 million, +42%; PBT INR56 million"},{"m":"Cattle feed sales","v":"+47% YoY"},{"m":"Own ice cream revenue (FY27)","v":"INR170 crores, INR180 crores vs about INR110 crores last year"},{"m":"Get-A-Way supply","v":"3,272 liters of ice cream per day; 71% stake"}],"takeaways":["Price hikes lag procurement inflation: Raw milk cost rose about 11% while weighted price hikes averaged about 8%. The CEO said September hikes were \"much higher than 8%\" and \"the benefit of it will accrue in quarter 3\".","Q3 procurement inflation may persist: The CEO said \"that 11% kind of thing might continue\". October procurement prices rose slightly over September. Management hopes a buffalo flush brings \"a little bit of a cooling effect\" after Diwali.","Margin upside if milk eases: The CEO said a INR0.5 cut in procurement price lifts the bottom line by 0.7%, \"almost INR3 crores… per month\". Consumer prices \"once it goes up… we have not seen it coming down\".","Cooperative price moves: Aavin raised farm procurement prices from INR38 to INR41 to INR44. Karnataka will raise consumer price by INR8 from 1st November, which the CEO sees as a Bangalore sales upside.","El Nino uncertainty: The WTD said drought in southern states leaves \"situation is unclear even today\". The ED said \"we are on a wait-and-watch mode\" on milk availability in the flush season."],"revenue":["Core dairy price-volume split: The CEO said about 49% of core dairy revenue growth came from price increases and 51% from volume, so volume held despite price increases.","VAP growth broad-based: The CEO said Q2 VAP growth was \"broad-based\" rather than weather-led. Q1 benefited from weather; temperature-sensitive products held at Q1 growth levels.","Premiumisation lifts realisation: Nourish+ paneer is \"more than 15%\" of paneer (WTD), or \"16% or 17%\" of volume (CEO). Ice cream earns more than INR200 per liter, and ghee revenue rose close to 20%.","Pricing by category: Ghee, paneer and curd are \"90%+\" of VAP sales. Premium curd variants rose \"upwards of 15%\". Ghee volume was flat, with \"bumper growth\" expected in Q3 on festive timing.","Going deeper, not wider: The CEO said growth comes without geographic expansion. Brands are in 12 countries, but international is about 0.5% of revenue."],"profit":["Cost absorption offsets milk cost: Employee expenses fell about 1% of revenue, operating expenses about 0.6% and freight about 0.1%. Only raw material rose as a share of revenue.","Depreciation from new projects: The CEO said large projects capitalised at the start of the year raised depreciation. As a result, sequential EBITDA improvement was \"far better than PBT or PAT\".","Operating leverage building: The CEO said \"operating leverage in Q2 is better than Q1. And the operating leverage in Q3 will be better than Q2.\"","Feed business pricing: The Nutrivet CEO said Q1 was hit by \"very, very high cost of the ingredients\". Price hikes in August and September across verticals lifted Q2 PBT and EBITDA."],"pipeline":["New high-protein launches: Launched Nourish+ High Protein Curd (25 grams protein), Greek Yogurt in 8-gram and 40-gram protein packs, organic curd and an expanded Alpenvie ice cream range.","Ice cream capacity: The newly commissioned ice cream facility supplies Alpenvie and Get-A-Way. The company holds 71% of Get-A-Way and supplies it 3,272 liters per day.","Subsidiary consolidation: Heritage Novandie Foods is now wholly owned. Its LIVO yogurt brand is among the top 5 in India.","Farmer procurement programs: About 400 field procurement and veterinary staff support farmers. Programs include subsidised cattle feed, third-party lending, sex-sorted semen and the VET+ app.","Milestones reached: Daily curd sales crossed 500 tonnes and quarterly yogurt volumes exceeded 100 tonnes."],"commitments":[{"c":"Price hike benefit","s":"September price hikes \"much higher than 8%\"; \"benefit of it will accrue\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q3 price hikes","s":"\"We'll take a call\" based on market and procurement prices","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Bangalore price pass-on","s":"Expect to pass on more once KMF raises Nandini prices","t":"From 1st November","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"VAP share expansion","s":"\"Our value-added products will continue to further expand in Q3\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Operating leverage","s":"\"Operating leverage in Q3 will be better than Q2\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ghee volume growth","s":"\"Ghee volumes will have a bumper growth coming into Q3\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Feed price pass-through","s":"Pass ingredient inflation to customers; strengthen PBT","t":"Q3, Q4; H2","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ice cream revenue target","s":"INR500 crores, excluding Get-A-Way","t":"\"next 6 or 7 years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Margin recovery","s":"Steady-state margins \"if raw material comes down\"; \"relatively better off\" even if inflation continues","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited a widening milk supply-demand gap, constrained buffalo milk supply, higher feed and fodder costs, and drought-linked water and electricity stress.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026; physical page numbers). Guidance p12, p20; K1 p8–9; K2 p9, p16; K3 p10, p16; K4 p9–10; K5 p7, p11; R1 p11–12; R2 p8; R3 p5, p11; R4 p13, p21; R5 p15, p20; F1 p19; F2 p12; F3 p12; F4 p18; P1 p5; P2 p5; P3 p6; P4 p11, p21–22; P5 p5; C1 p9; C2 p16; C3 p10; C4 p12; C5 p12; C6 p13; C7 p18; C8 p20; C9 p19; M1–M3 p3–4; M4 p3; M5–M7 p4; M8–M9 p4; M10 p5; M11 p5; M12 p8–9; M13 p11–12; M14–M15 p6; M16 p7; M17 p20; M18 p5; Risks p7, p17."},
  {"name":"LTM Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"No numeric revenue guidance. \"H2 will be better than H1\"; FY27 growth near FY26 is an \"endeavor\". FY27 EBIT margin to be held near last year's level, including the Randstad acquisition.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue (USD)","v":"USD1.235 billion, +1.1% QoQ, +5.1% YoY CC"},{"m":"Revenue (INR)","v":"INR11,808 crores, +1.7% QoQ, +13.6% YoY"},{"m":"EBIT margin","v":"15.6%, +10 bps QoQ"},{"m":"PAT margin","v":"11.7% vs 12.7% last quarter"},{"m":"Basic EPS","v":"Rs. 47.2 vs Rs. 49.5 in Q1 FY27"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue growth","v":"5.4% YoY"},{"m":"Order inflow","v":"USD1.3 billion"},{"m":"AI revenue share","v":"13.4% of revenue, +40% YoY"},{"m":"Segment growth YoY","v":"Tech 7%, Consumer 8.6%, Production 7.4%, Financial Services 0.4%"},{"m":"Geography growth QoQ","v":"Americas +1.5%, Europe +1%, Rest of world -1.4%"},{"m":"Headcount","v":"88,835, net addition of 949; freshers 1,743"},{"m":"Utilisation ex-trainees","v":"86.4%, flat"},{"m":"LTM attrition","v":"13.3%"},{"m":"DSO","v":"84 days vs 85 days"},{"m":"OCF to PAT","v":"39% vs 79% in Q1; FCF to PAT 24% vs 63%"},{"m":"Cash and investments","v":"USD1.6 billion or Rs. 15,300 crores vs Rs. 15,021 crores"},{"m":"Return on capital employed","v":"28.5% vs 29.8%"},{"m":"Effective tax rate","v":"25.8%, unchanged"},{"m":"Cash flow hedges","v":"USD4.49 billion; balance sheet hedges USD334 million"},{"m":"Interim dividend","v":"Rs. 25 per share, +13.6% YoY"}],"takeaways":["Saudi business drag: The CEO said Saudi revenue fell materially. Without it, QoQ growth would have been about 1.7%–1.8%, a gap of about 0.6%–0.7%. He said the Saudi situation \"is beyond our control\".","FY27 growth target softened: Asked whether FY27 matches FY26 growth, the CEO said \"that's our endeavor… coming as close as we can\". He reaffirmed that Q3 and Q4 will each beat the prior quarter.","TCV lower by design: The CEO said sales were pushed toward short-tenure, faster-converting AI-led deals using separate incentives. The order book is a \"very majority\" ACV deals, and the ACV split comes after \"one more quarter\".","Productivity headwinds mostly absorbed: The CEO said big-client productivity passbacks are done for \"probably 60-70%\" of the portfolio. Industry-wide, deflation should \"go for a couple of years\".","Top BFSI client consolidation: The CEO said the top BFSI client cut vendors from 50+ to about 5, and LTM is one of them. He expects \"absolutely no impact\" from its tech-spend cuts and a benefit over the multi-year program."],"revenue":["Segment performance: Financial Services grew 3.1% QoQ and returned to YoY growth (0.4%). Tech fell 4% QoQ on a seasonal renewal and Saudi impact. Consumer grew 0.3% QoQ; Production grew 3.7% QoQ.","Tech seasonal renewal: The CEO said the top tech client's renewal cycle starts in July each year. Saudi revenue sits in Tech because it flows through a JV with a tech company; \"we'll be back to the normal growth trajectory\".","Travel budgets tightened: The CEO said travel and hospitality clients tightened budgets because of fuel and energy prices. He expects them to come back to deploy budgets \"put on hold temporarily\".","AI revenue mix: AI revenue reached 13.4% of revenue, up 40% YoY, and cumulative AI revenue including Enterprise AI grew 47%. AI-native projects run 6 months to 1 year, with downstream work following.","Pricing split: The CEO said traditional large deals are fought on price that \"becomes a bit… unmanageable\", while \"our AI rate cards are priced higher than the traditional rate cards\"."],"profit":["Cash conversion one-offs: The CFO said OCF-to-PAT fell because of gratuity funding under the labour codes (deadline October), TDS on the Q1 dividend and advance tax. Q3 \"will normalize\", subject to remaining gratuity funding.","PAT margin dip explained: PAT margin fell from 12.7% because Q1 included a one-time gain from recognising the value of the Voicing.ai investment.","Margin outlook: The CFO said wage hikes are done and organic margins should \"continue to hold on and improve further\". The Randstad impact is mainly amortisation and \"not going to be that significant\".","Payout ratio: The CFO said the payout ratio rose from around 40% to around 45%. Cash is needed for M&A and organic reinvestment, and payout must be balanced with growth.","Hedging and forex: Cash flow hedges stood at USD4.49 billion. The CFO said similar forex losses below EBIT \"may continue for the next couple of quarters\"."],"pipeline":["Randstad acquisition: Approvals for the Randstad digital businesses in Europe and Australia are on track for Q3, with closure by end of Q3. The CEO said 3 to 4 months of realignment will follow.","Blueverse launches: Launches include Blueverse One Token (AI workload routing), RightLogic (cyber assurance) and Sovereign apps. The company built 3 new domain-specific SLMs and was granted 6 AI patents.","Partnerships: Anthropic's Claude and Claude Code are embedded in Blueverse. New partnerships include Glean, Cognition (Devin), IBM and Red Hat (Project Lightwell), and ChainGuard.","GCC business unit: A new business unit will help clients build and scale GCCs with group facilities and real estate capabilities. The CEO will \"share a lot more details in the coming days\".","Talent and delivery: The AI 1000 FDE program has nearly 1,000 employees, plus 2,000+ certifications. A Kolkata delivery and AI centre opened, the Blueverse Studio expanded to Hyderabad, and a global sales enablement program was completed.","CBDT deal ramp-up: The CBDT deal contributed revenue in Q2. The CEO said ramp-up will be \"gradual\" and tied to hardware delivery, which is \"definitely getting better\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"H2 vs H1 growth","s":"\"H2 will be better than H1\"; Q3 and Q4 better quarters","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 growth vs FY26","s":"\"That's our endeavor… coming as close as we can\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 EBIT margin","s":"\"Maintaining our margin… at similar levels to last year\", consolidated including M&A","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Randstad approvals and closure","s":"All approvals expected; closure \"by the end of Q3\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ACV disclosure","s":"Split of ACV vs traditional TCV; \"allow us one more quarter\"","t":"Next quarter","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"OCF normalisation","s":"\"It will normalize in Q3\", except remaining gratuity funding","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gratuity funding","s":"Remaining funding to complete","t":"By October","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"GCC business details","s":"\"I will share a lot more details in the coming days\"","t":"\"coming days\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"SLM build cadence","s":"\"We committed to build SLMs every quarter\"","t":"Every quarter","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Tech vertical recovery","s":"Back to normal growth; deals realising revenue","t":"\"Q3 and beyond\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY28 positioning","s":"Targeting stronger growth with Randstad capabilities; \"that's the aim\"","t":"FY28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Forex losses","s":"Similar losses below EBIT","t":"\"next couple of quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The CEO cited the Middle East conflict hitting Saudi revenue, fuel-price-driven travel budget cuts, and traditional services remaining \"challenged\" by AI deflation.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026). Guidance p5, p9, p11–12; K1 p8; K2 p8–9; K3 p6–8, p18; K4 p19; K5 p12–13; R1 p2; R2 p5, p10; R3 p17; R4 p1, p15; R5 p18; F1 p4, p10–11; F2 p4; F3 p11; F4 p13; F5 p4, p17; P1 p4, p8; P2 p2–3; P3 p3; P4 p3, p14; P5 p2–3; P6 p9; C1 p9; C2 p9; C3 p11–12; C4 p4, p8; C5 p7; C6 p11; C7 p10; C8 p14; C9 p15; C10 p10; C11 p8–9; C12 p17; M1 p4; M2 p4; M3 p4; M4 p4; M5 p4; M6 p5; M7 p1; M8 p1; M9 p2; M10 p2; M11 p2, p4; M12–M13 p4; M14–M20 p4; Risks p8, p17, p19."},
  {"name":"Polycab India Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"Project Spring FY30 guidance maintained: Wires and Cables EBITDA margin 11%–13% and volume at 1.5x market. FMEG growth 1.5x–2x industry with 8%–10% EBITDA margin. \"Our outlook remains unchanged\".","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated revenue growth","v":"53% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"+39% YoY; margin 14.4%, up more than 50 bps QoQ"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR 9,900 million, +43% YoY; margin 10.0%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"Crossed INR180 billion, +46% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA and PAT","v":"EBITDA +36%, margin 14.1%; PAT +38%, margin 9.9%"},{"m":"Wires and Cables revenue","v":"+47% YoY; domestic +50% YoY"},{"m":"W&C volume growth","v":"Mid-to-high single digits; wires early double digit; cables mid-single"},{"m":"W&C EBIT margin","v":"14.2%"},{"m":"Exports","v":"+17% YoY, +85% QoQ; 5% of revenue vs 6.5%"},{"m":"FMEG revenue","v":"+124% YoY"},{"m":"FMEG EBIT margin","v":"10.1%"},{"m":"FMEG premium mix","v":"Fans around 33%; lights 38%"},{"m":"EPC revenue","v":"INR 6,171 million, +53% YoY; EBIT INR444 million, 7.2%"},{"m":"EPC order book","v":"Close to around INR9,000 crores, INR10,000 crores"},{"m":"Net cash (30 Sep)","v":"INR 45.2 billion vs INR 29.4 billion a year ago"},{"m":"Working capital cycle","v":"Approximately 11 days"},{"m":"H1 FY27 capex","v":"INR 6.1 billion"},{"m":"Organised W&C market share","v":"Around 30% to 31%"}],"takeaways":["Margins above guide, guide kept: W&C EBIT was 14.2% and FMEG EBIT 10.1%, above long-term ranges. IR said \"we would be happy to operate at a higher end\", but \"we stick to our long-term guidance for FY30\".","Insolvency case order reserved: NCLAT stayed admission of CIRP over the Asier Metals commercial dispute of INR 2.79 crores. After the October 15 hearing the order is reserved; the CFO expects no \"adverse or material impact\".","New entrant, no response yet: On UltraTech's wires entry, IR said \"we don't see any real competition in the near term\". He called its revenue goal \"elusive\" and said there is no pricing action: \"Nothing as of now.\"","EHV plant timeline: The EHV plant is now expected to commission in FY28, possibly in April or May.","Working capital to normalise: The working capital cycle fell to approximately 11 days on higher payables from letters of credit. IR expects it to trend back to the long-term 45 to 50 days."],"revenue":["Wires and cables growth drivers: Domestic growth came from demand, channel execution and \"favourable commodity linked realizations\". Volume grew mid-to-high single digits on an 18% base; wires grew early double digits and cables mid-single digits.","Channel and regional mix: Channel sales outgrew institutional. West is the largest region, followed by South, North and East. IR said real estate demand stays strong.","Exports recovery: Exports grew 17% YoY and 85% QoQ despite geopolitical disruption and shipping delays of 10 to 12 days. Exports are 5% of revenue versus 6.5% a year ago, across 94 countries.","FMEG solar-led growth: Solar, the largest FMEG category, grew multi-fold, aided by PM Surya Ghar Yojana. Solar is inverters only, \"largely a trading business\". All six FMEG categories beat industry growth.","Copper pass-through: IR said pricing is \"cost-plus\", passed on monthly for cables and fortnightly for wires. He said there has been no meaningful channel restocking despite high copper prices."],"profit":["W&C margin drivers: IR cited operating leverage, wires growing faster than cables, channel outgrowing institutional, export mix and premiumisation. He said margins \"will normalize to a certain level\" over the long term.","FMEG margin drivers: IR cited scale and operating leverage, a premium mix (fans 33%, lights 38%), solution-led selling via building verticals, regional P&L strategy and better product availability.","Advance payment explained: About INR1,200 crores in the cash flow statement is advance payment for inventory procurement. IR said there is \"nothing one-off over there\".","Net cash and capex: Net cash was INR 45.2 billion versus INR 29.4 billion a year ago. H1 capex was INR 6.1 billion for capacity, supply chain and growth platforms.","Copper sourcing: IR said 100% of copper is imported and current arrangements are beneficial. He added \"we never know how the mix is going to go in future\"."],"pipeline":["EPC order book: EPC order book is close to INR9,000–10,000 crores across BharatNet and RDSS, milestone-based. IR expects high single-digit sustainable EPC margins.","Data center opportunity: The company supplies the conventional part of data centers. R&D is working on data center products, with India capacity of 1.6–1.7 GW versus projections of 8–15 GW.","Export expansion: Around 10 countries were added last year, with Chile contributing this quarter. IR cited a \"very strong order book\" across the US, Europe, LATAM and Asia.","Market share trajectory: Organised W&C market share is around 30% to 31%. IR said the company gains around 1% to 1.5% a year, and gained 3 to 4 percentage points last year."],"commitments":[{"c":"W&C EBITDA margin","s":"11% to 13%, medium to long term; \"happy to operate at a higher end\"","t":"FY30","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"W&C volume growth","s":"1.5x of market growth","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FMEG growth","s":"1.5x to 2x industry growth rate","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FMEG EBITDA margin","s":"8% to 10% over the longer term","t":"FY30","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EPC margin","s":"High single-digit range","t":"Medium to long term","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Working capital cycle","s":"Trend toward 45 to 50 days as LC benefits normalise","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EHV plant","s":"Commissioning \"may start in April, it may start in May\"","t":"FY28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"NCLAT order","s":"Order reserved; \"would not like to hazard a guess on the timeline\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Exports outlook","s":"\"Exports is certainly going to pick up from here\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited geopolitical tensions, elevated energy prices, higher global bond yields, shipping route disruptions, commodity volatility and US trade risk (\"risk element of Mr. Trump\").","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026; physical page numbers). Guidance p5, p7, p9, p11; K1 p7, p9, p11; K2 p5, p10; K3 p11–12; K4 p18; K5 p6; R1 p6, p10; R2 p6, p14; R3 p6–7, p12; R4 p7, p17–19; R5 p14–15; F1 p9, p15; F2 p8–9; F3 p15; F4 p6; F5 p18; P1 p7, p13–14; P2 p13; P3 p12; P4 p11–12, p17; C1 p7, p11; C2 p13; C3 p7; C4 p9; C5 p7; C6 p6; C7 p18; C8 p10; C9 p12; M1 p5; M2 p6; M3 p6; M4–M5 p6; M6–M7 p6, p10; M8 p7; M9 p6, p15; M10–M12 p7–8; M13 p7; M14 p14; M15–M17 p6; M18 p17; Risks p4–5, p12."},
  {"name":"Tata Technologies Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"14 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 \"strong double-digit\" consolidated revenue growth reiterated, with margin expansion expected in Q3 and H2. The Q4 18% exit margin was not confirmed: \"18% and 20% as the North Star\".","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"US$178 million, +22.2% YoY cc; INR 1,699 crores, +2.1% QoQ"},{"m":"Services revenue","v":"INR 1,356 crores (US$142 million), +4.3% QoQ, +26.2% YoY cc"},{"m":"Technology Solutions revenue","v":"INR 343 crores, +9.2% YoY cc, −6.6% QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR 273 crores, +2.2% QoQ, +31.5% YoY"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA margin","v":"16.1%"},{"m":"EBIT","v":"INR 239 crores, +25.3% YoY"},{"m":"PBT","v":"INR 242 crores, +7% YoY; underlying +16.4%"},{"m":"Net income","v":"INR 171 crores vs INR 166 crores"},{"m":"Auto excluding ES-Tec growth","v":"+7.6% QoQ; about 16% YoY"},{"m":"Non-auto growth","v":"+7.5% QoQ (US$)"},{"m":"BMW JV profit share","v":"INR 11.4 crores, +20% QoQ, +115% YoY; INR 19.7 crores total"},{"m":"Other income","v":"INR 31 crores, −35% YoY"},{"m":"Large deal win","v":"US$138 million full vehicle program"},{"m":"Headcount","v":"12,895, +316 (2.5%) QoQ"},{"m":"Attrition (TTM voluntary)","v":"15.6%, down 70 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Net cash","v":"About INR 330 crores vs INR 880 crores in Q1"},{"m":"Dividend paid in quarter","v":"Nearly INR 475 crores"},{"m":"DSO","v":"102 days vs 97; billed 63 vs 65; unbilled 39 vs 32"},{"m":"Ind AS 115 revenue impact","v":"INR 12.6 crores reduction"}],"takeaways":["Growth guidance reiterated: The CEO said first-half performance \"reinforces our positive outlook\" and expects to deliver \"strong double-digit growth for the full year\", with large programs contributing more meaningfully in H2.","Margin target evaded: Asked if the company is on track to exit Q4 at 18%, the CEO said \"we have positioned 18% and 20% as the North Star… We'll continue to keep you informed.\" He expects margins to expand in Q3.","Landmark full vehicle win: A US$138 million turnkey program for a European luxury OEM covers two vehicles over about two-and-a-half years. The CEO called it \"real turnkey ownership… from concept all the way through the launch\".","Germany and ES-Tec softness: The CFO said ES-Tec demand \"remains subdued\", with \"some additional softness in Q3\", stabilising in 2027. The CEO said headwinds in the first 12 to 18 months were anticipated.","Revenue recognition change: From July 1st, 2026, OEM-hosted SaaS licenses are recognised over the license term under Ind AS 115. This cut Technology Solutions revenue by INR 12.6 crores; ex-change, total revenue would have grown 2.8% QoQ."],"revenue":["Services broad-based: Services grew 4.3% QoQ and 26.2% YoY in constant currency, or 22.1% excluding ES-Tec, and are about 80% of revenue. Auto ex-ES-Tec grew 7.6% QoQ and about 16% YoY. Non-auto grew 7.5% QoQ.","Technology Solutions dip: The QoQ decline came from education revenue timing and the SaaS accounting change. The CFO expects products to recover in Q3 seasonally, while education faces \"near-term pressure\" from slower customer site readiness.","Anchor client bucket drop: The CEO attributed the drop in the US$50 million-plus client bucket to an anchor customer ahead of a structural change: \"We see that as tactical. We expect to bounce back in the second half.\"","Anchor outlook: The CEO is \"very, very bullish\" on JLR and Tata Motors CV and PV. He cited the push to double Tata Motors volumes, the Iveco acquisition, and JLR's North American pivot.","Aerospace second engine: The CEO said aerospace programs run 8–10 years, and Airbus plans a new single-aisle aircraft. \"Given the incumbency that we have established at Airbus, we are very, very excited.\""],"profit":["Margin held despite increments: Operating EBITDA margin was held at 16.1% despite a more than 100 bps salary increment headwind, through execution, efficiencies and mix. The CEO said attrition fell after increments.","Subcontracting elevated: The CFO said subcontracting supports program ramp-ups and \"will further go down and will be replaced by the permanent employees\" as programs scale. It is \"a very conscious call\".","Pune office costs: The new Pune facility raised D&A and lease interest. The CFO expects these \"to be increasingly absorbed\" as revenue scales, supporting profitability over the medium term.","Non-cash FX drag: Other income fell 35% YoY on foreign exchange items. The CFO said underlying PBT grew 16.4% and PAT would have risen 13% YoY excluding non-cash items.","Cash and receivables: Net cash fell to about INR 330 crores from INR 880 crores after a dividend of nearly INR 475 crores. DSO rose to 102 days on unbilled timing, \"with no change in the underlying quality\"."],"pipeline":["Large deal momentum: Five large deals were secured in Q2, including end-to-end development of a new vehicle platform and digital SDV infrastructure for a North American commercial vehicle maker. The CEO expects \"a couple of wins… before the end of the calendar year\".","BMW JV ahead of plan: The share of profit from BMW TechWorks India rose 20% QoQ and 115% YoY to INR 11.4 crores, and INR 19.7 crores including deferred income. The CFO said it \"remains ahead of our expectations\".","AI-native delivery: The CEO said nearly 1,000 associates are building AI agents, \"transitioning from AI-assisted… to AI-native ways of working\". Over 1,000 employees went through AI enablement.","Hiring for ramp-ups: Headcount rose by 316 to 12,895 for large program ramp-ups. Over 27,000 learning hours were delivered and more than 5,000 associates trained.","Group and humanoid adjacencies: The CEO said the company already supports Tata Electronics and sees group consortia as a differentiator. Humanoids are under POC but not material \"in the next 12 months to 18 months\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 revenue growth","s":"\"Strong double-digit\" consolidated growth; reiterated","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ES-Tec recovery","s":"Additional softness in Q3; stabilise and bounce back","t":"Q3; 2027","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Product business recovery","s":"Seasonal recovery in product business","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Sequential margin improvement","s":"Expand in Q3 despite soft quarter; sustain improvement in H2","t":"Q3, H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Exit margin target","s":"18% and 20% as \"North Star\"; Q4 18% not confirmed","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Large deal announcements","s":"\"A couple of wins… before the end of the calendar year\"","t":"By end of CY2026","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Anchor client bucket","s":"Expect bounce back after tactical dip","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"$138M program execution","s":"Ramp-up begins; about two-and-a-half years; contributes in H2","t":"H2 FY27 onward","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Subcontracting normalisation","s":"To decline and be replaced by permanent employees as programs scale","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Multi-year growth","s":"\"Strong double-digit growth\" for \"several years to come\"; 20%-plus \"an increasing challenge\"","t":"Several years","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Restructuring and cost cuts at German OEMs (VW, BMW), slower customer site readiness in education, and furlough-affected Q3 seasonality.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 14 Oct 2026; exchange cover letter p1, physical page numbers used). Guidance p6, p8, p10, p31–32; K1 p5, p8; K2 p31–32; K3 p5–6, p19–20, p26; K4 p9, p21; K5 p9–10; R1 p9, p25, p28; R2 p9–10; R3 p22; R4 p30; R5 p6–7, p16–17; P1 p4, p10, p15; P2 p15–16; P3 p11; P4 p11–12; P5 p12; I1 p5–6, p23; I2 p11; I3 p7, p13–14; I4 p13; I5 p18, p31; C1 p8, p14; C2 p9, p25; C3 p10; C4 p11, p31; C5 p32; C6 p23; C7 p22; C8 p19, p26; C9 p15–16; C10 p32; M1 p3, p10; M2 p4, p9; M3 p9; M4–M5 p10; M6 p11; M7 p12; M8 p12; M9 p9, p25; M10 p9; M11 p11; M12 p11; M13 p5; M14 p13; M15 p4, p13; M16–M17 p12; M18 p12; M19 p10; Risks p11, p20–21, p31."},
  {"name":"Tata Elxsi Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"13 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"No formal guidance (\"As a policy… we don't provide guidance at all\"). Management expects H2 FY27 revenue \"better than H1\", with \"a slightly upward margin trajectory\" through the rest of the year.","metrics":[{"m":"Operating revenue","v":"INR 1055.7 crores, +2.3% in constant currency"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR 231.4 crores, +7.1% QoQ, +19.7% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"21.9%, +70 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Transportation growth","v":"+2% QoQ in constant currency"},{"m":"Media and communication growth","v":"+3.4% QoQ in constant currency"},{"m":"Healthcare and life sciences growth","v":"+3.5% QoQ in constant currency"},{"m":"Utilisation","v":"About 78%, up QoQ; peak \"around 81%, 82%\""},{"m":"Margin bridge (QoQ)","v":"60–70 bps currency; 10–20 bps operating leverage and one-timers"},{"m":"Onsite-offshore revenue mix","v":"Roughly 25:75"},{"m":"\"Others\" segment share","v":"About 2% of revenue"}],"takeaways":["H2 better than H1: The MD expects H2 to beat H1 in media and communication and in healthcare. Transportation \"is dependent on how Europe turns\". The CFO said this implies H2 revenue higher \"even on a Y-o-Y basis\".","Europe deals stalled: The MD said large European deals have been \"in play for the last few quarters\", with Tata Elxsi \"in the final shortlist\", but OEM decisions are delayed: \"It could happen in this quarter. It could happen in the next quarter.\"","Q3 furlough uncertainty: The MD said Q3 \"is typically a short quarter with fewer working days\". \"We await customer-specific direction on possible furloughs.\"","Margin target withheld: The MD expects \"gradual improvement in our margins\" over 2 to 3 quarters through utilisation, pyramid correction and operating leverage. \"We don't want to give a number at this point in time.\"","JLR under watch: The MD said JLR is going through \"its own challenges in terms of their own business performance and layoffs\". \"We will get to know about it, maybe across H2.\" Auto growth has come mostly from non-group customers."],"revenue":["Transportation driven by US, India: Transportation grew 2% QoQ in constant currency, led by auto OEMs in the US and India plus aerospace and defense \"at a smaller base\". Japan and the rest of Asia were flat and Europe \"flattish\".","Media ramp-ups and AI wins: Media and communication grew 3.4% QoQ in constant currency on \"sustained ramp-ups across key engagements and AI-led deal wins\", including a GenAI virtual assistant engagement with a global technology leader.","Healthcare recovery: Healthcare and life sciences grew 3.5% QoQ in constant currency. The MD said \"we bottomed out last quarter\". Nitin Pai cited customer diversification beyond the US, naming Bayer (Germany) and Terumo (Japan).","Lower revenue leakage: Nitin Pai said leakage has improved from a year back. Multi-year large deals protect the base, and the earlier heavy leakage from automotive suppliers has reduced.","Aerospace growth undisclosed: The MD said aerospace \"showed a smart growth\" but declined to quantify it: \"it'll be misleading… we will disclose this at an appropriate time\"."],"profit":["Margin bridge: The CFO attributed about 60 to 70 bps of the QoQ margin gain to cross-currency benefits and 10 to 20 bps to operating leverage and one-timers going away. These largely offset the annual wage hike.","Wage cycle complete: The CFO said \"we are done with the salary hikes\" for the year, so there should not be \"any material movement in employee cost\" except selective investments.","Utilisation headroom: The CFO put utilisation at about 78%, up from last quarter. The MD said the peak was \"around 81%, 82%\", while Nitin Pai said \"Higher than that\". Junior hiring is being calibrated.","Pyramid correction: The MD said the pyramid shifted over four to six quarters with senior hires for new engagements. Over the next half year, the company will rebalance using a bench trained at lower levels.","Deal margin profile: Nitin Pai said new deals are innovation and product engineering, \"reasonably flat\" on margins, not back-ended like IT consolidation deals. The onsite-offshore revenue mix is roughly 25:75."],"pipeline":["Auto connected vehicle deal: A strategic multi-year deal from a leading automotive OEM for a cloud-based telematics and connected vehicle ecosystem has \"just started… not fully ramped up yet\".","Aerospace engagements: The company is collaborating with Sarla Aviation on Shunya, India's first indigenous eVTOL. It also won a test rig and validation engagement from a global aerospace supplier, with ramp-up taking \"at least two to three quarters\".","Home dialysis win: The company won a contract from a global renal care leader for end-to-end product engineering of next-generation home dialysis systems.","New vectors in \"skunk works\": Nitin Pai said smart manufacturing and BESS sit in the \"others\" bucket, about 2% of revenue, while the company experiments with customers. \"We're not calling them out as yet.\"","AI budget dynamics: Nitin Pai said enterprise AI budgets still draw on discretionary spend, but there is \"a little more pragmatism\" and ROI focus, \"a slight qualitative improvement\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"H2 revenue","s":"H2 \"better than H1\"; implies higher revenue YoY in H2","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Margin trajectory","s":"\"Slightly upward\"; \"gradual improvement\"; no number given","t":"Rest of FY27; \"2 to 3 quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Pyramid rebalancing","s":"Actively correct pyramid using trained junior bench","t":"\"Over the next half year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Europe deal closures","s":"Shortlisted large deals awaiting OEM decisions; \"very difficult to give a timeline\"","t":"\"this quarter… next quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Aerospace growth disclosure","s":"Will disclose aerospace growth figures","t":"\"at an appropriate time\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Large deal ramp-up","s":"Aerospace and defense deals to reach scale","t":"\"at least two to three quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"JLR visibility","s":"Monitoring JLR situation; clarity expected","t":"\"maybe across H2\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Employee cost","s":"No material movement after completed wage hikes, except selective investment","t":"Rest of FY27","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Systemic cost and electrification issues at European OEMs, Chinese vehicle competition in Europe, and project closures creating runoffs.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 13 Oct 2026; exchange cover letter p1, physical page numbers used). Guidance p6, p12, p15; K1 p5, p15; K2 p11; K3 p4; K4 p11–12; K5 p13–14; R1 p3, p10; R2 p4; R3 p4–5, p14–15; R4 p8; R5 p8; P1 p5; P2 p7; P3 p7, p10; P4 p6; P5 p11, p16; I1 p3–4, p8; I2 p4, p11; I3 p4; I4 p13; I5 p9; C1 p15; C2 p6, p12; C3 p6; C4 p11; C5 p8; C6 p11; C7 p13; C8 p7; M1–M3 p3; M4 p3; M5–M6 p4; M7 p7, p10; M8 p5; M9 p16; M10 p13; Risks p17. Omitted as inconsistent: \"others\" YoY growth stated as \"about a 75%\" and \"60%, 70%\". Analyst-cited ~15% H1 growth not used."},
  {"name":"Tejas Networks Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"No numeric guidance. Management said the order book gives \"strong business visibility for the next few quarters\", with focus on \"achieving quarterly positive EBITDA performance\" and \"a visible path to profitability in the upcoming years\".","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR406 crores; Q2 FY26 INR257 crore (MD) / INR262 crores (CFO)"},{"m":"Revenue growth (CFO)","v":"Around 55% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR108 crores (sign not stated) vs INR293 crores negative Q2 FY26"},{"m":"PAT","v":"Loss INR207 crores vs loss INR307 crores Q2 FY26"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR808 crores vs INR459 crores H1 FY26"},{"m":"Order book (quarter end)","v":"INR2,315 crores vs INR1,500 crores in Q1"},{"m":"Revenue mix","v":"India 88%; international 12%"},{"m":"Order book mix","v":"India 96%; international 4%"},{"m":"BSNL LOI sites","v":"18,685 sites"},{"m":"Net working capital","v":"INR4,235 crores, lower QoQ"},{"m":"Inventory","v":"INR2,314 crores vs INR2,358 crores"},{"m":"Net debt","v":"INR 4,384 crores vs INR4,277 crores Q1"},{"m":"Patents","v":"26 filed in Q2; 748 total; 266 standards-related"}],"takeaways":["BSNL 4G expansion order: An LOI covers 18,685 sites, with shipments started. The MD said \"all of BSNL's INR1,500 crore that we announced is not in this order book\", and \"most of it we expect to come by the end of this quarter\".","BSNL collections due: The MD said milestone deliveries are met and \"this is the quarter when we will see a significant amount of collections coming from BSNL\". \"I can't specify the exact number.\"","Inventory and debt reduction: The MD said inventory built for the BSNL order will fall \"over the next few quarters\" toward earlier levels, and gross debt will see \"a significant reduction\". He gave no numbers.","No repeat of provisioning: An analyst cited INR 145 crores of contract manufacturing losses. The MD said that provisioning related to September 2025 and the first BSNL order: \"There's no provisioning of that sort in the current quarter.\"","Private telco wireless entry: The MD said Indian private operators have largely finished 4G/5G build-outs, so Tejas is running PoCs and trials to qualify for \"their next phase of expansion\"."],"revenue":["Revenue drivers: Q2 revenue came from optical, IP and FTTx shipments to private operators and system integrators, initial BSNL expansion shipments, and initial end-to-end 5G shipments to a South American customer.","Geographic mix: Revenue was 88% India and 12% international. The order book is 96% India and 4% international, with the India share up on the BSNL expansion POs.","Wireline wins: Wins included an LOI from a new SI for IP MPLS routers in BharatNet middle mile across three states, a pan-India OTN network for a Government of India enterprise, and GPON FTTx orders from existing ISPs.","New international customer: A new Tier-1 pan-African telecom operator selected Tejas's 400 gig optical equipment, which the MD called \"a new customer footprint that we have won in that continent\".","Data center interconnect: The MD said Tejas is \"winning several business\" in data center interconnect in India and internationally. It works with system integrators building full racks, \"not working directly with the GPU vendors\"."],"profit":["Working capital reduction: The CFO said net working capital fell QoQ to INR4,235 crores on lower trade receivables from higher collections and non-recourse bill discounting.","Debt rising on capex: The CFO said net debt rose to INR 4,384 crores from INR4,277 crores on continued capex \"as per the plan\" and funding of higher operating costs.","Inventory for BSNL: The CFO said inventory was INR2,314 crores vs INR2,358 crores, with \"a significant portion\" to be used for the BSNL 4G order, for which deliveries started in Q2."],"pipeline":["International 5G PoCs: The company has multiple PoCs in North America and Asia-Pacific, often with partners. The CTO said they are typically second or third operators or greenfield 5G-only networks.","Custom radio for Tier-1s: The MD and CTO said several Tier-1 operators are discussing custom 5G radios for custom band combinations and high-power radios. The NEC partnership includes developing next-generation radios for NEC's international customers.","Product roadmap: The CTO described radios covering 700 MHz to 4.9 GHz, a compact one-RU baseband supporting five bands, AI RAN investment, higher-bit-rate IP/MPLS core routing, and 200G-plus low-latency access.","Railway 5G trial: The CTO said a 700 MHz 5G network is deployed on a railway trial section for internal communications. \"Based on the results of the trial, we expect to expand.\"","D2M broadcast positioning: The CTO said Tejas has developed broadcast radio heads and trialled sites around Delhi. It aims to supply radios to SI winners of the upcoming D2M RFP, \"we will not be the system integrator\".","IP and standards: The company filed 26 patents in Q2, taking the total to 748, of which 266 are 5G-Advanced and 6G standards-related. It demonstrated 6G sensing with Qualcomm at IMC."],"commitments":[{"c":"BSNL POs into order book","s":"Most of INR1,500 crore LOI expected as POs","t":"\"by the end of this quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"BSNL milestone collections","s":"\"Significant amount of collections\"; amount not specified","t":"\"this quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Inventory reduction","s":"\"Significant reduction\" toward earlier levels via BSNL execution","t":"\"over the next few quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gross debt reduction","s":"\"Significant reduction\" as inventory converts to collections; no number","t":"Next few quarters","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Positive quarterly EBITDA","s":"\"Major focus on… achieving quarterly positive EBITDA performance\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"BSNL order delivery","s":"Deliver BSNL 4G expansion \"within the required timelines\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Path to profitability","s":"\"Visible path to profitability\"","t":"\"upcoming years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Railway network expansion","s":"Expand based on trial results","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p4, p10; K1 p1–2, p5; K2 p4–5; K3 p8; K4 p7–8; K5 p5; R1 p1; R2 p1; R3 p2; R4 p2; R5 p6–7; P1 p1; P2 p1–2; P3 p1; I1 p2, p9; I2 p2, p9–10; I3 p3; I4 p7; I5 p9; I6 p1–2; C1 p5; C2 p4–5; C3 p4, p8; C4 p8; C5 p4; C6 p4; C7 p4; C8 p7; M1 p1; M2 p1; M3 p1; M4 p1; M5 p1; M6 p1; M7–M8 p1; M9 p2; M10–M12 p1; M13 p1–2. Omitted as garbled or contradictory: H1 growth \"approximately around 24%\" (inconsistent with INR459 crores to INR808 crores) and the QoQ revenue comparison \"against INR402 crores… we are at INR40 crores\". Q1 EBITDA \"INR99 crores\" not used because its sign was not stated."},
  {"name":"Tips Music Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 revenue and PAT growth guidance of 20%. Upgrade hinted but not formalised: CFO \"we would exceed our 20% guidance\"; CMD \"For time being, you consider as 20%\". Content cost INR90–100 crores maintained.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue","v":"INR120 crores vs INR89.2 crores, +35% YoY"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA","v":"INR84.64 crores, +25% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"70.5%"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR65.5 crores, +23% YoY"},{"m":"PAT margin","v":"54%"},{"m":"Content cost","v":"INR26.18 crores, almost doubled YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR227 crores, +28% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR109 crores, +11% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 content cost","v":"INR71 crore, front-loaded"},{"m":"H1 FY27 digital share","v":"73% of revenue"},{"m":"Paid subscription share","v":"10% to 15% of digital"},{"m":"Songs released","v":"73 (40 film, 33 non-film)"},{"m":"YouTube subscribers","v":"164.5 million"},{"m":"Total content views","v":"Surpassed 54.3 billion"},{"m":"Revenue market share","v":"Around 8% to 10% of industry"},{"m":"Buyback completed","v":"INR44.44 crores, open market"},{"m":"Interim dividend","v":"INR6.50 per share"}],"takeaways":["Guidance upgrade not formalised: The CFO said \"we would exceed our 20% guidance… we don't want to put a specific number\". The CMD said \"Let one more quarter go… We will brief you then in the next call.\" An analyst's 2–3 point upgrade was not confirmed.","Content spend front-loaded: H1 content cost was INR71 crore against full-year guidance of INR90 crores to INR100 crores. The CFO said \"the second half would be much lighter\", with \"smaller films and non-film music, not bigger names\".","YouTube Shorts deal pending: The CFO said the YouTube Shorts deal \"is still under negotiation… We'll update once the deal is done.\" The CMD attributed the delay to negotiations.","Capital return completed: The company completed an INR44.44 crores open market buyback, and the Board declared an interim dividend of INR6.50 per share.","No artist management or diversification: The CMD said \"Our focus is absolutely on new music\". Artist management \"is really a lot of trouble\", and the company prefers short-term artist contracts of two or three songs."],"revenue":["Volume-led, no one-offs: The CFO said there was \"no one-off\" and growth was \"primarily… volume-driven\", from both new releases and catalog across digital and non-digital. He confirmed an analyst's 30%–35% stream volume growth.","Digital mix and subscriptions: Digital was 73% of H1 revenue, with a range of 70% to 75% and \"no impact on profitability\" from variation. Paid subscription is 10% to 15% of digital, where per-stream rates are higher.","Catalog vs new content: The CFO said around 85% of revenue comes from content acquired up to three years back and 15% from content acquired within the last three years.","Warner partnership: The CFO put the Warner share \"in that range of… 25%, 30%\", with an increase over H1 FY26. Warner negotiates global platform deals, including Instagram Reels, and comes up for renewal \"next year, '28… Towards the end\".","Per-stream rate flat: The CMD said the YouTube per-stream rate \"will remain INR0.05\", and growth must come from higher stream volumes. Revenue market share is \"around that 8% to 10% of the industry\"."],"profit":["Content cost drives margin: Q2 content cost of INR26.18 crores \"almost doubled\" YoY. The CFO said promotion is part of content cost, with Q2 promotion continuing for mid-June releases.","Full expensing policy: The CMD said \"Tips is the only company, which writes off their entire content cost in the same year\". He reiterated content investment of \"20% to 22%\" of the top line.","Advance accounting: The CFO said advances from Warner and other partners are classified as non-current if over 12 months and current otherwise, moving to revenue as booked."],"pipeline":["Release pipeline target: The CMD wants to \"release at least five, six films a year, and four, five should be in Hindi and two, three in other regional languages. We'll achieve that.\"","New producer tie-ups: The CMD is \"talking to few more producers\" to sign audio rights, \"which we'll announce very soon\".","Q2 releases: The company released 73 songs (40 film, 33 non-film). Ishqnama crossed 100 million YouTube views and the recreated Aankhon Se Tune 2.0 crossed 66 million.","CEO search ongoing: The CMD said \"we are very aggressive on that, but in our industry, I am not finding a suitable person. So, still search is on.\"","Rural subscription push: The CMD said Tips is pushing OTT platforms to expand paid music to Tier 2–4 cities and rural areas, citing past ringback-tone subscription revenue: \"It will happen.\""],"commitments":[{"c":"YouTube Shorts deal","s":"\"Still under negotiation\"; update once concluded","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 content cost","s":"INR90 crores to INR100 crores maintained; H2 \"much lighter\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 revenue and PAT growth","s":"20%; \"we would exceed our 20% guidance\"; no specific number","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Content investment ratio","s":"\"20% to 22% we'll invest in content\" of top line","t":"Per year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Guidance update","s":"\"Let one more quarter go… We will brief you then in the next call\"","t":"Next call","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"New producer audio rights","s":"Deals with few more producers","t":"\"very soon\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Film releases","s":"At least five, six films a year; four, five Hindi; two, three regional","t":"Per year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"CEO appointment","s":"Search ongoing; \"As we get, I will immediately appoint\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Warner renewal","s":"Deal up for renewal","t":"\"next year, '28… Towards the end\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p1–2, p4, p7; K1 p4, p7; K2 p2, p4–5; K3 p2, p10; K4 p1; K5 p5; R1 p2, p8–9; R2 p2–4, p9, p11; R3 p8; R4 p12–13; R5 p8–9, p11; P1 p1, p3; P2 p7; P3 p13–14; I1 p10; I2 p6; I3 p1; I4 p12; I5 p11–12; C1 p2, p10; C2 p2, p4–5; C3 p4; C4 p7; C5 p7; C6 p6; C7 p10; C8 p12; C9 p13; M1–M6 p1; M7–M9 p1–2; M10 p2; M11 p4, p9; M12–M14 p1; M15 p9; M16–M17 p1. Omitted as garbled: market share \"85 to 10%\", streams \"around 3 -- say, 400 million\", and \"you should come back to 30% of growth\". Analyst-cited guidance upgrade of 2–3 points from a media interview not confirmed."},
  {"name":"Sri Lotus Developers and Realty Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"FY27 guidance maintained: pre-sales INR1,800–2,000 crores (H1: INR879 crores), revenue and PAT growth of 55%–60%, EBITDA margin 35%–40%, PAT margin 25%–30%.","metrics":[{"m":"Total revenue","v":"INR269 crores, +53% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR107 crores vs INR50 crores, +113% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"39.90%"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR89 crores vs INR46 crores, +91% YoY"},{"m":"Pre-sales","v":"INR470 crores vs INR257 crores, +83% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 pre-sales","v":"INR879 crores vs INR319 crores, +176% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR401 crores, +69% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"INR155 crores, +95%; margin 38.80%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR134 crores vs INR72 crores, +86%; margin 33.5%"},{"m":"Collections","v":"INR60 crores in Q2; INR209 crores in H1"},{"m":"Lotus Trident pre-sales","v":"INR178 crores"},{"m":"New GDV added","v":"INR2,700 to INR2,800 crores (three projects)"},{"m":"Pipeline","v":"24 projects, GDV approximately INR20,000 crores"},{"m":"H2 launch GDV","v":"INR3,500 crores to INR4,000 crores (four projects)"},{"m":"Net cash (30 Sep)","v":"INR494 crores"},{"m":"Estimated project-level surplus","v":"Approximately INR9,500 crores"},{"m":"IPO proceeds deployed","v":"INR305 crores of INR550 crores earmarked"}],"takeaways":["H1 vs full-year pre-sales: H1 pre-sales of INR879 crores compare with FY27 guidance of INR1,800–2,000 crores. The MD said four H2 launches with INR3,500–4,000 crores GDV support it; \"more or less 90% approvals are in place\".","Collections lag pre-sales: Collections were INR60 crores in Q2 and INR209 crores in H1, against H1 pre-sales of INR879 crores. The CEO said construction-linked plans collect about 25% at plinth, and \"H2 will be much better than H1\".","Project timelines shifted: Lotus Monarch moved by one year because a society, trust, Charity Commissioner and government are involved; Ghatkopar Salona was also extended. The CEO said \"our guidance will remain same\".","Rate hike seen immaterial: After RBI raised the repo rate 25 bps to 5.5%, the MD said luxury buyers rely \"far less on home loans\". He does not expect the hike to \"materially alter demand\" in the company's micromarkets.","OCF timing not committed: Asked when operating cash flow turns positive, the CFO said \"it will maybe not exactly clear\" given new launches. FY28 collection numbers will be given \"at the beginning of the year\"."],"revenue":["Trident drives pre-sales: Lotus Trident, a luxury commercial project in Andheri West, contributed INR178 crores of pre-sales in its first full quarter. The CEO said 35% of it sold in three months; Celestia sold 36% in six months.","Prabhadevi slow start: Lotus Aquaria in Prabhadevi contributed no pre-sales; the CEO expects results \"in the next two quarters\". The MD cited a new micromarket and \"little more premium pricing\", with traction expected after plinth.","Price appreciation: The MD said almost all projects saw price appreciation in H1. Prices \"will definitely go up from here\" if the market supports it.","Commercial portfolio: The MD said the portfolio has about five commercial projects, including two to three commercial redevelopments. He said Arc One is the only completed commercial redevelopment in the suburbs."],"profit":["Margin drivers: The MD cited no debt and so no interest burden, minimal marketing spend, a fast 2.5- to 3.5-year project cycle, efficient fund deployment and premium pricing.","Cash use for development: The CEO said new projects need only 8% to 10% of project cost upfront. Upfront government fees were paid this quarter to save cost and win supplier discounts; \"we don't see any stress\".","IPO proceeds deployment: The IPO fresh issue raised approximately INR792 crores, with net proceeds of approximately INR732 crores. Of INR550 crores earmarked for Amalfi, Arcadian and Varun, INR305 crores had been deployed by 30th September.","Project-level surplus: The estimated project-level surplus is approximately INR9,500 crores over the portfolio's development cycle. The CEO said this is distinct from current cash or accounting profits."],"pipeline":["New project additions: Three projects were added: Lotus Impressa at Shivaji Park, Lotus Stellar in Versova and Lotus Supremus in Juhu, adding INR2,700 to INR2,800 crores of GDV.","Total pipeline: The ongoing and upcoming pipeline is 24 projects with GDV of approximately INR20,000 crores, 19 of them redevelopment-led. The CEO intends to complete them over \"next four, four and a half years\".","H2 launches: Four planned launches are Lotus Aurelia, Lotus Sky Plaza, Lotus Portofino and Lotus Odyssey.","GIFT City project: A 5.5-acre land parcel on the Sabarmati could hold about 1.3 to 1.5 million square feet of mixed use. Approval is expected once the town planning scheme is declared.","Project completions: Amara has completed RCC, with OC hoped for this quarter or \"this financial year definitely\". Athena is at RCC stage, with OC hoped \"before March\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 pre-sales","s":"INR1,800 crores to INR2,000 crores; \"remain confident\"; H1 INR879 crores","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 revenue and PAT growth","s":"55% to 60% over FY26","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 margins","s":"EBITDA 35% to 40%; PAT 25% to 30%","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 launches","s":"Four projects, GDV INR3,500 to INR4,000 crores; \"more or less 90% approvals\"","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Collections pickup","s":"\"H2 will be much better than H1\" as projects pass plinth","t":"H2","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Trident margin","s":"EBITDA \"33% to 35%… it will remain the same\" (CFO)","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Amara OC","s":"Occupation certificate; \"this quarter… otherwise, this financial year definitely\"","t":"This quarter / FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Athena OC","s":"\"Hopeful that before March, we'll get OC\"","t":"Before March","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Project completions","s":"Three projects completed, with 95% to 100% collection","t":"\"next year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Lotus Monarch timeline","s":"Shifted by one year; \"conservative timeline\"","t":"One year later","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"IPO fund utilisation","s":"INR550 crores earmarked; INR305 crores deployed; \"on track\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Project additions","s":"\"Every quarter, yes, we will be adding some projects\"","t":"Every quarter","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited regulatory and approval delays involving societies, trusts and the Charity Commissioner, and possible repo-rate impact on affordable and mid-income housing.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026; physical page numbers). Guidance p4; K1 p4, p12; K2 p4, p7, p10; K3 p8–9; K4 p3; K5 p10–11; R1 p4, p10; R2 p10; R3 p12; R4 p7; F1 p6; F2 p11; F3 p6; F4 p5; P1 p4; P2 p4, p8; P3 p4; P4 p9; P5 p9; C1 p4; C2 p4; C3 p4; C4 p4, p12; C5 p10; C6 p7; C7 p9; C8 p9; C9 p8; C10 p9; C11 p6; C12 p12; M1–M3 p3, p5; M4–M5 p5; M6–M9 p4–5; M10 p4; M11 p4; M12 p4; M13 p4; M14 p4; M15–M16 p5; M17 p6; Risks p3, p9."},
  {"name":"Billionbrains Garage Ventures Limited (Groww)","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"14 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"No formal guidance. Cost to Grow restated at INR400 crores to INR500 crores a year, flexed with market conditions. The MTF book is expected to keep adding INR600–700 crores a quarter.","metrics":[{"m":"One-time Cost to Serve hit","v":"Around INR10 crores to INR13 crore (expiry-day file delay)"},{"m":"Cost to Grow average","v":"Around INR125 crores per quarter"},{"m":"Annual Cost to Grow range","v":"INR400 crores to INR500 crores a year"},{"m":"MTF book quarterly addition","v":"INR600 crores, INR700 crores on average per quarter"},{"m":"MTF share of cash turnover","v":"Slightly above 5%"},{"m":"Maximum MTF haircut on stocks","v":"80% in some cases, funding only 20%"},{"m":"Cash brokerage per order","v":"Closer to INR15, INR16 vs INR20 in derivatives, commodities"},{"m":"IPOs in quarter","v":"About 30, including SBI AMC and NSE"},{"m":"Customers using multiple products","v":"More than 50% use 2+; more than 5% use 4+"}],"takeaways":["One-off Cost to Serve hit: Files were delayed on one expiry day, causing a one-time cost of around INR10 crores to INR13 crore. AI costs, compute and more trading days also raised Cost to Serve.","Cost to Grow lower, by design: The CFO said Cost to Grow fell because last quarter's IPL branding event did not recur. Spend is cut in weak markets: \"it's not worth spending in a bad market\".","CAS change expected to normalise: The CFO said both SEBI options leave derivatives \"more or less\" unaffected, and expects volumes to \"normalize\". Timing depends on when the circular becomes applicable. \"We don't expect it will go up.\"","MTF risk controls tightened: Policy changes after the March episode include real-time approved-list changes and haircuts above regulatory levels, up to 80% on some run-up stocks. The CFO said there was \"no like one-off cost\" this quarter.","Wealth and US stocks not quantified: The CFO declined to split wealth AUM: \"we don't want to disclose too much\". The CEO gave no full-launch timeline for US stocks: \"I don't think we have the timeline\"."],"revenue":["Brokerage tied to orders: The CFO said derivatives and commodities revenue is 100% linked to orders at INR20 each, not ADTV. Cash is 10 bps or INR20, whichever is lower. August was the lowest month.","Other operating income drivers: About 30 IPOs in the quarter, including SBI AMC and NSE, and accrued BSDA income lifted other operating income. IPO and BSDA income were regrouped there from stocks revenue.","MTF lifts cash turnover: The CFO said MTF is slightly above 5% of cash turnover and should rise. Its share \"depends on the churn\", and high volatility this quarter raised the contribution.","Market share convergence long-term: The CFO said cash and derivatives share should converge with NSE active-client share as customers age, but \"probably it will take 10 years\".","Wealth user growth from Prime: The CFO confirmed most user additions came from Groww Prime. Fisdom users are now in active customers, and wealth income sits in other operating income."],"profit":["Cost to operate flat: The Group Head Finance said cost to operate (people, G&A, CSR) was flat QoQ, with only CSR rising. Lower employee cost was \"a short-term blip\", but \"structurally the cost is going to go up\".","Float income unchanged: Treasury returns follow market yields on NCDs, bonds and debt funds. Customer wallet money can only go into SEBI- and exchange-prescribed FDs, \"so, you won't see much change in the yield\".","In-house AI stack: The CEO said the in-house AI stack lowers per-token cost. Using commercial models would make costs \"much higher\" given the company's token consumption.","UPI MDR exposure: The CFO said the UPI MDR outcome is pending and he expects no material cost change. A INR300 cap is \"insane\", and the company will \"make our case\" with the regulator."],"pipeline":["US stocks testing: The CEO said US stocks is \"very, very early\", with good customer feedback. The product is being made visible to more customers.","Wealth acquisition integrated: Integration of the acquired wealth company is done, and its products are going mainstream across apps. MF Prime continues.","Lending products: The loan against shares product launched in the last two quarters has seen \"good traction\". The personal loan business is \"gradually growing\".","Power trader platform: The CFO said 915 is \"not a significant contributor\" to volume yet. The goal is to win power traders from other platforms, but this \"hasn't been yet kind of proven\".","Multi-product usage: More than 50% of customers use more than two products, and more than 5% use more than four. The CFO said the company will not push customers into extra products."],"commitments":[{"c":"Annual Cost to Grow","s":"INR400 crores to INR500 crores; \"INR500 crores plus\" in a good year; closer to INR400 crores in a bad year","t":"Per year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Quarterly Cost to Grow","s":"Around INR125 crores average; may exceed it if market does well","t":"Q3, Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MTF book additions","s":"INR600 crores to INR700 crores per quarter; \"expectation is that trend should continue\"","t":"\"foreseeable future\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"CAS normalisation","s":"Volumes to \"normalize\"; awaiting circular for applicability date","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MTF haircut optimisation","s":"Faster haircut changes and better review \"are in pipeline\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"US stocks full launch","s":"No timeline given: \"I don't think we have the timeline like this\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"New products disclosure","s":"Products still in work; \"we'll probably be in a position to talk about them\"","t":"\"in future calls\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Employee cost trend","s":"\"Structurally the cost is going to go up\"; Q2 dip a short-term blip","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MTF share of cash turnover","s":"Slightly above 5%; \"we expect… that number will kind of go up\"","t":"\"foreseeable future\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"UPI MDR response","s":"Awaiting final outcome; to work with regulator; \"not going to be a material change\" expected","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited volume dependence on market volatility, regulatory disruptions such as CAS, and UPI MDR cap uncertainty.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 14 Oct 2026; physical page numbers). Guidance p5, p12; K1 p3–4; K2 p3, p12; K3 p4, p12; K4 p7–8; K5 p7–8; R1 p10; R2 p16; R3 p16; R4 p9; R5 p8–9; F1 p5–6; F2 p14; F3 p13; F4 p14–15; P1 p7; P2 p7; P3 p14; P4 p11; P5 p13; C1 p12; C2 p12; C3 p5; C4 p4; C5 p5; C6 p7; C7 p7; C8 p6; C9 p16; C10 p15; M1 p4; M2–M3 p12; M4 p5; M5 p16; M6 p8; M7 p10; M8 p16; M9 p13; Risks p12, p15."},
  {"name":"Rossari Biotech Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 revenue growth raised from the \"initial assessment of 12%, 14%\" to \"between mid-teen to a little high teen\". FY27 EBITDA margin maintained at \"around 12% plus/minus\".","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR697.6 crores, +19% YoY"},{"m":"Gross profit","v":"INR220.3 crores, +19% YoY, +6.7% QoQ"},{"m":"Gross margin","v":"31.6% vs 29.6% in Q1"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR87 crore, +21% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"12.5% vs 12.3% Q2 FY26, 11.6% Q1 FY27"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR38.9 crores, +5.5% YoY, +11% QoQ"},{"m":"Diluted EPS","v":"7.02"},{"m":"HPPC revenue","v":"INR 541.9 crores, +18% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 HPPC revenue","v":"INR1095.1 crore, +20%"},{"m":"Textile chemicals revenue","v":"INR120.8 crores, +26% YoY; H1 INR226.9 crores, +24%"},{"m":"Animal Health and Nutrition revenue","v":"INR34.8 crores, +11% YoY; H1 INR72.8 crores, +19%"},{"m":"Core B2B revenue","v":"INR619.9 crores, +21% YoY"},{"m":"Core B2B EBITDA","v":"INR87.2 crores, +15% YoY; margin 14.1% vs 14.8%"},{"m":"Institutional & B2C","v":"Revenue INR77.7 crores, +3% YoY; EBITDA INR0.2 crores vs loss INR3.7 crores"},{"m":"Export share","v":"31% of Q2 revenue; 27% in H1"},{"m":"Capacity utilisation","v":"Blended close to 60%; new ethoxylation about 30%"},{"m":"Gross borrowings","v":"INR361.8 crores, reduced around INR46 crores in H1"},{"m":"Net debt","v":"INR221 crores vs INR248 crores June, INR281 crores March"},{"m":"Net debt to equity","v":"0.16"},{"m":"Working capital days","v":"86 days vs 97 days at end FY26"}],"takeaways":["Revenue guidance raised, hedged: The CFO raised FY27 growth to mid-to-high teens but said \"it's very difficult for me to hazard a guess what's going to happen in the second half\" given geopolitics, demand and logistics.","Margin gains deferred: The CFO kept FY27 EBITDA \"around 12%\". He said the initiatives are \"still work-in-progress\", and \"probably next year, we should start seeing us improvements in the EBITDA percentage\".","B2C exit still planned: The CFO said the company is still \"moving out of this B2C business… at an appropriate time and at an appropriate value\". Institutional/B2C broke even this quarter after portfolio rationalisation.","Saudi project early stage: The Board approved leasing land in KSA for a greenfield plant. Capex, returns, products and timelines will be shared after internal finalisation and a further Board approval.","MD declines to forecast: The MD said shipments via Bab-el-Mandeb or Hormuz are uncertain and containers and ISO tanks are unavailable: \"we do not want to hazard any guess\"."],"revenue":["Volume-led growth: The CFO said Q2 growth was \"most of it is a volume-driven growth\". Prices of some products were cut after raw material prices fell in early Q2.","Export share rising: Exports were 31% of Q2 revenue and 27% of H1, up 34% YoY and almost 35% QoQ. HPPC (personal care, oil and gas) and textiles drove the growth, and textile exports rose about 15% YoY.","HPPC constrained by EO: Value-added personal care, agro surfactants, adjuvants and oil and gas solutions led HPPC growth. Limited ethylene oxide availability capped ethoxylate volumes, keeping HPPC broadly flat QoQ.","Textile and AHN drivers: Textiles grew about 14% QoQ on exports to Bangladesh, Southeast Asia, Turkey and Latin America, plus Thailand. Edward Menezes said Q2 is AHN's weakest quarter, with Q3 and Q4 the peak.","Oil and gas geographies: The MD said oil and gas sales focus on India, the Middle East, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan. U.S. distributors sell to the industry, but the company lacks segment-wise figures."],"profit":["Freight squeezes core margin: The CFO said freight costs \"almost doubled, tripled\" YoY and phenol prices rose. Most raw material increases were passed on, but \"not all customers are really ready to absorb the entire freight increase.\"","Mix lifts gross margin: Gross margin rose about 200 bps from 29.6% in Q1 on better mix. The MD said \"tail cutting\" stopped the bottom 5% of products with the lowest margins.","Working capital improving: The CFO cited 86 days in Q2 (prepared remarks) and \"about 87 days in September\" (Q&A), against a 90-day aim. He credited inventory rationalisation and collections, not one-offs.","Cash goes to debt: The CFO said \"currently, all cash flows will go into debt reduction\". KSA funding will be equity plus debt, sized later. The MD cited positive cash flow of INR131 crores in H1.","ROCE recovery timing: The CFO said ROCE will improve as new capacities ramp up, with an upward trajectory \"probably in the next year\". The MD attributed the longer working capital cycle to in-house ethoxylation with advance raw material purchases."],"pipeline":["Ethoxylation ramp-up: The new ethoxylation facility is at about 30% utilisation and blended utilisation is close to 60%. Peak utilisation is expected by end FY28, with peak revenue at three times capex value.","EO supply from Reliance: The MD said Reliance's expansion should kick in in Q1 of next financial year, bringing a higher EO allocation. He said \"there is no confirmation on what is the incremental quantities\".","Pharma excipient build-out: EXCIPACT registration was received for the Unitop plant. Pharma exports were about INR50 crores last year. The MD said approvals take six months to two years, and the ramp-up \"is always slow, but it's steady\".","Capex winding down: Most projects close by year end, covering pharma, aroma, hydrogenation and biosurfactant expansion. The CFO expects no significant capex next year, with India capex then low and the focus on KSA.","International footprint: The new Thailand blending facility is ramping up to serve Southeast Asia. The company targets new customers in Southeast Asia, Bangladesh, Turkey, Latin America and Africa."],"commitments":[{"c":"B2C exit","s":"Move out of B2C business \"at an appropriate time and at an appropriate value\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"B2C profitability","s":"H2 EBITDA positive; at least breakeven for the full year","t":"H2; full year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Peak ethoxylation utilisation","s":"Peak utilisation; peak revenue three times capex value","t":"End of FY '28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Reliance EO allocation","s":"Higher EO allocation when Reliance expansion kicks in; quantity not confirmed","t":"\"first quarter of… next financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"New ethoxylation utilisation","s":"\"Healthy utilization\" of the new ethoxylation facility","t":"\"Q2 or Q3 of next year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"KSA land activities","s":"Lease land; fencing and boundary wall; return to Board with full plan","t":"\"next few months\"; \"next eight to 12 months\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Capex completion","s":"Most projects close; no significant capex spend next year","t":"End of this year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Export share target","s":"\"At least 30% plus\" of revenue from exports","t":"\"by the end of this year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 EBITDA margin","s":"\"Around 12% plus/minus\"; improvement expected next year","t":"FY27; next year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 revenue growth","s":"Raised from \"12%, 14%\" to \"between mid-teen to a little high teen\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Analyst plant visit","s":"Plant visit to be organised \"pretty soon\"","t":"\"pretty soon\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Working capital debt-free","s":"Working capital debt to be cleared; working capital days within 87 to 90 days in March","t":"\"mid of next year\"; March","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Volatile raw material prices, Middle East freight and logistics pressure, rising insurance costs, and cautious customer buying across end-user industries.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p13; K1 p13; K2 p13; K3 p4–5; K4 p2, p8–9; K5 p14; R1 p8; R2 p4, p11; R3 p2; R4 p2, p8; R5 p5; F1 p7; F2 p2, p4; F3 p3, p15; F4 p6, p14; F5 p16; P1 p5, p8; P2 p10–11; P3 p6, p9; P4 p6, p11–12; P5 p1–2; C1 p4–5; C2 p4; C3 p5; C4 p10–11; C5 p8; C6 p8–9; C7 p11; C8 p4; C9 p13; C10 p13; C11 p14; C12 p14–15; M1–M7 p2; M8–M10 p2; M11 p3; M12–M14 p3; M15 p4; M16 p8; M17–M20 p3; Risks p1, p14. Omitted for inconsistency: the AHN \"INR27-odd crores\" (conflicts with INR34.8 crores), the \"similar to the 100\" revenue remark (garbled), and the H2 capex figures (INR50–75 crores vs about INR50 crores)."},
  {"name":"Hindustan Zinc Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 zinc cost of production guidance improved to $825–$850 per ton, from $975–$1,000. Silver 680 tons and capex about INR5,000 crore maintained.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR15,093 crore, +77% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR9,285 crore, +108% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"62%"},{"m":"Net profit","v":"INR6,412 crore, +142% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR28,840 crore, +77% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"INR17,359 crore, +108% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 net profit","v":"INR11,881 crore, +143% YoY"},{"m":"Zinc cost of production","v":"$703 per ton, better by 29%; H1 $776 per ton"},{"m":"Mine metal production","v":"+5% YoY; highest ever second quarter"},{"m":"Refined metal production","v":"+7% YoY"},{"m":"Saleable silver production","v":"173 tons, +20% YoY"},{"m":"Lead concentrate sold","v":"10 KT; INR300 crore revenue, INR155 crore profit"},{"m":"Ore grade","v":"Around 7.6%"},{"m":"Free cash flow before growth capex","v":"INR6,389 crore"},{"m":"Net cash (end Q2)","v":"INR11,268 crore"},{"m":"Q2 capex","v":"Around INR1,200 crore sustaining; around INR700 crore growth"},{"m":"Domestic coal usage","v":"32%"},{"m":"Renewable energy share","v":"27% of power consumption"},{"m":"H1 FY27 dividend","v":"INR11 per share; total shareholder return 15%"}],"takeaways":["Cost guidance lowered sharply: The CEO cut full-year zinc cost of production guidance from $975–$1,000 to $825–$850 per ton. Q2 was $703 per ton, 29% better.","Sulfuric acid offsets geopolitical costs: The CFO said sulfuric acid by-product realisation gave a \"positive surprise\", while geopolitical cost escalation hit \"almost by $50\". He declined sulfuric guidance: \"absolutely dependent upon the market\".","Silver guidance reaffirmed: The CFO is \"confident\" of 680 tons for FY27, saying H1 was effectively 340 tons including 20 tons in concentrate. For FY28, the CEO said of 730–750 tons, \"We hope to do that.\"","Silver drives profitability: Saleable silver contributed approximately 45% of first half profitability. The CEO said it \"remains very important to us\".","Concentrate sales opportunistic: The 10 KT of lead concentrate sold earned INR300 crore revenue and INR155 crore profit. The CFO said there is no set plan; sales depend on concentrate exceeding metal capacity."],"revenue":["Revenue growth drivers: The CFO attributed growth to higher metal production, supportive commodity prices, better by-product realisation, lead concentrate sales and a stronger dollar.","Production records: Mine metal production was the highest ever for a second quarter, up 5% YoY. Refined metal rose 7% YoY, aided by debottlenecking and additional roasters.","Market backdrop: The CEO cited a 2026 refined zinc deficit of almost 180 KT (demand 13.87 million tons vs supply 13.69 million tons). Silver is in deficit for a sixth year, at about 1.44 thousand tons."],"profit":["Hedging position: Zinc hedges are 40 KT for Q3 and 36 KT for Q4. Silver is 8 tons hedged for Q3 only, at $68 per ounce.","Cash and returns: H1 total shareholder return was 15%, including INR64 per share capital appreciation and INR11 dividend. The CFO said the balance sheet gives \"ample headroom\" for growth and shareholder returns.","Energy mix: Domestic coal usage was 32% in Q2. Renewable energy was 27% of power consumption, targeted at 35% \"in quarters to come\".","Exchequer contribution: The CFO said the company contributed more than INR13,000 crore to the national exchequer in H1 through taxes, royalties, duties and levies."],"pipeline":["250 KT zinc expansion: Engineering and procurement for the 250 KT per annum integrated zinc expansion is \"almost complete\", and mine development is underway.","Tailings reprocessing plant: The 10 million tons per annum tailings reprocessing plant is \"progressing as per plan\".","Critical mineral blocks: Three of the four critical mineral blocks are exploration blocks, with exploration to finish in Q3 FY29. The Gundlupet REE and Yttrium mining lease is executed, with a resource indication in two to three years.","AI and electrification: The CEO said the company aims to make processes \"AI-native\". It introduced its first electric crane and electric cherry picker, and signed an MOU for 30 more electric vehicles."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 zinc COP","s":"$825 to $850 per ton; revised from $975 to $1,000","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 saleable silver","s":"\"Confident we will be able to deliver our guidance of 680 tons\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY28 silver","s":"730–750 tons asked; \"We hope to do that\"; numbers to be finalised","t":"FY28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 capex","s":"\"In the range of INR5,000 crore only\"; more spend expected in Q3 and Q4","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Renewable energy share","s":"From 27% to 35% of power consumption","t":"\"in quarters to come\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Critical mineral exploration","s":"Exploration program completion; then production plan","t":"Q3 FY29","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gundlupet REE block","s":"Resource indication and business plan from mining lease","t":"\"in two to three years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"250 KT zinc expansion","s":"Engineering and procurement almost complete; mine development underway; \"on track\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Tailings reprocessing plant","s":"10 million tons per annum; \"progressing as per plan\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Net zero","s":"Committed to achieving net zero","t":"By 2050","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The CFO cited escalating geopolitical tension, energy and trade disruptions, changing tariff policy and near-term volatility in inflation, currency and financial markets.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026). Guidance p2, p5–6; K1 p2; K2 p4, p6; K3 p6; K4 p2; K5 p5–6; R1 p2; R2 p1–2; R3 p1; F1 p3–4; F2 p3; F3 p1, p4; F4 p3; P1 p2; P2 p2; P3 p5; P4 p1–2; C1 p2; C2 p6; C3 p6; C4 p5; C5 p1; C6 p5; C7 p5; C8 p2; C9 p2; C10 p1; M1–M3 p2; M4 p3; M5–M7 p3; M8 p2–3; M9–M11 p1–2; M12 p5; M13 p5; M14–M15 p3; M16 p4; M17 p4; M18 p1; M19 p3; Risks p2."},
  {"name":"L&T Technology Services Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"No quarterly or annual guidance. \"H2 will be better than H1\" sequentially and YoY. Mid-16% EBIT to be held after the Q3 wage hike. Aspiration of 13–15% revenue CAGR with 16–17% EBIT reiterated.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue (USD)","v":"$317.1 million, +2.7% QoQ, +4.8% YoY CC"},{"m":"Revenue (INR)","v":"Rs 3,040 crores, +3.4% QoQ, +12.1% YoY"},{"m":"EBIT margin","v":"16.3%, +60 bps QoQ, +220 bps YoY"},{"m":"Net income","v":"Rs 548 crores, +66.8% YoY; includes Rs 165 crores one-time gain"},{"m":"EPS (continuing operations)","v":"Rs 35.78; annualised Rs 143.12 vs FY26 Rs 115.89"},{"m":"Large deal TCV","v":"$203 million; over 50% AI-led"},{"m":"Sustainability revenue","v":"+13.4% YoY, +4.9% QoQ; 38% of revenue; margin 29.5%"},{"m":"Mobility","v":"+2.3% QoQ; margin 16.9%, +130 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Tech segment margin","v":"12%, +50 bps QoQ"},{"m":"AI-led revenue","v":"$120 million annualised; around 9.5% of revenue"},{"m":"Offshore and fixed-price mix","v":"Offshore 55.9%; fixed-price 35.6%"},{"m":"Headcount","v":"24,035 vs 23,845 in Q1"},{"m":"Attrition","v":"14.7%"},{"m":"DSO","v":"78 days (billed 60, unbilled 18) vs 77 in Q1"},{"m":"H1 FY27 free cash flow","v":"Rs 790 crores, 108% of net income"},{"m":"Cash and investments","v":"Rs 4,197 crores"},{"m":"Effective tax rate","v":"26%"},{"m":"Interim dividend","v":"Rs 20 per share"},{"m":"Realized rupee","v":"95.9 to the dollar, 1.09% depreciation QoQ"}],"takeaways":["Margin aspiration reached early: EBIT reached the mid-16% aspiration \"ahead of time by couple of quarters\", helped by segment mix, AI revenue and quality of revenues. The CFO said near-term margins stay \"around the mid-16%\".","Q3 wage hike absorbed: Wage hikes will be given in Q3, with an impact of 50 to 80 bps. The CEO said holding mid-16% \"is after considering all of this\".","H2 better, Q3 muted: The CEO declined quarterly or annual guidance, saying only \"H2 will be better than H1\". He called Q3 a \"muted quarter\", with regular furloughs expected.","Deal win catch-up: The CEO said \"I still owe you all $100 million from last quarter\". He pledged the extra $100 million in Q3 and Q4 to keep a $200 million pace.","SWC divestment completed: The SWC divestment closed on schedule, booking a one-time gain of Rs 165 crores in Q2. From Q3 only the continuing business will be reported."],"revenue":["Sustainability leads growth: Growth came as large deals won over six quarters were executed, across data centers, energy transition and robotics. Sustainability is 38% of revenue and nearly half (CEO: \"more than 50%\") of Q2 large deals.","Mobility mix: Growth came from Auto North America, Aero and Rail, and Truck and Off-Highway, while Auto Europe stayed muted. Auto is about 50% of mobility, and four new truck and off-highway logos were added.","Tech segment mixed: A medtech program ramped down as scheduled, with the next phase only in H2. Semcon and tech infra grew, and media is \"steady\". The CEO expects tech \"muted in Q3\" before growth returns.","AI-led revenue: Annualised AI-led revenue is $120 million, around 9.5% of revenue. AI revenue counts only where \"a significant part of the deliverable\" uses the AI stack, and the CEO said \"we do charge a premium\".","Deal tenures: The CEO said tenures have \"increased a little\". Rough norms: 5–7 years for a $100 million deal, 4–5 years for $50 million, about 3 years for $25 million."],"profit":["Segment margin drivers: Mobility margins rose on quality of growth and efficiency, and sustainability on large-deal execution. Tech improved on the telecom AI-led deal ramp-up. Margins expanded in all segments.","Tax rate outlook: The effective tax rate was 26%. The CFO expects it to stay \"in the range of 26% to 26.5%\".","DSO comfortable: The CFO said DSO is \"well below our guided range of 80 to 85 days\" and \"this is where we likely operate\". Only overall DSO will be reported from now on.","Margin levers: The CFO named revenue growth, healthy segment mix, AI-led productivity and disciplined SG&A. He declined to quantify AI-led profitability.","Cash generation: Year-to-date free cash flow was 108% of net income. The board approved an interim dividend of Rs 20 per share."],"pipeline":["Pipeline and backlog up: The CEO said the pipeline is up \"more than 25% year on year\", and order backlog is up. Over 50% of the $203 million large deal TCV was AI-led.","Data center business line: A dedicated data center engineering line serves AI infrastructure, servers, storage, ODMs and OEMs. A large AI-led telecom equipment engagement started ramping up this quarter.","AI capabilities: More than 16,000 engineers have been upskilled. EIOne has 100+ AI solutions, and AI patent filings crossed 253, out of 1,808 total. An NVIDIA physical AI lab was launched.","Auto Europe recovery view: The President expects Europe auto to be \"steady for now\" for 6 to 9 months, with growth back \"in the next 9 to 12 months\". Large European deals are in the pipeline.","Partnerships and programs: The partner ecosystem now includes Cognite, Anthropic, NVIDIA and Databricks, with MIT Media Labs workshops. A Farm program for deep-tech startups and a Distinguished Fellows program were launched."],"commitments":[{"c":"H2 revenue","s":"\"H2 will be better year-on-year and quarter-on-quarter than H1\"; no quarterly guidance","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Near-term EBIT margin","s":"\"Around the mid-16%\" EBIT levels, after wage hike","t":"\"near future\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Wage hike","s":"Wage hikes in Q3; impact \"in the range of… 50 to 80 bps\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Long-term aspiration","s":"13 to 15% revenue CAGR, EBIT 16 to 17%","t":"\"next 3 to 5 years\" (CEO); \"next 5 years\" (CFO)","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Large deal pace","s":"Extra $100 million owed; keep \"$200 million pace\"","t":"Q3 and Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Effective tax rate","s":"26% to 26.5%","t":"\"Going ahead\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"DSO range","s":"Guided 80 to 85 days; comfortable at current levels","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Auto Europe recovery","s":"\"Should bounce back next year\"; growth in \"next 9 to 12 months\"","t":"Next year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Medtech next phase","s":"Next phase of ramped-down program; CEO \"careful and say Q4\"","t":"H2; Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Systems and platforms","s":"Growth trajectory \"will accelerate\"","t":"\"sometime in Q4 and beyond\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Sustainability growth","s":"\"On track to deliver another double-digit growth year\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"AI revenue disclosure","s":"Will quantify AI-led revenue \"every quarter\"","t":"Quarterly","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The CEO said Middle East exposure is small but \"as these things drag on, God knows what will happen\". Pure-play software is impacted by new-age AI tools.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026). Guidance p3–4, p9, p11; K1 p3, p10; K2 p11, p13; K3 p9, p11; K4 p9; K5 p1, p3, p8; R1 p2–3, p6; R2 p2, p6; R3 p2–3, p8; R4 p1, p13; R5 p13; F1 p4; F2 p3; F3 p4, p8; F4 p4, p13; F5 p4; P1 p1, p9; P2 p2, p12; P3 p1–2; P4 p14; P5 p2; C1 p9, p11; C2 p4, p10; C3 p11, p13; C4 p3–4; C5 p9; C6 p3; C7 p4, p8; C8 p2, p6; C9 p3, p8; C10 p8; C11 p2; C12 p15; M1 p1, p4; M2 p3; M3 p3; M4 p3; M5 p3; M6 p1; M7 p2–4; M8 p2, p4; M9 p4; M10 p1, p13; M11 p4; M12–M13 p4; M14 p4; M15–M16 p4; M17 p3; M18 p4; M19 p4; Risks p3, p7."},
  {"name":"Crompton Greaves Consumer Electricals Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"Medium-term guidance maintained (\"very well on track\"); an analyst cited the slide's FY29 EBITDA margin ambition of 11%–12%, and the CFO replied \"when we reach a 12% EBITDA\".","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue","v":"INR2,263 crores, +18.2% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR205 crores, +24.1% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"9%, +40 bps YoY (MD); CFO closing: \"expanding… by 20 bps\""},{"m":"PBT excluding exceptional items","v":"+36.2% YoY"},{"m":"PAT","v":"+65% YoY; PAT margin +160 bps (base had INR20 crores exceptional)"},{"m":"H1 FY27 A&P spend","v":"Around INR100 crores vs historical INR250–260 crores a year"},{"m":"ECD","v":"+19.4% YoY; EBIT margin 11.6%"},{"m":"BLDC fans","v":"Growth of 40%"},{"m":"Small domestic appliances","v":"Growth in excess of 20%"},{"m":"Lighting","v":"INR298 crores, +14.3% YoY; EBITDA 12.9%"},{"m":"Butterfly","v":"+15.9% YoY excluding in-house sale in base; core 16% (CFO)"},{"m":"Exports","v":"Growth of more than 70%; highest ever quarterly revenue"},{"m":"New businesses share of revenue","v":"Around 10% (share of revenue, per CFO)"},{"m":"Cost increases passed on","v":"About 80%"},{"m":"Fans price hikes (last nine months)","v":"17% to 18% cumulative"},{"m":"AP solar rooftop order","v":"38,000 units; 20,000 installed"},{"m":"General trade share","v":"80% of business"}],"takeaways":["Price hikes cover most inflation: About 80% of cost increases have been passed on. The rest is to come from Unnati savings and operating leverage. The MD said price rises are \"a continuing process now\".","Q1 supply disruption behind: The MD said the supply chain disruptions that hit Q1 \"are behind us\". Inventory days are now back to about the levels of a year ago.","Margin outlook not quantified: Asked whether gross margin would improve, the MD said \"Tough to say… this is a commodity market which is pretty unpredictable just now\". He committed to passing on further cost increases.","LKA restructured under Butterfly: LKA and Rhion now report to Butterfly's CBO and will use Butterfly's ecosystem. LKA's EBITDA loss was between INR25 crores and INR16–17 crores at various points in time.","No separate cost centres: The MD said new businesses will not get \"fresh cost centers and heavy people expenses\". Instead, they will use the existing go-to-market, supply chain and innovation infrastructure."],"revenue":["Channel mix: General trade and rural trade both grew double digits. E-commerce was subdued because its go-to-market was repositioned to improve channel profitability. General trade is 80% of the business.","Solar pumps slowdown: Solar pump orders fell as KUSUM 1.1, now nearing completion, tapered off. The MD expects a pickup next quarter after the government announced KUSUM 2.0.","Volume returns after pricing: The CFO said Q2 is the first quarter in which price increases were largely passed on and volume grew positively in all categories. In fans, volumes grew in both general trade and rural.","Butterfly growth price-led: The CFO said the large part of Butterfly's growth was price-led. The Idea First Series contributes more than a double-digit share of business. Pressure cooker growth is disproportionate after the GST cut.","Exports mix and markets: Exports are largely fans, with some pumps, LDA and SDA, sold mainly in Africa, the Middle East, Southeast Asia and South Asia. The MD declined to name countries, citing competitive reasons."],"profit":["Lighting margin hit: Lighting EBITDA was lower than last year, \"largely due to the B2B business being impacted by pre-contracted orders\".","Solar mix effect: The CFO said gross margin, excluding solar rooftop, is back to similar levels. Solar rooftop and pumps weigh on mix, but installation costs are \"pretty much nil\" between gross margin and EBITDA.","A&P not comparable: A&P fell because e-commerce promotions were cut. Brand relaunch costs were booked outside A&P, and new businesses need no A&P. The MD said A&P is not being used for \"cost management\".","Export margin profile: Export margins are \"a little lower\" than domestic. The MD said this is compensated because exports get no credit: \"It's cash.\"","Butterfly margin stance: Butterfly's gross margin has a \"marginal difference\" from last year. The CFO wants to hold it there \"because key is to capture the market share for Butterfly\"."],"pipeline":["Greenfield plant evaluation: Evaluation of a greenfield manufacturing plant for fans, appliances and wires is in advanced stages. Today pumps, appliances and new businesses are mostly outsourced, and the MD said there are no supply constraints.","Solar rooftop build-out: Of the 38,000-unit Andhra Pradesh order, 20,000 units are installed. The MD put the solar rooftop market at INR26,000 crores. The company will bid for government tenders selectively, only where profitability is worthwhile.","Wires expansion: Wires launched in South India in Q1 FY27. Wires was then extended to Punjab, Delhi NCR and Gujarat. The CFO called wires \"very very nascent\".","Brand refresh: In August 2026 the company launched a refreshed brand identity, with a new monogram, a new tag and a sonic identity.","Butterfly national expansion: Butterfly's pan-India push starts with Maharashtra, using a regional campaign for its new five-star gas cooktop. The MD said the cooktop saves two cylinders a year.","Infrared cooktop opportunity: The CFO called infrared cooktops an \"emerging opportunity\" and said recent buying was a \"one-time spike\". Butterfly leads cooktops on a pan-India basis, and the CFO sees a \"right to win\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"Medium-term guidance","s":"\"We believe we are very well on track for our medium-term guidance\"","t":"Medium term","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY29 EBITDA margin","s":"Analyst cited 11%–12% ambition from slide 12; CFO: \"when we reach a 12% EBITDA… that's on a higher revenue base\"","t":"FY29","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Crompton 2.0 revenue","s":"Double revenue, growing the business \"at about 15%\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Solar pumps ramp-up","s":"Pickup with KUSUM 2.0; \"ramp up accordingly\"","t":"\"next quarter\"; \"coming quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"LKA restructuring","s":"Restructured under Butterfly to \"materially reduce the overall cost structure\" and move towards profitability","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Greenfield plant","s":"Evaluation \"in advanced stages\" for fans, appliances, wires","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Export growth","s":"\"Hopefully… this rate of growth will sustain\"","t":"\"next couple of years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"AP solar rooftop order","s":"Completion \"pretty much\" in coming quarter, \"with some spillover in the quarter thereafter\"","t":"Coming quarter","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 A&P spend","s":"\"Try to keep absolute similar to last year\", depending on macro headwinds","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Butterfly Maharashtra campaign","s":"Regional campaign for five-star cooktop","t":"\"very soon\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"New businesses share","s":"About 14% to 15% of revenue","t":"\"in the long run\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Fans growth vs category","s":"1.5 times to 1.7 times category growth (Investor Day target)","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited commodity cost pressures, \"unpredictable geopolitical developments\" and a \"challenging macro environment\"; A&P plans depend on \"how the macro headwinds come through\".","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026). Guidance p3, p18–19; K1 p4–6; K2 p1, p9–10; K3 p14; K4 p2, p15; K5 p15–16; R1 p2, p18; R2 p2; R3 p17–18; R4 p12–13; R5 p10; F1 p3; F2 p6, p8–9; F3 p11–12; F4 p11; F5 p13; P1 p3, p16–17; P2 p5, p7–8; P3 p2, p15; P4 p1; P5 p13–14; P6 p12, p14; C1 p3; C2 p18–19; C3 p19; C4 p2; C5 p2, p15; C6 p3; C7 p10; C8 p5; C9 p12; C10 p13; C11 p15; C12 p18; M1–M2 p2; M3 p2, p19; M4–M5 p2; M6 p12; M7–M9 p2; M10–M11 p3; M11 also p12; M12 p3; M13 p6, p11; M14 p4–5; M15 p9; M16 p8; M17 p18; Risks p2, p12."},
  {"name":"HFCL Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 revenue growth raised to \"at least 80%\", from 40% after Q1 and about 20% at the start of the year. INR10,000 crores revenue expected \"much before\" FY29.","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated revenue","v":"INR2,541.79 crores, +143.62% YoY, +32.73% QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR658 crores, +223.55% YoY, +47.78% QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"25.89%, +640 bps YoY, +264 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Profit before tax","v":"INR538.60 crores vs INR106.34 crores, +406.49% YoY"},{"m":"Profit after tax","v":"INR394.22 crores vs INR71.92 crores, +448.14% YoY"},{"m":"PAT margin","v":"15.51%, +862 bps YoY, +268 bps QoQ"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PBT","v":"INR870.12 crores vs INR61.64 crores, +1,311.62%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR639.86 crores vs INR42.62 crores, +1,401.31%"},{"m":"Export growth","v":"Approximately 493% YoY"},{"m":"Order book","v":"INR28,900 crores; previously INR26,000 crores"},{"m":"OFC and DCI order book","v":"INR18,500 crores; \"maybe 70%\" of exports from hyperscalers"},{"m":"Revenue mix","v":"Data center 65% to 75%; telcos 25% to 35%"},{"m":"Q2 connectivity revenue","v":"About INR300 crores; FY27 expectation INR800 crores"},{"m":"Fiber capacity utilisation","v":"100%"},{"m":"Fiber prices","v":"Up \"some 20%\""},{"m":"Planned capex","v":"Approximately INR1,800 crores"},{"m":"Cable capacity expansion","v":"34 to 63 million fiber kilometers"},{"m":"Fiber capacity expansion","v":"28 to 34 to 43 million"},{"m":"Turnkey contract EBITDA margin","v":"\"Something like 15% to 20%\""}],"takeaways":["Guidance raised again: The MD raised FY27 growth to at least 80%. Asked whether this implies a flat H2, he said \"it is always good to be conservative\", with no preform supply constraint.","Aerospace acquisition closing: Completion is expected this quarter (\"within November\"). Its order book was given as approximately INR1,600 crores and as INR1,800 crores. Revenue will be consolidated after completion.","Defense scale-up targets: The MD gave FY27 defense revenue as \"approximately INR500 crores\" and \"INR500 crores to INR600 crores\". He targets at least INR2,000 crores next year and around INR5,000 crores in \"three years'\" (also \"next two years\").","EPC losses to narrow: The MD said the EPC loss came from BharatNet expenses billed late because of milestones. Losses over the next two quarters together \"should not be more than INR75 crores to INR100 crores\", with profit from Q1 next year.","Not a peak quarter: The MD said \"we do not see this quarter as a peak\". There was no one-time high-margin order: \"there is no diamonds falling down from the sky\"."],"revenue":["Hyperscaler-led order book: Of the INR18,500 crores OFC and data center interconnect book, \"maybe 70%\" of export orders are from hyperscalers. Domestic orders come mostly from telcos.","Data center vs telco mix: The MD put data center at 65% to 75% of revenue and telcos at 25% to 35%. Data center A2 fiber costs about $25 versus about $15 for telco D fiber.","Volume and price both: The MD said Q2 growth came from both volume and price but declined to split them: \"If I give, that would be an incorrect statement\". Fiber prices rose \"some 20%\" and are \"more or less stabilizing\".","Telco demand returning: The MD said telcos had not bought much for two years because of high prices. They are returning, with \"very concrete inquiries\" and possible large orders, including from international telcos.","Capacity fully utilised: The MD said fiber runs at 100% capacity, and cable is at 100% \"in money terms\", with data center lines running 24/7."],"profit":["Margin outlook improving: The MD said margins are \"going to get better only\". Drivers are more connectivity solutions, high-capacity cables with limited suppliers, higher capacity and backward integration into preform.","Contract price resets: Preform supply is on 12- to 24-month contracts with \"no pressure\". Long-term customer contracts have yearly price reset clauses; short four-to-six-month contracts lack them but carry higher prices.","Preform market prices: The MD said spot preform prices have risen \"600%\", are \"insane\" and \"have to come down\" as capacity is added. Pre-done contracts mean \"that would not mean any dent\" on HFCL's profitability.","Debt reduction ahead: The MD said \"the debt reduction will start in the current year\". He said March '27 and March '28 debt \"will substantially go down\".","O&M and turnkey margins: The MD said O&M margins are broadly the same as normal turnkey contracts. He put turnkey EBITDA margin at \"something like 15% to 20%\"."],"pipeline":["Capacity expansion: About INR1,800 crores of investment is planned across optical fiber, cable and preform. Fiber capacity goes from 28 to 34 to 43 million fkm, and cable from 34 to 63 million fkm.","Own preform plant: A 600 MT preform facility, timeline October 28, covers about half of requirement; delays of two to three months are absorbed. A \"mid-term solution\" starts Q1 next calendar year, but the MD declined detail: \"trade secret\".","Connectivity solutions ramp: Connectivity revenue is expected at more than INR800 crores in FY27, about INR300 crores in Q2, and INR2,000 crores next year. Products are already certified for use inside data centers.","Defense products pipeline: The second batch of AK-203 thermal sights was delivered. The foliage penetration radar's second trial is due in about two weeks. Bids include drone detection radar and 800,000 multi-mode hand grenades.","Ammunition complex: Andhra Pradesh allotted 1,000 acres for an ammunition complex. Construction \"will start soon\", with revenue in about two years.","EPC restructuring: A board committee advised by E&Y is finalising restructuring recommendations, with a board meeting in about a week or 10 days. The Army AMC via BSNL is in final approval."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 revenue growth","s":"\"At least 80%\"; raised from 40% after Q1, about 20% at start","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"INR10,000 crore revenue","s":"\"Would not be surprised\" to reach it \"much before\" FY29","t":"FY29","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Aerospace acquisition","s":"Completion; order book approximately INR1,600 crores / INR1,800 crores","t":"\"current quarter\"; \"within November\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 defense revenue","s":"Approximately INR500 crores; also \"INR500 crores to INR600 crores\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Defense revenue ramp","s":"At least INR2,000 crores next year; around INR5,000 crores","t":"\"three years' timeframe\" / \"next two years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Connectivity revenue","s":"More than INR800 crores FY27; INR2,000 crores next year","t":"FY27; next year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EPC losses and profit","s":"Next two quarters' loss ≤INR75–100 crores; profit from \"first quarter of next year\"","t":"Next two quarters","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EPC restructuring decision","s":"Board meeting after committee recommendation; finalise in \"another couple of weeks\"","t":"Couple of weeks","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Own preform availability","s":"600 MT facility; \"October 28 timeline… would be met\"; mid-term solution from Q1 next calendar year","t":"October 28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Debt reduction","s":"\"Debt reduction will start in the current year\"; \"substantially go down\"","t":"March '27, March '28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q3 revenue growth","s":"\"There will definitely be growth in Q3\", barring geopolitical events","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ammunition complex","s":"Construction \"will start soon\"; revenue \"in about two years' time\"","t":"About two years","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The MD cited geopolitical events \"nobody can predict\", Middle East political conditions, and possible slower data center build-out at heavily debt-reliant operators.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026). Guidance p2; K1 p2–4; K2 p2, p5, p8; K3 p2, p5, p19; K4 p9; K5 p2, p12; R1 p3; R2 p14–15; R3 p15, p22; R4 p5; R5 p6–7, p11; F1 p4, p8; F2 p7, p23; F3 p24; F4 p12; F5 p12; P1 p2, p8; P2 p13, p17, p23; P3 p8, p21–22, p24; P4 p2, p5–6; P5 p2, p6; P6 p9, p12; C1 p2; C2 p2, p10; C3 p2, p5, p8; C4 p2, p5; C5 p5, p19; C6 p8, p22; C7 p9; C8 p12; C9 p13; C10 p12; C11 p14; C12 p2, p6; M1–M9 p1; M10 p1, p18; M11 p3; M12 p14; M13 p21; M14 p6, p11; M15 p15; M16 p2, p20; M17 p17; M18 p8; M19 p12; Risks p5, p10, p26."},
  {"name":"Le Travenues Technology Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"No formal guidance. Hotel investment of INR10–12 crore a quarter for the next couple of quarters (\"don't take this as a guidance\"), keeping group EBITDA around mid single-digit. Flights outlook cautious.","metrics":[{"m":"GTV","v":"INR5,304.47 crore, +22% YoY"},{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR320.96 crore, +13% YoY"},{"m":"Contribution margin","v":"INR124.86 crore, +14% YoY"},{"m":"Adjusted EBITDA","v":"INR23.58 crore vs INR28.48 crore, -17% YoY"},{"m":"Reported PAT","v":"INR63.42 crore; includes INR40.66 crore Freshbus gain"},{"m":"Trains","v":"Revenue INR149.14 crore, +16%; CM INR47.23 crore, 32% of revenue"},{"m":"Buses","v":"GTV INR752.65 crore, +32%; revenue INR81.04 crore, +24%; CM 53%"},{"m":"Flights","v":"GTV +37%; revenue INR82.66 crore, -8%; CM INR35.68 crore, 43%"},{"m":"Flight gross take rate","v":"7% vs 9.4%"},{"m":"International flight GTV","v":"+59% YoY"},{"m":"Hotels GTV","v":"INR111.34 crore, 2.6 times YoY, +39% QoQ"},{"m":"Hotels revenue and loss","v":"Revenue INR8.1 crore; segment loss INR1.21 crore vs INR0.71 crore"},{"m":"Hotel room nights","v":"Approximately 4.7 lakh, 3.9 times YoY; over 5,100 per day"},{"m":"Hotel gross take rate","v":"More than 12%"},{"m":"Direct hotel tie-ups","v":"Over 12,000 hotels vs 10,000 last quarter"},{"m":"OTA rail market share","v":"Close to 64%"},{"m":"Other income","v":"INR22.08 crore vs INR5.17 crore"},{"m":"Cash net of borrowings","v":"About INR1,831.71 crores"},{"m":"Operating cash flow (YTD)","v":"INR76.44 crore vs INR91.55 crores last year"}],"takeaways":["Hotels disclosed, investment stepped up: Hotels are now a separate segment. Total hotel investment was \"slightly north of INR11 crore\": INR1.21 crore segment loss plus about INR10 crore in people and technology below contribution margin.","Flights constrained by fares: Capacity has not returned and fares are up about 30% YoY on several routes, while the market fell nearly 6% in volume. The CEO said October \"started softer\" and festive demand is \"a shade lower\".","Freshbus stake sale one-off: Reported PAT includes a one-time gain of INR40.66 crore from the August 13 partial sale of the Freshbus stake. The CEO said this reflects focus on \"being an asset light tech and distribution platform\".","Profitability guardrail: The CEO said company-level profitability is a guardrail \"we will never try to breach\". He wants to stay around mid single-digit EBITDA, \"not sort of go too much below\" this quarter's level.","Train recovery not yet confirmed: Festive advance bookings helped train volumes, and the company shifted from conserving margins to growth. The CEO wants momentum \"beyond the festive season before calling it a sustained recovery\"."],"revenue":["Flight take rate compression: Gross take rate fell from 9.4% to 7% on higher base fares and international mix. More discounting funded NBU affordability as bank offers fell. The CEO said \"I don't think we will have to step the pedal beyond this on discounts\".","Hotels from captive users: More than 86% of hotel bookings came from existing platform users. Of 70,000 hotels sold, over 12,000 are directly contracted, up from 10,000. Brevistay consolidation makes part of the growth inorganic.","International flights growing: International flight GTV grew 59% YoY. Attach rates for products like travel guarantee fall when prices are high, which affects CM2 per segment.","Train market share: The OTA rail market share is close to 64%. The CEO expects trains' share of GTV to keep falling (from over 50% to early 40s) as other lines grow, while trains still grow.","Bus growth pace: AbhiBus has grown faster than the OTA bus market for close to three years. The CFO said \"we are very comfortable with this pace of growth at this CM\"."],"profit":["Adjusted EBITDA decline: Adjusted EBITDA fell 17% as the company stepped up hotel investment and flight revenue fell. Treasury income of INR22.08 crore is excluded from adjusted EBITDA.","Hotel unit economics: The CFO said hotel contribution margin \"will vary\" because investment shifts between customer inducement, technology and people. When \"settled\", hotels and buses should be the highest-CM businesses.","Hotel profitability timeline: The CEO said \"for two to three years, we expect to continue investing\" before profitability becomes more important. Spend steps up only if customer payback is seen.","Capital allocation: Cash and equivalents net of borrowings are about INR1,831.71 crores. The CFO said acquisitions need strategic fit, a strong team, synergy and reasonable pricing, and \"we will inform the markets right away\".","AI service productivity: TARA handled 45 lakh queries, resolving 94% of chats and 82% of calls end-to-end. AI-handled queries more than doubled while customer service costs \"did not rise in proportion\"."],"pipeline":["Hotels ambition: The CEO aims to be the largest budget-to-mid-market hotel OTA by room nights in four to five years. Hotels should become a top-two GTV contributor, from 2.1% today, with triple-digit growth targeted.","Agentic AI products: A fully agentic booking flow with payments was demoed, but launch awaits \"regulatory clarity\". Smart Lock, an agentic flight price-lock product, was launched, and the apps are integrated on ChatGPT.","AI traffic still small: The CEO said traffic from AI platforms remains \"low single-digit percentage\". He expects specialist vertical agents to outperform horizontal ones.","Beyond-the-ticket offerings: Food on trains, metro tickets, Bharat Darshan rail packages and a Women Zone in AbhiBus were added. Hotel peace-of-mind products are in development."],"commitments":[{"c":"Hotel investment","s":"INR10 crore to INR12 crore per quarter, \"don't take this as a guidance\"; could move \"one way or the other\"","t":"\"next couple of quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Group EBITDA guardrail","s":"Stay around mid single-digit EBITDA; profitability guardrail \"we will never try to breach\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Hotel take rate","s":"Gross take rate to \"edge up\"; no number given","t":"\"next three to four quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Hotel growth","s":"\"Target will be to continue growing in triple-digits if we can\"","t":"Next four quarters","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Hotel market leadership","s":"Largest budget-to-mid-market hotel OTA by room nights","t":"\"next four to five years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Hotel investment phase","s":"\"For two to three years, we expect to continue investing\"","t":"Two to three years","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Agentic booking launch","s":"Fully agentic booking flow; awaiting regulatory clarity","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Acquisition disclosure","s":"Selective acquisitions; \"we will inform the markets right away\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Flight discounts","s":"\"I don't think we will have to step the pedal beyond this\" at current fares","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Third-party hotel channels","s":"Going outside own funnel \"will not happen probably in the next couple of quarters\"","t":"Next couple of quarters","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited limited airline capacity, oil-price-driven fare increases, slightly lower airline load factors and fewer bank offers.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026). Guidance p5–6, p7–8; K1 p2, p4; K2 p1–2, p4–5; K3 p3–4; K4 p7–8; K5 p1, p3; R1 p4–5; R2 p2, p4; R3 p2, p5; R4 p3, p11–12; R5 p1, p14; F1 p4; F2 p10–11; F3 p6–7; F4 p4; F5 p3; P1 p2, p9, p11; P2 p3; P3 p12–13; P4 p1, p8; C1 p8; C2 p7–8; C3 p6; C4 p9; C5 p2; C6 p7; C7 p3; C8 p4; C9 p5; C10 p7; M1–M3 p3; M4 p3; M5 p3–4; M6–M7 p3; M8–M9 p4; M10 p2; M11 p2, p4; M12 p2, p4; M13 p2; M14 p2; M15 p2; M16 p3; M17–M19 p4; Risks p2, p4–5."},
  {"name":"UltraTech Cement Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"INR1,400 EBITDA per ton exit March '28 reaffirmed (\"we will deliver our commitment\"). FY27 capex about INR10,000 crores maintained, \"plus/minus\". Net debt/EBITDA below 1x by March.","metrics":[{"m":"Net profit growth","v":"+21%"},{"m":"Volume growth","v":"13.8% YoY vs industry closer to 10%"},{"m":"Capacity utilisation","v":"71% vs 66% last year"},{"m":"Domestic cement capacity","v":"204.7 million tons; 4.6 million tons added in September"},{"m":"Fuel cost per bag","v":"INR51.50 vs INR45"},{"m":"Fuel cost per Kcal","v":"About INR2.09, \"might have gone to INR2.2\""},{"m":"Fixed cost per ton","v":"6% lower YoY; absolute fixed costs +4%"},{"m":"Spot realisation","v":"About 5% above Q2 average"},{"m":"Petcoke share in fuel mix","v":"About 25%"},{"m":"Premium and blended cement share","v":"Premium 37%; blended 67.3%"},{"m":"Trade ratio","v":"Between 65% and 67%"},{"m":"UBS share of trade sales","v":"25%; about 6,500 stores"},{"m":"Green power share","v":"47% of power needs; 2 GW captive green power"},{"m":"Thermal substitution rate","v":"About 7.2%"},{"m":"Net debt/EBITDA","v":"1.26x vs 0.94x in March"},{"m":"NCDs raised","v":"About INR5,000 crores at 7.25%"},{"m":"Ultravolt spend to date","v":"INR1,100 crores of INR1,800 crores"},{"m":"Ultravolt retail reach","v":"Over 80,000 points; 1,500+ UBS dealers"},{"m":"FY26 company sales volume","v":"145 million tons"},{"m":"India Cements EBITDA per ton","v":"INR500"}],"takeaways":["Prices turning up: The CFO said trade prices exited September at least about 3% above the Q1 average, and spot realisations are about 5% above the Q2 average. He expects more price improvement in the next couple of months, with full benefit in Q3.","Fuel cost pressure: Fuel cost rose from INR45 to INR51.50 per bag on the West Asia crisis, with petcoke at about $180/ton. The CFO expects around INR2.2 per Kcal for H2 \"if nothing else happens\", with limited Q3 impact.","Next growth phase pending: The CFO said a detailed organic expansion plan \"beyond our 240 million tons\" will come \"before the end of this financial year\", with the North a focus. \"Don't let your imagination fly anywhere else.\"","EBITDA target reaffirmed: On the INR1,400 per ton exit March '28 target, the CFO said cost pressures \"will get covered by price increases. So yes, we will deliver our commitment.\"","Ultravolt scope limited: Asked about fans or appliances, the CFO said \"we're not doing fans and tube lights\". Only PVC conduit is being developed. \"The Board hasn't approved anything beyond INR1,800 crores.\""],"revenue":["Volume ahead of industry: Volume grew 13.8% vs industry demand \"closer to 10%\". The North grew more than 20%, the South 15% to 20%, the West and Central around 10%, and the East around 5% due to heavy rains.","Regional pricing: Mumbai prices rose 3% to 4% per bag in September, and the East is catching up since early October. The CFO said the South \"has been the strongest momentum in prices, volume, and capacity utilization\".","Demand drivers: Public capex, new road awards, about 100 project announcements worth INR13.4 lakh crores, data centers, metro extensions and urban housing support demand. The CFO expects rural demand \"might soften in some areas\" after the weak monsoon.","Trade mix steady: The trade ratio stays between 65% and 67%. The CFO said \"retail demand is not going anywhere\". Premium cement is 37% and blended 67.3%. UBS outlets carry 25% of trade sales.","Industry growth outlook: The CFO estimated industry H1 growth around 8% (Q1 6.5%, Q2 \"could be 9%\"). He said \"Cement industry goes through the roof November onwards\" and expects strong demand through March '29."],"profit":["Operating leverage: Fixed cost per ton was 6% lower YoY, as absolute fixed costs grew only 4% against 13.8% volume growth. The CFO said \"the operating leverage advantage will kick in\" further next quarter.","Fuel sourcing response: The CFO cut petcoke to about 25% of the fuel mix. Domestic coal availability \"is a constraint\" as Coal India prioritises power, and industrial diesel prices are at an all-time high.","Leverage up temporarily: Net debt/EBITDA rose from 0.94x in March to 1.26x on capex and a one-time dividend. The CFO raised INR5,000 crores of NCDs at 7.25%, raising interest cost, and expects below 1x by March.","India Cements turnaround: The CFO said India Cements at INR500 per ton will move \"to a four-digit mark\" after kiln upgrades, WHRS and preheater work. He called the January–March '28 quarter \"a fabulous quarter for India Cements\".","Dividend and guarantee: The Board will review dividend payout yearly. UltraTech gave a corporate guarantee to UHG (41% UltraTech, 50% Hindalco, 9% Grasim) to buy aircraft and ships for group business use."],"pipeline":["Capacity additions: 4.6 million tons were commissioned in September, including a 3.6 MT greenfield at Petnikota, taking domestic capacity to 204.7 million tons. The company is \"on track to reach 240 million tons\".","Ultravolt wires launch: Launched 3 September, with INR1,100 crores spent or committed of INR1,800 crores and the balance over about two quarters. The revenue target set at INR10,000 crores is now \"around INR16,000 crores to INR18,000 crores\" depending on copper.","Ultravolt distribution: Over 1,500 UBS partners signed as dealers, with retail reach over 80,000 points across about 15,000 PIN codes. Power and control cable lines commission through H2. The CFO expects \"matured performance for fiscal '28\".","Green energy build: 116 MW of renewable power and 10 MW of WHRS were added. Non-fossil sources met 47% of power needs, with 2 GW of captive green power and a 7.2% thermal substitution rate.","UBS store expansion: The UBS network is about 6,500 stores. The CFO said \"We will not stop before 10,000 stores\", targeted in about three years ('28–'29)."],"commitments":[{"c":"Price benefit","s":"Full benefit of price increases in Q3; \"more price improvement… in the next couple of months\"","t":"Q3 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Capacity target","s":"240 million tons \"as planned without any surprises\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ultravolt capex completion","s":"Balance of INR1,800 crores spent","t":"\"next few, maybe two quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 capex","s":"INR10,000 crores, \"plus/minus\"; revisit after Q3 results","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EBITDA per ton YoY","s":"Higher YoY over the next two quarters: \"That's the intention\"","t":"Q3–Q4 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Net debt/EBITDA","s":"Below 1x from 1.26x","t":"By end of March","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Next expansion plan","s":"Detailed plans, including North, beyond 240 million tons","t":"\"before the end of this financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Fuel cost","s":"Around INR2.2 per Kcal \"if nothing else happens\"","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ultravolt maturity","s":"\"Matured performance\"; April–June '27 \"a quarter worth talking about\"","t":"Q1 FY28; FY28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EBITDA per ton target","s":"INR1,400 per ton; \"we will deliver our commitment\"","t":"Exit March '28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"India Cements upgrade","s":"Four-digit EBITDA per ton from INR500 after kiln upgrades","t":"January–March '28 quarter","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"UBS store network","s":"10,000 stores from about 6,500","t":"'28–'29","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"West Asia escalation raising petcoke beyond $180 per ton, higher inflation, tighter monetary conditions, less fiscal room, drought pockets, and East monsoon disruptions.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p7–8, p17; K1 p1, p8; K2 p1, p3–4, p9–11; K3 p11, p21; K4 p17; K5 p6, p9; R1 p1–2; R2 p1; R3 p2, p13; R4 p14–15, p18–19; R5 p14; P1 p1, p9; P2 p3, p15; P3 p7–8; P4 p17, p20; P5 p12; I1 p3; I2 p3, p9–10; I3 p3, p10, p18; I4 p3; I5 p19; C1 p1; C2 p3; C3 p3; C4 p7; C5 p8; C6 p8; C7 p11; C8 p9–11; C9 p10, p16; C10 p17; C11 p17; C12 p19; M1 p1; M2 p2; M3 p2; M4 p3; M5 p1; M6 p1, p9; M7 p1; M8 p8; M9 p3; M10 p18; M11 p14; M12 p19; M13 p3; M14 p3; M15 p8; M16 p7; M17 p3, p12; M18 p3; M19 p15; M20 p17; Risks p3, p11, p13. Omitted as garbled: \"13% or 3% quarter-over-quarter\" fuel and raw material rise, \"$10, $20 a bag\", industry FY26 volume \"465 million -- 463 million tons\", and blended \"68%\" (corrected on call to 67.3%). Fuel per bag stated as \"INR51\" in Q&A vs INR51.50 in prepared remarks."},
  {"name":"Kross Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"Management expects Q3 and Q4 volumes above Q2 (\"H2 is always 20% up on H1\") and FY27 exports 45% higher. It declined to time a return to 14–15% EBITDA margin: \"it is difficult currently\".","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR235 crores, +80% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR36 crores (CMD); INR30.6 crores (WTD); margin 13%"},{"m":"PBT","v":"INR26.94 crores, increased more than 100%"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR19.69 crores; CMD: INR196 million, +143% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR419.4 crores vs INR270 crores last year"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA and PAT","v":"EBITDA INR53.32 crores; PAT INR33.16 crores"},{"m":"Trailer segment revenue","v":"Close to INR115 crores vs approximately INR58 crores YoY, INR75 crores in Q1"},{"m":"Segment mix","v":"Trailer close to 49%; components 51%"},{"m":"Trailer axle volume","v":"In excess of 10,500 in Q2; almost 20,000 in H1"},{"m":"Axle volume growth","v":"H1 more than 55%, 60% YoY; Q2 50%, 55%"},{"m":"Extruded beam share","v":"80% of axles produced"},{"m":"Export share of components","v":"Approximately 8%"},{"m":"Largest customer share","v":"Approximately 15%, 16% of top line"},{"m":"Receivable days","v":"Around 81 days in Q2; around 90 days for H1"},{"m":"Term loan and working capital","v":"Term loan INR80 crores; working capital approximately INR32 crores"},{"m":"Seamless capex spent","v":"INR120 crores of approximately INR170 crores budget"},{"m":"Preferential issue","v":"INR63.6 crores"},{"m":"Tipping jack realisation","v":"Approximately INR1.4 lakh per set; capacity close to 800"}],"takeaways":["Margin recovery not timed: An analyst cited guided 14–15% EBITDA margins. The WTD said the return is \"difficult currently\" amid escalations and settlements, though costs will be \"compensated, obviously retrospective\".","Cost recovery lags inflation: The WTD said steel rises \"every quarter\" and has been only partly compensated, with another increase expected in Q3. Conversion cost settlements with OEMs and Tier 1s should benefit \"mostly in quarter three and quarter four\".","H2 demand outlook: The CMD said OEM indications point to Q3 and Q4 volumes above Q2. He called trailer-segment revenue \"sustainable for this financial year… unless something unforeseen\".","Extruded axle switchover: About 80% of trailer axles now use the extruded single-piece beam, with mass rollout since August. Management targets 100% \"in the next one or two quarters\" and called customer feedback \"positive\".","Seamless tube backward integration: Production trials are planned for February or March 2027, with full captive integration by end of H1. Tubes should cost 8% to 10% less than bought-in, adding about 3% to 4% to axle margin."],"revenue":["Trailer growth drivers: The WTD cited demand returning after the monsoon ended in July, a higher share of business with large customers and new customers. Chhattisgarh demand picked up \"after a really long time\".","Segment mix: Trailer was close to 49% of Q2 revenue and components 51%. Tractor is close to 10% or 11%, and the largest customer is approximately 15%, 16% of the top line.","CV and agri demand: The WTD said CV demand has been \"exceptionally good\" since GST rationalisation. Q3 tractor volumes should be similar to Q2; after Diwali, demand depends on manufacturers' inventory.","Exports building: Exports are approximately 8% of component business, from almost none a couple of years ago. A new European customer for propeller shaft parts is at validation stage, not yet in mass production.","Price and volume mix: Management said growth came from axles, a \"drastic increase\" in mechanical and air suspensions, and higher prices from commodity increases."],"profit":["Receivables improving: Receivable days fell to around 81 in Q2 from over 100–110 a year ago. Management expects around 80 days and a working capital cycle \"lesser than 90, 95 days\".","Debt position: The term loan is INR80 crores and working capital/CC is approximately INR32 crores. The only planned debt is the seamless tube term loan of about INR110–120 crores; \"no further debt plans\".","Preferential issue funding: A preferential issue of INR63.6 crores will fund axle shaft expansion, cold-drawn tubes and robotic automation, spent over the next two quarters.","Seamless capex status: The budget is approximately INR170 crores, about INR110 crores from term loan. INR120 crores has been spent as of Q2, with about INR50 crores left for H2."],"pipeline":["Seamless tube progress: Sizing and straightening mills are installed. The furnace and piercing mill are \"almost ready\", with remaining equipment due by end of November or December. Phase 1 is 30% of capacity.","Cold-drawn tube integration: Forward integration into cold-drawn tubes will cost about INR20-odd to INR25 crores. It is needed in-house because no cold-drawing facility exists in Jamshedpur.","Tipping jacks ramp: Tipping jack capacity is close to 800 units, with Q2 volumes similar to Q1. The WTD targets \"at least 50%, 60% of that capacity by quarter four\", at about INR1.4 lakh per set.","H2 capex plan: H2 capex covers shaft lines, robotic automation and the seamless tube. An analyst cited roughly INR110 crores for H2, and the WTD said \"Right\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"Price revision benefit","s":"Concluded revisions with OEMs and Tier 1s \"expected to support us\"","t":"Q3 and Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 volumes","s":"Q3 and Q4 stronger than Q2; \"H2 is always 20% up on H1\"","t":"H2","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Extruded axle switch","s":"Trailer axles fully by extruded beam process; switching to 100%","t":"\"by Q3 or Q4\"; \"next one or two quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Export growth","s":"\"We expect this year to be 45% higher than our previous year\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Seamless tube trials","s":"Production trials; full backward integration by \"end of H1\"","t":"February or March 2027","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Preferential issue capex","s":"Close to INR60 crores on capacity expansion","t":"\"between the next two quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Seamless balance capex","s":"Close to INR50-odd crores of INR170 crores","t":"H2","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Tipping jacks utilisation","s":"At least 50%, 60% of close to 800 capacity","t":"By Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Working capital targets","s":"Receivables around 80 days; working capital cycle below 90, 95 days","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Seamless margin benefit","s":"8% to 10% cheaper tubes; about 3% to 4% axle margin gain","t":"Once own tubes used","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q2 volume data","s":"Exact axle and suspension volumes \"we'll have it sent over to you\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited rising consumable and commodity costs, quarterly steel increases, and post-Diwali seasonal tractor dips depending on OEM inventory.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026). Guidance p1, p14; K1 p14; K2 p1, p14; K3 p1, p8–9; K4 p1, p3–4; K5 p2, p13; R1 p3, p8; R2 p2, p11; R3 p3, p7; R4 p1, p3, p11; R5 p5, p12; F1 p3–4; F2 p10–11; F3 p1–2, p10; F4 p9; P1 p2, p9, p13; P2 p7; P3 p6–7; P4 p10; C1 p1; C2 p1; C3 p1, p4; C4 p1; C5 p2, p9; C6 p10; C7 p10; C8 p6; C9 p4; C10 p13; C11 p12; M1–M2 p1–2; M3–M6 p2; M7 p6; M8 p2; M9 p5; M10 p5; M11 p4; M12 p1; M13 p11; M14 p4; M15 p11; M16 p9; M17 p1; M18 p6–7; Risks p1, p7, p14."},
  {"name":"HDFC Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"17 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"No numeric guidance. The CFO said margin is \"at the bottom\", with \"neutral to positive\" movement ahead. The MD said \"the trajectory will be upward from the next quarter onwards\".","metrics":[{"m":"Deposit growth","v":"18.8% YoY reported, including 4 percentage point FCNR-B lift"},{"m":"CASA growth","v":"About 10.8% YoY; retail CASA about 11.2% vs 9%"},{"m":"Total AUM","v":"+15.3% YoY, including 1.9% FCNR-B leverage impact"},{"m":"Retail disbursements","v":"Crossed 90,000 crores vs 76,000 crores in Q1"},{"m":"Retail ex-agri loan growth","v":"About 11.2% YoY (Sep) vs 9.1% (Jun), 7.4% (Mar)"},{"m":"MSME and mid-market loans","v":"+23.5% YoY EOP; business banking 28% (DMD)"},{"m":"Corporate book","v":"+2.8% QoQ; +16.4% YoY (CFO); \"16.5-17%\" (DMD)"},{"m":"ECLGS disbursements","v":"41,000 crores, highest across all banks"},{"m":"New credit card issuances","v":"1.4 million, up nearly 19% QoQ"},{"m":"Liability relationships added","v":"About 1.7 million, 25% higher than Q1"},{"m":"Q2 deposit vs loan growth","v":"Deposits 1,57,000 crores; loans 1,58,000 crores"},{"m":"Cost of funds","v":"About 4.4%, flat QoQ"},{"m":"GNPA","v":"1.11%, 13 bps lower YoY"},{"m":"Credit costs","v":"44 bps"},{"m":"RoA","v":"1.8%"},{"m":"LCR","v":"113 or so; band 110 to 120"},{"m":"Floating-rate book share","v":"Approximately 70% of total book; MCLR-linked single digit"},{"m":"Risk-weighted assets to total assets","v":"69%"},{"m":"Cost to assets","v":"About 1.74, 1.73"},{"m":"Aggregate PSL achievement","v":"About 50%"}],"takeaways":["Leadership transition: MD & CEO Sashidhar Jagdishan is leaving and Anup Bagchi is the incoming MD & CEO. Deputy MD Kaizad Bharucha has requested early retirement by end of December.","Strategy reset pending: Anup Bagchi said he will \"soon present a perspective on growth, margins, quality, and productivity\". The outgoing MD expects it \"in about a month's time\", including balance sheet and LCR changes.","Margin trough called: Rate-hike repricing of the floating book should be \"largely factored in by December\". This offsets the full-quarter FCNR cost, which the CFO declined to call a headwind. He gave no quarterly number.","Retail deposits masked by outflows: The MD said \"extraordinary one-off outflows\" masked underlying momentum. These included government, inherited HDFC Limited and old leveraged FCNR-B deposits, forcing reliance on non-retail deposits with lower LCR cover.","Insurance commission risk: On draft insurance commission norms, the CFO said \"in its current form… it is pretty, pretty strong, pretty high impact\". Offsets may come from persistency, volumes and cost structures."],"revenue":["Retail and MSME lead assets: The MD said disbursements are balanced across personal loans, wheels, gold, agri and MSME. ECLGS 5.0 created \"significant opportunities\", and business banking grew 28% (DMD).","Corporate growth calibrated: The DMD said the bank will not walk away from relationships \"for, you know, 10 basis points more\". It will take a \"calibrated approach\" to higher-yield consumer products.","Productivity gains: Turnaround times fell 10% to 15%, and customer visits per resource per day rose about 50%. Savings accounts per branch per day rose 19%, and core incremental deposits per branch rose 2.3 times from Q1.","FCNR deployment: The CFO said FCNR funds \"you can take it as deployed\", as deployment is needed for the PSL benefit. Sources and uses were \"largely matched within the quarter\".","Branch maturity opportunity: The CFO said in more than 54% of 765 districts the bank is 2 to 3 percentage points below its average market share. Branch-driven deposits are about 80% of total."],"profit":["Cost of funds held flat: The CFO said FCNR term deposit mix offset back-book repricing. Of 1.57 trillion deposit growth, almost 1.3 trillion was time deposits. AT1 bonds called in August were offset by leverage borrowing.","Retail back-book repricing: The DMD said 20 to 30% of the retail book runs off yearly. \"Across the next 2 quarters you will see that change coming through.\"","LCR as growth floor: LCR is about 113. The CFO said the band is 110 to 120, \"probably in the middle, 115\". The MD said \"a low LCR does not mean a low liquidity.\"","Asset quality stable: The DMD said agri GNPA held \"largely constant\" from June to September despite a monsoon shortfall. The CFO said slippages improved across most portfolios, with a credit environment \"benign for a long time\".","PSL shortfall: Aggregate PSL achievement is about 50%. The bank is \"short by a few percentage points\" on small and marginal farmers, where loans are \"very scarce\" and costly to buy."],"pipeline":["AI and digital safety: Launched Edge Security, Kavach and Secure Lock, AI conversational banking and an AI collections bot. Real-time fraud prevention was also strengthened.","Headcount outlook: The MD expects staff count to stay flat while shifting people to frontline roles. It will \"probably\" start to increase \"over maybe 3, 4 years' time\".","Technology investment phase ending: The MD said technology investments are \"coming out of the investment phase\" and will be \"extremely intense and large going forward\".","Non-deposit funding strategies: The MD said alternate balance sheet strategies, including \"some amount of non-deposit strategies\", should raise the retail share. He called these \"blue-sky thoughts\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"New CEO strategy","s":"Perspective on growth, margins, quality, productivity","t":"\"soon\"; \"in about a month's time\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Margin trajectory","s":"\"Neutral to positive\"; \"at the bottom\"; MD: \"upward from the next quarter onwards\"","t":"\"next quarter onwards\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Rate hike pass-through","s":"25 bps October change on ~70% floating book \"largely factored in by December\"","t":"By December","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Retail back-book effect","s":"Margin change from repricing as retail and MSME volumes grow","t":"\"across the next 2 quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"LCR and liability plan","s":"Strategic plan on core liability momentum and LCR glide path","t":"\"over a period of next couple of quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"LCR operating band","s":"110 to 120; \"probably in the middle, 115\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Business banking growth","s":"\"You will see business banking continuing to grow\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Insurance norm response","s":"Assess impact once commission rate settles; comments due","t":"\"sometime in November\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Headcount","s":"Flat, then \"probably it will start to increase\"","t":"\"maybe 3, 4 years' time\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"DMD retirement","s":"Kaizad Bharucha early retirement requested","t":"\"by end of December\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The MD cited a \"structural shift\" in household savings shrinking system CASA accretion. The DMD flagged a non-uniform monsoon shortfall affecting agri.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 17 Oct 2026). Guidance p8; K1 p2–3, p16; K2 p3, p5, p12; K3 p8–9; K4 p2, p4, p14; K5 p15; R1 p1, p6; R2 p9; R3 p2; R4 p7; R5 p11, p15; F1 p6; F2 p7; F3 p14–15; F4 p5, p11; F5 p15–16; P1 p2; P2 p13; P3 p13; P4 p5, p12; C1 p3, p5, p12; C2 p8; C3 p8–9; C4 p7; C5 p14; C6 p15; C7 p7; C8 p15; C9 p13; C10 p16; M1–M3 p1; M4–M6 p1; M6 also p6; M7 p6–7; M8 p1; M9–M10 p1; M11 p7; M12 p6; M13–M15 p2; M16 p14–15; M17 p8, p10; M18 p2; M19 p10; M20 p15; Risks p5, p12."},
  {"name":"Ujjivan Small Finance Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"Upgrade hinted, not formalised: \"we could overshoot our guided ROA range\"; IR sees \"high probability of a significant beat on the upper end\". Loan guidance on track; H2 NIM around 8.35%.","metrics":[{"m":"PAT","v":"INR365 crores, all-time high quarterly"},{"m":"NII","v":"INR1,244 crores, +35% YoY"},{"m":"NIM","v":"8.4% (interim CEO) / 8.45% (CFO)"},{"m":"ROA and ROE","v":"ROA 2.4%; ROE 19.7%"},{"m":"Total income","v":"INR2,450 crores"},{"m":"Gross loan book","v":"INR45,699 crores, +32.1% YoY; 12.4% YTD"},{"m":"Total deposits","v":"INR50,643 crores, +29.2% YoY; CASA 26%"},{"m":"Secured share of loans","v":"52.1%"},{"m":"Micro banking book","v":"INR22,037 crores"},{"m":"Affordable housing","v":"INR10,056 crores; GNPA 1.2%"},{"m":"Micro mortgages","v":"INR2,031 crores, +85.7% YoY; GNPA 0.7%"},{"m":"MSME book","v":"INR3,871 crore, +51.3% YoY"},{"m":"Gold loan book","v":"INR1,275 crore, +209.8% YoY"},{"m":"GNPA and NNPA","v":"2.1% and 0.3%"},{"m":"Credit cost and slippage","v":"Credit cost 0.7% of ATA; slippage 1.6%"},{"m":"PCR","v":"85%"},{"m":"Bucket X collection efficiency","v":"99.7%"},{"m":"CRAR","v":"20.69%, up 33 bps"},{"m":"Cost of funds","v":"Around 6.9%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 network additions","v":"104 branches; 1,335 employees"}],"takeaways":["ROA overshoot hinted: The interim CEO said \"we could overshoot our guided ROA range\" on growth, slower opex and asset quality. IR cited opex-to-ATA at 6.2% vs the 6.4% indicated, and a \"high probability of a significant beat\".","Preferential capital raise: The bank proposes INR409 crores from IFC, Bajaj Life, HDFC Life and Aditya Birla Sun Life, subject to shareholder approval. It adds about 1.2% to CRAR pro forma and INR0.8 per share to BVPS.","MD succession underway: After MD Sanjeev Nautiyal's early retirement for health reasons, the NRC is searching for a successor. The interim CEO hopes \"to complete the process in around three to four months starting September 1st\".","Rate hike margin impact: The CFO said the 25 bps repo hike affects margin by about 10 bps, taking NIM to around 8.35% in H2. Planned direct assignment will offset mix-change impact.","Insurance rules pending: The interim CEO said IRDAI's consultation submissions are due October 25th, and \"it would be premature to elaborate\". About 50% of insurance income comes from credit life. Stress would come only if rules apply in Q4."],"revenue":["Secured mix rising: Secured lending is 52.1% of loans. The CFO targets 56% by year end and about 5% improvement each year after. Affordable housing and micro mortgages combined crossed INR12,000 crores, +38.1% YoY.","Micro banking on track: Micro banking grew \"approximately 6%\" in H1 (interim CEO) or \"about 6.5%\" (micro banking head). 12% to 14% for FY27 is \"intact\", with 2 lakh new customers in Q2 and bucket X collection efficiency of 99.7%.","Drought exposure limited: Of INR2,400 crores of Karnataka microfinance, INR15.1 crore is SLBC-eligible. Of INR1,682 crore in Maharashtra, INR18 crores qualifies. The micro banking head said no state exceeds 15% of the portfolio.","Secured products growth: MSME reached INR3,871 crore (+51.3%), gold loan INR1,275 crore (+209.8%) with LTV below 78%, agri INR914 crores (+79.2%), and two-wheeler INR1,081 crores (+64.8%).","Deposits and CASA: Deposits crossed INR50,000 crore with CASA at 26%. The liabilities head said reaching 30% CASA by year end \"will be difficult… slightly lower, let's say 2% lower than that\". Third-party products grew 41% QoQ."],"profit":["Cost of funds rising: Downward deposit repricing held cost of funds at around 6.9%. The interim CEO expects it \"to start moving upwards\" as Q1 rate increases flow through. The CFO estimated about 10 bps.","Opex for brand and branches: Opex rose 6% QoQ on the brand campaign with R. Madhavan and branch expansion. Part of the campaign cost spills into Q3, and the CFO now expects opex-to-ATA of 6.2%.","Asset quality steady: The chief credit officer said micro banking PAR moves only in \"decimal points\" and \"credit cost in H2 is similar to the credit cost in H1\". Micro mortgage PAR of 1.8% should stabilise around 2.5%, \"just below 3%\".","Funding actions: The securitisation pool was increased by INR500 crores in October, \"NIM accretive and P&L neutral\". CRAR rose 33 bps to 20.69%, \"largely one-time\". The CFO agreed the raise saves \"more or less\" INR30 crore of annual interest."],"pipeline":["Distribution expansion: H1 added 104 branches and 1,335 employees. The brand campaign \"nothing small about us\" runs across TV, print and digital, targeting around 35 crore viewers. Digital and technology spend \"will reflect in H2\".","Housing growth plan: The housing head said \"around 35% growth\" is planned. The affordable housing ticket stays around INR17 lakhs to protect yield, while micro mortgages shift toward INR5–15 lakhs, stabilising \"around INR8 lakhs\".","Balance capital raise: The CFO said the shareholder resolution allows up to INR2,000 crores, and the bank plans to raise the balance within the resolution's validity.","New deposit services: The ASBA facility launched in Q2. The liabilities head said UPI MDR, if implemented, would add income without new infrastructure; it has been \"deferred post Diwali\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"MD appointment","s":"NRC search; submission to regulator; complete \"in around three to four months starting September 1st\"","t":"By around Dec 2026–Jan 2027","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Preferential equity raise","s":"INR409 crores from IFC and insurers; subject to shareholder approval","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Micro banking growth","s":"12% to 14%; \"remains intact\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ROA outcome","s":"\"Could overshoot our guided ROA range\"; no revised number","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"NIM","s":"Around 8.35% (10 bps impact from repo hike)","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Credit cost","s":"H2 similar to H1; within guided range","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Opex to ATA","s":"6.2% vs 6.4% indicated earlier","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Secured loan share","s":"56% by year end; about 5% improvement annually after","t":"FY27; per year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"CASA ratio","s":"Below 30%; \"let's say 2% lower than that\"","t":"End of FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Balance capital raise","s":"Up to INR2,000 crores total; raise balance within resolution validity","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Micro mortgage ticket size","s":"Stabilise around INR8 lakhs","t":"\"remaining quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Borrowings outlook","s":"Year-end borrowing level to be shared offline","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Middle East conflict, energy and freight price volatility, inflation, a deficient southwest monsoon, El Nino, policy rate tightening and supply chain disruptions.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p3–4, p11; K1 p3, p10–11; K2 p1; K3 p1, p14; K4 p4; K5 p3, p8, p11; R1 p2, p11–12; R2 p2, p5, p7; R3 p4–5; R4 p2; R5 p2, p10–11; P1 p2, p9; P2 p3, p10; P3 p4, p13; P4 p1, p3, p13; I1 p1–2; I2 p6; I3 p8–9, p12; I4 p2, p14–15; C1 p1, p14; C2 p1; C3 p2, p5; C4 p3, p11; C5 p4; C6 p9, p13; C7 p10; C8 p11–12; C9 p10; C10 p8–9, p12; C11 p6; C12 p17; M1 p3; M2 p3; M3 p3–4; M4 p3; M5 p3; M6 p2; M7 p2; M8 p2; M9 p2; M10 p2; M11 p2; M12 p2; M13 p2; M14–M16 p2–3; M17 p2; M18 p3; M19 p2; M20 p1; Risks p1, p3. Omitted as contradictory: resolution validity \"July 2026\" vs \"July 2027\", and \"117 branches\" added in H1 (vs 104 branches). Analyst-cited credit cost guide of 90–100 bps not confirmed. COMMITMENT_1 timeline is derived from \"three to four months starting September 1st\"."},
  {"name":"Angel One Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"Standalone (broking + distribution) operating margin about 45% annually including IPL, maintained; consolidated 40% to 45% near term; FY27 employee cost about INR1,100 crores, flat.","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated gross revenue","v":"INR14.3 billion, +18.9% YoY"},{"m":"Consolidated operating profit","v":"INR4.9 billion, +52% YoY"},{"m":"Consolidated PAT","v":"INR3.35 billion, +58% YoY"},{"m":"Consolidated operating margin","v":"45.1% vs 34.5% in Q2 FY26"},{"m":"Normalised consolidated operating margin","v":"43.8% vs 43.6% in Q1"},{"m":"Standalone operating margin","v":"48.9% vs guided range of 40% to 45%"},{"m":"Interest income","v":"INR5.1 billion, +34% YoY, +9% QoQ"},{"m":"Gross broking share of revenue","v":"About 55% vs nearly 60% a year ago"},{"m":"Average client funding book","v":"INR74.5 billion, up about 40.5% YoY"},{"m":"Closing client funding book","v":"INR82.1 billion"},{"m":"Funded clients","v":"437,000, up nearly 30% YoY"},{"m":"New clients","v":"Nearly 1.5 million, 11.6% more than Q1"},{"m":"September average daily orders","v":"5.8 million, up nearly 12% from August"},{"m":"F&O retail turnover share","v":"22.1% for Q2; 22.5% in September"},{"m":"Equity retail share (option premium)","v":"19.7%"},{"m":"Commodities market share","v":"44%"},{"m":"Credit distribution run rate","v":"INR33 billion annualised (September)"},{"m":"Ionic Wealth AUM","v":"INR160 billion, +19.1% QoQ"},{"m":"Net worth","v":"INR67.1 billion vs INR58.3 billion a year ago"},{"m":"Second interim dividend","v":"INR1.4 per share"}],"takeaways":["CAS circular seen as tailwind: CEO said SEBI's CAS circular \"provides clarity, and it will create more tailwinds\"; he cited industry options ADTO in October month-to-date up about 39% over September.","Higher margin not guided: Standalone operating margin 48.9% against the 40%–45% range; CEO: \"It will at times go towards 50%, but I don't want to sort of provide that specific guidance\".","Asset management rethink: AUM nearly INR6 billion; evaluating \"a comprehensive play\" in the business, including bringing a new leader; \"We will share more in future quarters.\"","Data withheld: Management declined to share product per customer, the IPO vs other split of distribution income, and per-segment realisation; distribution details to be disclosed \"once we get to a certain scale\".","Pricing held: On competition and pricing, CEO said \"We are not looking to either increase or reduce it\"; the Jio app offer is standard free orders, \"no special pricing for Jio\"."],"revenue":["Mix shifting from broking: Gross broking about 55% of revenue versus nearly 60% a year ago; interest income INR5.1 billion, up 34%, on funding book growth; distribution helped by credit and IPOs.","Commodities supported realisation: Fees fell less than volumes; CBO said commodities \"played one of the critical roles\" in realisations; commodity share came down to 44% as the market expanded.","Segment market share: F&O retail turnover share 22.1% for the quarter and 22.5% in September; cash share moderated, which CEO attributed to customer segment mix as MTF rose.","Activation over KYC counts: Nearly 1.5 million new clients, 11.6% more than Q1; CEO said focus is activations, not \"CDSL share or just the number of KYCs\"; second-largest active additions in September.","Channel mix: Net broking income about 75-25 in favour of direct over AP; regular plans about one-third of MF distribution; AP distribution products in \"triple-digit growth\" from a low base."],"profit":["Normalised margin stable: Adjusted for seasonal IPL spend and ESOP reversals, consolidated operating margin 43.8% versus 43.6% in Q1; new-business burn \"about 3.5% to 4%\", up from 2.5%–3% earlier.","Lower employee cost: Return-to-office caused expected attrition; headcount close to 3,050 at 30 September; backfills slowed; CFO does not expect the count to \"go up dramatically\".","MTF funding cost: Lending rate unchanged at 14.99%; borrowing cost \"marginally gone up\" after RBI benchmark increase; CFO: \"The NIMs haven't really gotten impacted.\"","Funding book quality: Closing book record INR82.1 billion; borrowing about INR75.5 billion; 84% of funded clients below INR100,000; 82% of book under 30 days old; blue chip \"almost upwards of 85%\".","Dividend continuity: Second interim dividend INR1.4 per share, record date 22 Oct 2026; CFO said quarterly dividends can continue alongside investment in newer businesses."],"pipeline":["Credit distribution scaling: Highest-ever credit distribution in September, annualised run rate INR33 billion; upgraded probability of default model; early beta of loan against securities launched.","US stocks pilot: Authorised as Global Access Provider in IFSC; US stocks pilot live for a certain number of customers through GIFT City with a US brokerage partner.","Wealth business: Ionic Wealth AUM INR160 billion, up 19.1% over Q1; margins expected in line with industry, \"closer to the 0.5% kind of a mark\".","Insurance opportunity: CEO said IRDAI's proposed insurance commission reset \"favors digital platforms like ours\"; noted \"It is still a proposal\".","Brand spend ratio: CEO said brand and IPL spend as a percentage of revenue will \"definitely\" come down, as he does not see spends \"going up too much from here\".","AI and security: AI being built as a group-wide intelligence layer; added AI-driven vulnerability detection and automated remediation workflows to cybersecurity this quarter."],"commitments":[{"c":"Standalone operating margin","s":"About 45% including IPL; \"we will endeavor to achieve 45%\"; quarterly \"gyrations\" possible","t":"\"on an annual basis\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Consolidated operating margin","s":"\"Anywhere between 40% to 45%\"; too early for a number beyond that","t":"\"in the near term\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Employee cost","s":"About INR1,100 crores including stock options, flat year-on-year","t":"\"this financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Non-broking revenue share","s":"\"Low double digits\"; currently about 5% from distribution","t":"\"next two to three years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Asset management plan","s":"Comprehensive play and new leader; \"We will share more\"","t":"\"in future quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"US stocks rollout","s":"Pilot live; standard rollout as issues are fixed","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Credit distribution","s":"\"Hopefully we'll go on to create newer highs\"","t":"\"over the next few quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Distribution income disclosure","s":"Details to be disclosed \"once we get to a certain scale\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Brand spend ratio","s":"Percentage of revenue \"will come down\"","t":"\"over a period of time\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MTF lending rate","s":"\"Not looking at any increase\"; remains 14.99%","t":"\"for the time being\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Product-per-customer data","s":"\"We'll think about looking at sharing it in the future\"","t":"\"in the future\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Headcount","s":"\"Do not think that this count will go up dramatically\"","t":"\"over the next few quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"CEO cited muted stock market performance and F&O regulatory structural changes reducing investor interest; CBO cited regulator and lender risk appetite affecting credit distribution.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026). Guidance p3, p5, p18; K1 p1, p6; K2 p3, p10; K3 p2; K4 p16, p19; K5 p15; R1 p3; R2 p1, p19; R3 p1, p8; R4 p1, p12; R5 p10–11, p19; F1 p3, p10; F2 p5, p18; F3 p7; F4 p3, p7; F5 p3, p14; P1 p2; P2 p2, p14; P3 p2, p9; P4 p2; P5 p16–17; P6 p2; C1 p5, p13; C2 p18; C3 p5, p18; C4 p8; C5 p2; C6 p14; C7 p9; C8 p19; C9 p16–17; C10 p7; C11 p16; C12 p18; M1–M8 p3; M9 p1; M10–M11 p3; M12–M16 p1; M17–M18 p2; M19–M20 p3; Risks p8, p12."},
  {"name":"Sterling and Wilson Renewable Energy Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Cautious","guidance":"FY27 revenue growth \"up to 10%\", described as maintained. Under questioning, the CEO said \"our range was 10% to 15%… it will be around 10%\", excluding Reliance.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR1,527 crore vs INR1,749 crores Q2 FY26, INR1,590 crore Q1"},{"m":"Revenue split","v":"Domestic EPC INR1,366 crore; international EPC INR62 crore; O&M INR99 crore"},{"m":"Gross profit","v":"INR140 crore; margin 9.2% vs 8.9%"},{"m":"Operational EBITDA","v":"INR56 crore, 3.7% vs INR62 crore, 3.5%"},{"m":"Reported EBITDA","v":"INR66 crore vs INR96 crore in Q1"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR17 crore vs loss INR478 crore Q2 FY26"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR3,118 crore vs INR3,510 crore, about 11% lower"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR70 crore vs loss INR439 crore"},{"m":"H1 FY27 operational EBITDA","v":"INR134 crore vs INR184 crore; gross margin about 9.5%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 international revenue","v":"INR165 crores vs INR929 crores"},{"m":"O&M revenue","v":"INR99 crores, about +55% YoY; gross margin about 23%"},{"m":"Order book (30 Sep)","v":"INR12,481 crores, about +34% YoY"},{"m":"Order book split","v":"India INR6,943 crores (56%); international INR5,538 crores (44%)"},{"m":"H1 FY27 order wins","v":"INR3,626 crores; nearly 90% international"},{"m":"O&M portfolio","v":"18.9 GW vs 13.5 GW in March"},{"m":"Recurring overheads","v":"INR84 crore vs INR94 crore, about 11% lower"},{"m":"Net working capital","v":"Negative INR301 crore vs negative INR260 crore Q1"},{"m":"Receivable and payable days","v":"Receivables 176 vs 138; payables 164 vs 135"},{"m":"Gross borrowings and net debt","v":"INR920 crore; net debt INR601 crore vs INR589 crore"},{"m":"Shareholders' equity","v":"INR719 crore vs INR651 crore"}],"takeaways":["Guidance effectively narrowed: An analyst said the earlier 10%–15% guidance was now \"up to 10%\". The CEO said four projects that were about 40% of the April order book have not started, so \"around 10%\" is achievable. H1 revenue fell about 11%.","Delayed project starts: The CEO said NTPs for Coal India and two NTPC projects were held back by unsigned PPAs. Four international NTPs were delayed by customer financing. Substantial PSU project revenue \"will come in Q4\".","Avoiding low-margin domestic bids: The CEO said some new players bid at prices \"tough to maintain margins\", so domestic wins have been lower. Nearly 90% of H1 order wins were international \"at healthy margins\".","Reliance order timing: The CEO said Reliance work is at \"the flag end of the planning process\". Commencement is expected \"this quarter for sure\", but \"it's premature to tell you that how much order will come\".","H2 back-loaded: The CEO said Q3 will not be at 2,600 per quarter. \"Q3 will be better than Q2 and Q4 will be heavily loaded.\""],"revenue":["International gap drives decline: H1 international revenue was INR165 crores vs INR929 crores, as four projects in South Africa, Spain and Italy finished and new ones had not started. Domestic EPC grew about 13% to INR2,768 crores.","Industry headwinds: The CEO said domestic cell rules (ALMM II) raised project costs by about 15% to 17%. About 40 to 50 GW of awarded capacity awaits PPAs, and grid curtailment pushed Rajasthan and Gujarat pipelines out by several quarters.","O&M scaling: The O&M portfolio reached 18.9 GW from 13.5 GW in March. The CFO said O&M is \"around 5%\" of revenue and may rise \"up to 10%\". The CEO expects 22 to 25 GW by year end.","O&M FY27 revenue: The CEO said O&M revenue \"should be more than INR400 crore\" this year. Separately, he said \"right now, we are at around INR200 crores. We'll move up to INR400 crores… in the beginning of next year.\"","Egypt project ramp: The CEO said the roughly 14-month West Minya project, with NTP received, will load Q3 and Q4, with completion in Q1 and Q2 of next fiscal. The turnkey margin is \"more than 8%\"."],"profit":["Overheads flat, ratio to fall: Recurring overheads stayed at INR79 crore to INR84 crore a quarter. The CEO said they are around 5.5% of revenue now and expected \"around 4.5% for the current year\" as revenue rises.","EPC margin by mix: The CFO said turnkey projects have lower percentage but higher absolute margins, while balance-of-system projects carry better gross margin. Asked about 4%–4.5% EBITDA for the year, the CEO said \"that's the minimum requirement\".","Finance cost increase: The CFO said finance cost rose from new credit lines and longer-period LCs, despite gross borrowings falling. Net debt was steady as cash came down.","Banking lines: The CFO cited \"over INR3,000 crore\" of new credit lines this year in prepared remarks and \"around INR2,800 crore\" in Q&A. Credit line utilisation is around 70%. A rating review is planned after \"Reliance orders or any other large positive news\".","Legacy claims: A settlement was signed with a Chile customer, with the balance to be claimed from promoters. The CFO expects around INR156 crore under indemnity from promoters this year."],"pipeline":["Q2 order wins: Q2 orders exceeded INR985 crores: a 534 MW balance-of-systems project in Rajasthan from a new Indian IPP, and two 308 MW battery storage projects in South Africa from a repeat Middle East developer.","Bid pipeline: The solar bid pipeline, excluding storage, grew to 34 GW from 27.7 GW, with 74% in India. The CEO sees INR45,000 to INR50,000 crores of solar and storage bidding opportunities in H2.","Adani framework: The RFQ for the next 1.2 GW under the five-year Adani framework arrived. The CEO expects closure \"maybe this month or maybe early next month\".","Floating solar and BESS: The Tilaiya floating solar project for NTPC-DVC is nearing completion, pending a transmission line. The company is \"selective in bidding new BESS projects\" as battery prices rise and warranty demands grow.","Nigeria project stalled: The CEO said the Nigeria order is \"still cold\" with elections and US tariff issues. \"We are pursuing this. But I can't commit you the timeline.\"","International O&M expansion: The company won third-party O&M in Saudi Arabia last year and is \"about to close few projects in the other geographies\". Africa, Saudi Arabia and Europe are focus regions."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 revenue growth","s":"\"Up to 10%\"; \"around 10%\", from earlier 10%–15% range; excludes Reliance","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Quarterly revenue shape","s":"Q3 better than H1/Q2; Q4 \"heavily loaded\"","t":"Q3, Q4 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EPC gross margin","s":"8% to 10% depending on mix","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"O&M gross margin","s":"Around 20%","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Order book floor","s":"Above INR10,000 crores","t":"End of FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Overheads ratio","s":"Around 4.5% of revenue","t":"Current year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Promoter indemnity","s":"Around INR156 crore expected from promoters","t":"Current year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Reliance project start","s":"\"Commencement of course is expected\"; order size not committed","t":"\"this quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Adani next tranche","s":"Next 1.2 GW under framework to close","t":"\"this month or maybe early next month\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"O&M portfolio","s":"Around 22 to 25 GW","t":"By year end","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"O&M revenue","s":"\"More than INR400 crore\" this year vs \"INR400 crores… beginning of next year\"","t":"FY27 / next year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Credit rating review","s":"Approach agencies after Reliance orders or \"other large positive news\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Rising battery prices and long warranty demands, US tariff uncertainty, land border closure affecting imports, and new transmission land-compensation rules raising costs.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p3, p11–12; K1 p2, p11–12; K2 p4–5; K3 p1, p2, p8; K4 p6, p9, p12–13; K5 p7; R1 p2; R2 p1; R3 p3, p15–16; R4 p8, p15; R5 p2, p8–9; P1 p4–5; P2 p3, p8; P3 p12; P4 p4, p9–10; P5 p4; I1 p2; I2 p2–3; I3 p9; I4 p2, p7; I5 p11; I6 p15–16; C1 p3, p12; C2 p3, p7; C3 p3; C4 p3; C5 p3; C6 p5; C7 p4; C8 p6, p12–13; C9 p9; C10 p15; C11 p8, p15; C12 p9–10; M1–M2 p3; M3–M6 p3; M7–M9 p3–4; M10 p2; M11 p3; M12–M14 p2; M15 p3; M16 p3; M17–M20 p4; Risks p2, p11. Omitted for inconsistency: H1 O&M revenue stated as INR150 crores and INR183 crores, O&M portfolio \"18.5 gigawatt\" in Q&A (vs 18.9 GW), the March order book dated \"March 2027\", and the domestic-vs-international O&M margin \"30-35, 30% to 40% higher\"."},
  {"name":"Poonawalla Fincorp Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"09 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"No new numeric guidance. Opex-to-AUM movement was \"within the 10 to 25 basis point range we had guided\". The MD sees ROA peaking in \"2.5, 3 years\" and expects credit costs to keep improving over one to two years.","metrics":[{"m":"AUM","v":"₹74,008 crore, +10.4% QoQ"},{"m":"NII including fees","v":"₹1,589 crore, +12.3% QoQ, +75.6% YoY"},{"m":"NIM plus fees","v":"9.26%, +16 bps QoQ, +86 bps YoY"},{"m":"Pre-provisioning operating profit","v":"₹877 crore, +11.8% QoQ"},{"m":"Profit after tax","v":"₹375 crore, +21.8% QoQ"},{"m":"ROA","v":"2.18%, +20 bps QoQ, about +149 bps YoY"},{"m":"Credit cost","v":"2.19% vs 2.40% (MD) / 2.41% (ED) in Q1; 2.67% YoY"},{"m":"GNPA","v":"1.2%, -17 bps QoQ, -39 bps YoY"},{"m":"NNPA","v":"0.61%, -9 bps QoQ, -20 bps YoY"},{"m":"Provision coverage ratio","v":"49.14%"},{"m":"Portfolio yield","v":"14.08%, about +45 bps over Q1"},{"m":"Cost of borrowing","v":"7.76% vs 7.72%"},{"m":"Opex-to-AUM","v":"4.15%"},{"m":"6 MOB 30+ (latest cohort)","v":"Approximately 0.35% vs 0.67% last quarter"},{"m":"New products share of disbursements","v":"28% vs 26% previous quarter"},{"m":"Gold loan disbursements","v":"₹1,066 crore vs ₹875 crore; 550 branches"},{"m":"Capital adequacy","v":"18.68%; Tier 1 17.15%"},{"m":"Debt-equity","v":"4.3x"},{"m":"Liquidity coverage ratio","v":"194%; surplus liquidity ₹6,526 crore"},{"m":"Long-term borrowing share","v":"88.92% vs 88.52%"}],"takeaways":["Credit cost falling: Credit cost fell to 2.19%; the MD called it \"the first level of milestones… more structural and stable\". The CCO said 12 MoB 90-plus for post-September 2024 origination improved over 53%.","Rate hike passed on: The 25 bps hike was passed on \"effective a week back\". The CFO said the diversified borrowing mix keeps cost-of-borrowing increases minimal; NCDs (about 30% of borrowing) are fixed-rate.","ROA path, three levers: The MD expects lending spread, credit cost and opex-to-AUM to all lift ROA over two years. New businesses are not at full ROA, and the peak is \"probably 2.5, 3 years from now\".","Investment continues: The MD said opex reflects front-loaded investment in collection capacity, AI and gold branches, and \"we won't shy away from sensible investments\". He expects some quarterly fluctuation.","Insurance commission risk absorbable: On proposed insurance commission rules, the MD acknowledged \"the possibility of that fee compression\". He expects lending spreads and operating leverage to absorb it."],"revenue":["New product contribution: New products contributed 28% of disbursements, versus 26% and 24% in the two prior quarters. Prime Personal Loans were 42% of PL disbursements, up from 38%.","Yield expansion: Disbursement yield rose about 20 bps over Q1, after about 50 bps last quarter, on product mix. Portfolio yield rose about 45 bps to 14.08%.","Product disbursement scaling: Gold loan quarterly disbursement was ₹1,066 crore versus ₹875 crore. Consumer durable was ₹606 crore versus ₹433 crore. Monthly averages were ₹127 crore for CV and ₹218 crore for education loans.","Cross-sell starting: The MD said the cross-sell engine \"is now beginning to fire\". Products-per-customer data will be \"more relevant to figure a year down the line\".","Seasoned products: The CCO said LAP, business loans and pre-owned cars have more than 36 months in the book. Consumer durable, launched 18 months back, has seen almost 10 cycles."],"profit":["Stage-wise improvement: Stage 1 rose to 97.86% from 97.6%, Stage 2 fell to 0.94% from 1.03%, and Stage 3 fell to 1.20% from 1.37%. Stage 1 and Stage 3 slippage ratios each improved 16% QoQ.","Early vintage quality: 6 MOB 30+ for the latest cohort was approximately 0.35% versus 0.67% last quarter, excluding gold loans. The MD said this \"keeps a firm downward bias on our credit cost\".","Funding profile: Long-term borrowings are 88.92%, versus 88.52% in Q1. Cost of borrowing was 7.76% versus 7.72%. Surplus liquidity was ₹6,526 crore, with positive cumulative mismatch in all buckets.","Capital and leverage: CAR was 18.68% (Tier 1 17.15%), debt-equity 4.3x and LCR 194%. The ED said capital \"gives us enough headroom for our growth\".","Collections investment: The CCO described AI-led predictive collections paired with specialist teams by product, stage bucket and delinquency cycle, \"driving sharper resolution efficiency, lower roll forward rates\"."],"pipeline":["Gold loan branch rollout: 90 gold branches were added in the quarter, taking the network to 550 from 460. The MD said \"approximately 200 new branches in this financial year\", and elsewhere \"300-400 branches every year\"; break-even is about 13 months.","Distribution expansion: CV has 1,250 channel partners (from 1,100) in 90+ locations across 13 states. Education loans have 700 consultants. Consumer durable has 20,000 dealers across 360 locations.","AI programme scale: AI projects total 143 (84 live, 59 underway), with 34 delivered this quarter. AI-led chat, voice and video bots are driving disbursements. AI campaigns give 1.5 to 2 times higher click-through at over 60% lower cost.","Consumer franchise growth: The MD aims to \"probably double our customer franchise this year\" through consumer durable, feeding cross-sell."],"commitments":[{"c":"Gold loan branches","s":"\"Approximately 200 new branches in this financial year\"; also \"300-400 branches every year\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Opex-to-AUM","s":"Quarterly movement within guided 10 to 25 bps range","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Credit cost trajectory","s":"\"Scope for further moving strength-to-strength\"; \"will gradually stabilize\"","t":"\"next one year to two years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ROA peak","s":"Peak ROA from new businesses reaching full ROA","t":"\"2.5, 3 years from now\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ROA commitment","s":"ROA commitment referenced; no number restated","t":"June 2028","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Customer franchise","s":"\"Probably double our customer franchise this year\" via consumer durable","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Products per customer","s":"Metric to become \"more relevant… a year down the line\"","t":"One year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"AI progress update","s":"\"I look forward to reporting further progress next quarter\"","t":"Next quarter","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Opex as ROA driver","s":"Opex-to-AUM to become \"one of the important contributors to ROA\"","t":"\"two-year window\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited global fuel price uncertainty affecting CV, a rising interest rate environment, and possible fee compression from insurance commission regulation.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 09 Oct 2026; physical page numbers). Guidance p4, p15–16; K1 p4, p7, p12, p14; K2 p14–15; K3 p15–16; K4 p4, p12, p16; K5 p18; R1 p3, p5; R2 p4; R3 p5; R4 p6, p13; R5 p18; F1 p5, p7; F2 p4, p7; F3 p10–11; F4 p11; F5 p8; P1 p5, p12; P2 p5; P3 p6, p9–10; P4 p12, p14; C1 p5, p12; C2 p4; C3 p14, p16; C4 p16; C5 p15; C6 p12, p14; C7 p13; C8 p10; C9 p16; M1 p3, p10; M2 p10; M3 p4, p11; M4 p11; M5 p4, p11; M6 p4; M7 p7, p11; M8 p11; M9 p7, p11; M10 p11; M11 p4; M12 p11; M13 p4, p11; M14 p4; M15 p3; M16 p5; M17–M19 p11; M20 p10; Risks p5, p14, p18."},
  {"name":"South Indian Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Positive","guidance":"NIM guided at 3.36% to 3.38% in the shorter term and 3.45% to 3.50% over 12 months or four quarters.","metrics":[],"takeaways":["Net profit reached INR426 crores, growing 21% year-on-year from INR351 crore.","Total deposits grew 19% to INR137,279 crore year-on-year.","Gross advances grew 19% to INR109,517 crore year-on-year.","Gross NPA declined to 1.24% from 2.93% year-on-year; net NPA declined to 0.09% from 0.56%."],"revenue":[],"profit":[],"pipeline":[],"commitments":[],"margin":"NIM was 3.33%, exceeding the earlier guidance of around 3.25%.","risks":"Current-account deposits included one-off IPO flows; recovery income may decline after four to six quarters as the NPA stock is exhausted.","source":"Earnings call transcript, pages 1, 3–5, 15–17."},
  {"name":"Wipro Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"Q3 FY27 IT services revenue of $2.571 billion to $2.625 billion, or −1.5% to +0.5% QoQ in constant currency, newly given and hit by furloughs. Quarterly guidance only.","metrics":[{"m":"IT services revenue","v":"$2.61 billion; flat QoQ; +0.6% YoY cc"},{"m":"Operating margin","v":"16.5%, +0.5% QoQ"},{"m":"Net income","v":"INR32 billion, −5% QoQ"},{"m":"EPS","v":"INR3.23, +0.9% QoQ, +4.2% YoY"},{"m":"TCV","v":"$2.6 billion; nine large deals"},{"m":"BFSI","v":"+1% QoQ, +1.3% YoY cc"},{"m":"Consumer","v":"+2.6% QoQ, +6% YoY"},{"m":"EMR","v":"+1% QoQ, −6% YoY"},{"m":"Technology and Communications","v":"−2.6% QoQ, +7% YoY"},{"m":"Health","v":"−3.8% QoQ, −6.5% YoY"},{"m":"Americas 1","v":"−2.1% QoQ, −2.7% YoY"},{"m":"Americas 2","v":"+0.9% QoQ, −4.7% YoY"},{"m":"Europe","v":"+0.6% QoQ, +5.1% YoY"},{"m":"APMEA","v":"+3.1% QoQ, +13% YoY"},{"m":"Net other income","v":"−68% QoQ"},{"m":"Operating cash flow","v":"166% of net income"},{"m":"Gross cash (30 Sep)","v":"4.6 billion"},{"m":"Effective tax rate","v":"23.9%"},{"m":"India investment yield","v":"7.1%"},{"m":"AI-Native Unit engineers","v":"Over 2,000"}],"takeaways":["Q3 guide weighed by furloughs: The CFO said furloughs and fewer working days are \"largely\" what drive the Q3 range. Furloughs are expected \"broadly in line with what was there last year\".","Deals slipped to Q3: The CEO said two deals \"could not be signed on time\" and slip to Q3, where \"we are very optimistic of better closure\". The CFO said \"we will share that with you when we meet next in January\".","Margin band timeline withheld: The CFO said \"we would like to get back to the band as quickly as possible, but we're not putting a timeline out there\". Investment in the AI-Native Unit continues. The 17–17.5% band was cited by an analyst.","Client issues largely behind: The CFO said the client-specific issues highlighted earlier are \"largely behind us\", and in Q3 \"large part of it should be done\". Americas 1 declined on these issues and healthcare softness.","Pricing deflation near inflection: The CFO said average contract tenor is 2 to 2.5 years and the deflation cycle has run 1.5 to 2 years. \"We should be at that inflection curve in a few quarters.\""],"revenue":["Demand unchanged: The CEO said \"demand environment has remained largely unchanged\". Clients focus on efficiency while accelerating AI adoption, and cost optimisation and vendor consolidation remain drivers.","BFSI recovery: The CEO said a delayed mega deal \"is now starting to ramp up\", and a European BFSI client that moved work to captive shows \"good momentum\". BFSI and Tech compete to lead growth.","Health under pressure: The CEO said US health is \"impacted by policy changes\" and payers are tightening discretionary spend. First-time outsourcers are emerging, and Q3 seasonality will help \"though not to the extent seen previous years\".","Acquisition contributions: Mindsprint, acquired with the Olam deal, contributed \"one month's revenue\". The CFO called the Alpha Net tuck-in \"not very material\". HARMAN DTS helped win new logos.","Regional mix: APMEA grew 3.1% QoQ and 13% YoY, and Europe grew 0.6% QoQ and 5.1% YoY. Americas 2 grew 0.9% QoQ but fell 4.7% YoY, and Americas 1 declined 2.1% QoQ."],"profit":["Margin drivers: The CFO attributed the 0.5% margin gain to operational improvements and rupee depreciation, offset by business investments. The CEO said investments continue \"even when they create near-term margin headwinds\".","Lower other income post-buyback: The CFO said net other income fell 68% QoQ as the investable surplus shrank after the June 2026 buyback. Net income fell 5%, but EPS rose 0.9% QoQ.","Cash generation: Operating cash flow was 166% of net income. Gross cash at 30 September was 4.6 billion, the effective tax rate 23.9%, and the India investment yield 7.1%.","No wage hike now: The CFO said a salary increase was given effective 1st March 2026, and \"right now, it's not under consideration\". The CHRO said campus hiring resumes in H2 for volume growth."],"pipeline":["Order book and pipeline: TCV was $2.6 billion with nine large deals. The CEO said the \"pipeline for quarter three, quarter four, and year ahead, it is extremely strong\", with Q3 wins giving \"revenue upside in quarter 4\".","AI-Native Unit scaling: Launched in April, the unit has over 2,000 engineers including FDEs, with outcome-based wins including SLM deployments for a US retail bank and a US healthcare payer.","Five AI growth themes: The CEO said Wipro accelerated investment in AI data centers, AI foundry services, physical AI, AI devices and design-to-manufacturing, positioning across \"the full AI value chain\".","Inorganic strategy unchanged: Asked about large cash-funded acquisitions, the CEO said the strategy \"will continue to be the same\", looking for opportunities that enhance the consulting-led, AI-powered strategy in priority sectors.","GCC opportunities: The CEO said Wipro partners on greenfield, brownfield and GCC monetisation opportunities. A mid-market consumer products client chose Wipro to set up an AI capability center in India."],"commitments":[{"c":"Q3 revenue guidance","s":"$2.571B–$2.625B; −1.5% to +0.5% QoQ cc","t":"Q3 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Delayed large deals","s":"Two deals to be signed; \"confident of securing both\"","t":"Q3 FY27; update in January","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Margin improvement","s":"\"Get back to the band as quickly as possible\"; no timeline","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q4 outlook","s":"Q3 deal closures \"should increase… confidence for the outlook for Q4\"","t":"Q4 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Campus hiring","s":"Return to campus hiring for H2 volume growth","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Pricing deflation","s":"Inflection expected","t":"\"in a few quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Wage hike","s":"Not under consideration after 1 March 2026 increase","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Sequential growth","s":"\"That is the endeavor\"; no annual guidance; more in January","t":"January update","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"US healthcare policy changes, a challenging macro environment, Europe geopolitics, and slower client decision-making.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026; exchange cover letter p1, physical page numbers used). Guidance p5–6, p14; K1 p5, p14; K2 p7, p11, p14; K3 p10; K4 p4, p9; K5 p11; R1 p3, p10; R2 p8–9; R3 p4, p12; R4 p5, p8–10; R5 p4, p6; P1 p5–6; P2 p6; P3 p6; P4 p7, p14; I1 p4, p10–11, p13; I2 p5; I3 p5; I4 p8; I5 p4, p7; C1 p5–6; C2 p7, p14; C3 p10; C4 p13, p15; C5 p14; C6 p11; C7 p7; C8 p14; M1 p4, p6; M2 p4, p6; M3 p6; M4 p6; M5 p4; M6–M10 p6; M11–M14 p6; M15–M19 p6; M20 p5; Risks p4–5, p11, p12. Analyst-cited figures not confirmed: 6,500 headcount reduction and the 17–17.5% margin band."},
  {"name":"Cyient DLM Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 EBITDA margin \"close to around 9%\" and \"9% to 10% range\" (MD). FY27 revenue growth 30%–35% (\"probably, yes\"). Double-digit EBITDA margin from FY28 onwards.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue","v":"INR3,931 million, +26.5% YoY (vs INR3,106 million)"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR342 million, +9.5% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"8.7%, down 135 bps YoY; CFO also cited 10% last year"},{"m":"Normalized PAT","v":"INR158 million, +25.8% YoY; margin 4%"},{"m":"Reported PAT","v":"INR340 million, incl. INR182 million earn-out reversal"},{"m":"Reported PBT","v":"INR393 million"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR7,669 million, +30.2% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"INR733 million, +30.4% YoY; margin 9.6%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 normalized PAT","v":"INR321 million, +60.4% YoY; margin 4.2%, +79 bps"},{"m":"H1 FY27 reported PAT","v":"INR503 million, +27% YoY"},{"m":"Order book (end Q2)","v":"INR28.1 billion vs INR26 billion in Q1; book-to-bill 1.6x"},{"m":"Aerospace","v":"47% of revenue, +62% YoY"},{"m":"Industrial","v":"31% of revenue, +29% YoY"},{"m":"Defense and Medtech","v":"Defense 7%, +13% YoY; Medtech 14%, +14% YoY"},{"m":"PCBA","v":"48% of revenue, +19% YoY"},{"m":"Box build","v":"27% of revenue, +85% YoY (CFO); MD: 86%"},{"m":"Geography mix","v":"Rest of world 93%, India 7%"},{"m":"Net working capital days","v":"153 vs 161 in Q1; inventory 162; DSO 73; DPO 68"},{"m":"Operating free cash flow","v":"INR172 million"},{"m":"Finance costs","v":"INR61 million, -10.4% YoY"}],"takeaways":["Margin dip from investments: The CFO put the investment impact at 100 to 150 bps, all expensed. The Chairman said margins dropped \"about 100 bps\" because sales and marketing costs are \"fully loaded\", with \"zero absorption right now\".","No new S&M costs: The Chairman said no net new sales and marketing costs will be added until quarterly revenue at least doubles. The MD said \"There won't be any future investments in that area.\"","B2S programs outside order book: Four electronic products programs worth close to $1 billion over seven to nine years are not in the order book. Production may start in FY29, with full ramp-up from FY30.","H1 vs full-year growth: H1 revenue grew 30%. The MD said for H2 \"we'll definitely going above 30% growth\". He said Q2 growth was lower because of material availability and delays at customer sites.","Development spend not disclosed: The CFO declined to give a product development budget: \"we don't want to be putting a number\". He said costs are expensed, not capitalized, \"from a pure conservatism principle\"."],"revenue":["Mix shifting to box build: The MD said the shift towards higher-value integration reflects \"progression beyond component level manufacturing towards more integrated solutions\". Box build growth was given as 86% by the MD and 85% by the CFO.","Order book conversion lag: The MD said material challenges slow conversion of the order book. Execution typically takes 18 to 24 months, and a few orders run beyond two years.","Semiconductor equipment contributing: Semiconductor capital equipment has produced revenue for the last two quarters. The MD said \"we'll be seeing the growth every quarter in that area.\"","Geography stays export-led: The MD said the export market will continue to lead, with \"a little growth in that India market\" from domestic build-to-spec opportunities.","New customers: Three new logos were added in the quarter."],"profit":["Q2 margin drivers: The CFO attributed the margin moderation to revenue mix, product development costs, and higher employee costs from investment in sales, strategy and new growth areas.","Contribution margin higher: The Chairman said gross and contribution margins are \"significantly higher in FY '27 year-to-date than they're in FY '26\". He said Q2 contribution margin was also higher than a year ago.","Earn-out reversal one-off: Reported PAT includes a one-time earn-out provision reversal in other income. The CFO said other income also includes forex variations and export incentives, and \"every quarter, it keeps varying.\"","Working capital improved: Net working capital improved on lower DSO and higher DPO. The CFO said inventory optimisation and collections remain a focus. Finance costs fell on lower working capital borrowings.","Margin framework by phase: Strengthen phase is about 9%–11%, Expand phase (FY27–FY29) about 11%–13%, and Transform phase (FY30 onward) about 13%–18%."],"pipeline":["Pipeline and order intake: The pipeline is in excess of $1 billion, down from about $1.4 billion earlier. The MD expects order intake of $400 million to $500 million from the existing pipeline.","New sectors being explored: AI/data center and robotics are at an early stage (\"just started exploring\"). The MD will update on these \"by the end of this financial year\".","Sales leadership build-out: Salespeople, bid staff and marketing were added, including a Chief Growth Officer. The Chairman said \"we're ready to convert some large orders from the new sales team\".","Altek integration complete: The Altek Electronics integration is complete and the business is renamed Cyient DLM Electronics, Inc. The MD said a US sales team on the ground is pursuing growth.","Honeywell and eVTOL programs: Honeywell build-to-print prototypes are in testing with engineering changes, which delayed serial production. eVTOL contracts are signed but not yet in the order book; the MD expects production in FY29.","Addressable market cited: The MD estimated the high-reliability EMS opportunity at roughly $230 billion and the AI/data center EMS opportunity at about $58 billion."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 EBITDA margin","s":"\"Higher end of the single-digit range\"; \"close to around 9%\"; \"9% to 10% range\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY28 EBITDA margin","s":"Double digit; Chairman: backlog execution \"will take our margins into 10-plus percent\"","t":"\"FY '28 onwards\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 revenue growth","s":"\"Probably, yes… 30%, 35% growth\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 revenue growth","s":"\"We'll definitely going above 30% growth\"","t":"H2","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Order intake run-rate","s":"Close to INR600 crores plus in each of last two quarters; expected similar","t":"\"next two quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Pipeline order intake","s":"$400 million to $500 million from existing pipeline; timing \"a little difficult to say\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"AI/data center, robotics update","s":"Report on progress and pipeline","t":"\"by the end of this financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Sales and marketing cost freeze","s":"No net new S&M costs until at least double quarterly revenue","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"B2S programs production","s":"Four programs, close to $1 billion, seven to nine years; production may start FY29","t":"FY29; ramp-up \"FY '30 onwards\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"eVTOL revenue","s":"Contracts signed, not in order book; revenue when production starts","t":"\"from FY '29\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Semiconductor equipment growth","s":"\"We'll be seeing the growth every quarter in that area\"","t":"\"Now onwards\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Phase 2 margin framework","s":"Approximately 11% to 13% while expanding from four to seven industries","t":"FY27 to FY29","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited continued supply chain shortages, longer lead times and geopolitical uncertainties, \"especially driven by what is happening in the memory market\".","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026). Guidance p8–10, p13; K1 p7, p10–11; K2 p11; K3 p11–12, p15–16; K4 p13; K5 p7, p14; R1 p4–5; R2 p8–9; R3 p8, p17; R4 p16; R5 p4; F1 p5; F2 p10–11; F3 p4, p17; F4 p5; F5 p3; P1 p1, p12–13; P2 p7; P3 p1, p11, p18; P4 p4, p16; P5 p15; P6 p2; C1 p3, p8–9; C2 p10, p18; C3 p13; C4 p13; C5 p9; C6 p12–13; C7 p7, p17; C8 p11; C9 p15–16; C10 p15; C11 p17; C12 p3; M1 p5; M2–M3 p4–5; M4 p4, p6; M5 p4–5; M6 p6; M7–M10 p6; M11 p4–5; M12–M17 p5; M16 also p4; M18–M19 p5; M20 p5; Risks p1."},
  {"name":"Anand Rathi Wealth Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"12 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 revenue INR1,415 crores and PAT INR460 crores, maintained; H1 delivered 49% of revenue and 52% of PAT guidance.","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated net profit","v":"INR122 crores, +22% YoY"},{"m":"Total revenue","v":"About INR357 crores, +16% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT (ex non-business items)","v":"INR238 crores, about +23% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR693 crores, +17% YoY"},{"m":"H1 share of revenue guidance","v":"49% of INR1,415 crores"},{"m":"H1 share of PAT guidance","v":"52% of INR460 crores"},{"m":"Net new money","v":"INR4,186 crores, +39% YoY"},{"m":"Equity mutual fund net flow","v":"INR2,867 crores, +39%"},{"m":"Net flow split","v":"62% existing clients, 38% new clients"},{"m":"Client families","v":"14,309; 1,500 net added in last 12 months"},{"m":"AUM attrition","v":"0.17% in Q2; 0.26% in H1"},{"m":"Relationship managers","v":"431"},{"m":"Primary structured product issuance","v":"INR2,073 crores"},{"m":"Secondary structured product issuance","v":"INR750-odd crores"},{"m":"Digital wealth AUM","v":"INR2,531 crores, +14% YoY"},{"m":"Digital wealth clients","v":"7,584, +15%"},{"m":"OFA subscribers","v":"6,898"},{"m":"Non-cash charges","v":"INR46 crores vs zero in Q2 FY26"},{"m":"Interim dividend","v":"INR4 per share (post-bonus)"}],"takeaways":["Guidance held, no cut: CEO said that if 20%–25% PAT growth were in doubt \"with the slightest of the probabilities, I would have revised my PAT guidance down from 460.\"","Mutual fund licence filing: CFO said \"up to 20th of October, we can file\"; CEO said filing is expected \"by this month end\"; the AMC will be a wholly owned subsidiary, \"not going to be me-too\".","Structured product streak broken: Some products delivered 6%–7% instead of the full 14%–15%; CEO said none issued since December 2012 \"have ever lost money at maturity.\"","GIFT City inward only: CEO said GIFT City will be used \"always inward so far, long term also inward\"; nothing from it is provisioned in this year's guidance.","AUM target uncertain: CEO said about INR2,000 crores mark-to-market loss hit AUM this quarter; on the INR1.2 lakh crores target: \"will we get to 1,20,000, don't know\"."],"revenue":["Record net flows: Net new money INR4,186 crores, up 39%; equity mutual fund net flow INR2,867 crores, up 39%; CEO said in bad times \"clients have shared more moneys with us.\"","Existing vs new clients: Last quarter's net flows were 62% from existing clients and 38% from new clients.","No fee discounts: CEO said yields on 50 crore-plus clients are \"if not the same, marginally better\"; \"we don't give any discount on fee.\"","Structured product issuance: Primary issuance INR2,073 crores and secondary INR750-odd crores in the quarter; CEO said his concern is \"when do I get my trails to 50%\".","Wallet share: CEO estimated 50%–60% wallet share with platinum clients and 60%–70% for the top 100, measured on total wealth including physical assets."],"profit":["Non-cash ESOP charges: CFO said non-cash charges were INR46 crores against zero a year ago; CEO said the ESOP cost will reduce \"dramatically\" next year.","Margin floor and cap: CEO's target is PBT above 40% and PAT above 30%; if PBT rose to 48, he would reinvest to bring it back down.","Margins maintained: CFO said \"We have maintained all the margin. No treasury wise risks in our case.\"","Dividend: Board declared an interim dividend of INR4 per share on the post-bonus 16-odd crore shares."],"pipeline":["RM capacity: 431 RMs with capacity for 8,000 more clients; the next 100 RMs are identified and in training for promotion in 1 to 1.5 years; 500 account managers.","Referral-led acquisition: 165 client events this financial year; references are 50%–70% of clientele; CEO's 3-year goal is referrals plus website leads, avoiding cold calling.","International set-up: Started operations in London, UK, last quarter; received regulatory approvals to commence operations in GIFT City.","Research and technology: 182 research people focused on client alpha; hired four IITians designing algos for research.","Digital wealth business: Digital wealth AUM INR2,531 crores, up 14%; clients 7,584, up 15%; OFA platform has 6,898 subscribers."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 revenue","s":"INR1,415 crores; 49% delivered in H1","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 PAT","s":"INR460 crores, not revised; 52% delivered in H1","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"PAT growth","s":"\"Summation of 20%-25% PAT growth\"","t":"\"these two quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Structured product trails","s":"\"My trails to 50%, which I had given as guidance\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ESOP cost reduction","s":"\"Will the ESOP cost reduce? Yes, dramatically, yes.\"","t":"Next year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"PBT and PAT margins","s":"PBT above 40%, PAT above 30%; reinvest if PBT rises well above","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Mutual fund licence filing","s":"CFO: \"up to 20th of October, we can file\"; CEO: \"By this month end\"","t":"\"this month end\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"AUM target","s":"INR1.2 lakh crores; \"will we get to 1,20,000, don't know\"","t":"\"for the year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Net flows","s":"INR6,000 crores – INR7,000 crores – INR8,000 crores","t":"\"for the residual year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"GIFT City funds","s":"Two funds to become operational","t":"\"next 2- 3 months, latest\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"RM promotions","s":"Next 100 RMs identified, being trained for promotion","t":"\"next 1 year, 1.5 years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Acquisition model","s":"Referrals plus website leads only; avoid cold calling","t":"\"in 3 years\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"CEO said new clients may be unhappy, making 2%–3% against expected 10%–15%; in bad times it is difficult to prove merit to new clients.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, call dated 12 Oct 2026; filed 16 Oct 2026). Guidance p3; K1 p6; K2 p12–13; K3 p6; K4 p11–12; K5 p14; R1 p3; R2 p15; R3 p7; R4 p6, p18; R5 p5; F1 p5, p10; F2 p9; F3 p9; F4 p3; P1 p16; P2 p7–8; P3 p4; P4 p9, p15; P5 p4; C1 p3; C2 p3, p6; C3 p6; C4 p6; C5 p5; C6 p9; C7 p12; C8 p14; C9 p14; C10 p19; C11 p16; C12 p8; M1–M6 p3; M7–M8 p3; M9 p15; M10–M11 p4; M12 p16; M13–M14 p18; M15–M17 p4; M18 p10; M19 p3; Risks p15–16."},
  {"name":"RBL Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"12 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"Loan growth baseline of 22%–25% \"minimum\" (5%–6% per quarter). NIM \"slightly lower next quarter… inch up in Q4\". Card credit cost to fall to 5.5%–6%. The MD declined FY28 ROA targets.","metrics":[{"m":"Net interest income","v":"INR2,131 crores, +37% YoY, +29% QoQ"},{"m":"NIM","v":"4.61%, +48 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Non-interest income","v":"INR1,077 crores, +16% YoY; fee income INR1,040 crores, +12% YoY"},{"m":"Operating profit","v":"INR1,396 crore"},{"m":"Net profit","v":"INR482 crores, +170% YoY, +90% QoQ"},{"m":"Cost-to-income","v":"56.5% vs 70.7% in Q2 FY26"},{"m":"Advances","v":"INR142,314 crore, +42% YoY; average +33%"},{"m":"Deposits","v":"INR156,272 crore, +34% YoY; ex-FCNR +6%"},{"m":"CASA","v":"INR42,526 crores, +14% YoY; average CASA ratio 24%"},{"m":"Credit cost","v":"INR771 crores or 63 bps; specific 52 bps vs 62 bps"},{"m":"GNPA","v":"1.02%, down 28 bps QoQ"},{"m":"NNPA","v":"0.29%"},{"m":"PCR","v":"71.5%"},{"m":"Capital","v":"CAR 30.1%; CET1 29%"},{"m":"Average LCR","v":"148%"},{"m":"Balance sheet size","v":"INR2,22,344 crore"},{"m":"Term deposits","v":"INR1,13,746 crore"},{"m":"Card provisioning","v":"Approximately INR575 crores"},{"m":"FCNR leverage to customers","v":"Approximately INR10,300 crores"},{"m":"Investment book","v":"INR43,000 crore vs INR37,700 crore last quarter"}],"takeaways":["Card stress easing: The CFO said card slippages were 600-plus in the quarter but 125–130 in September alone. He expects about 400 per quarter in Q3 and Q4, with card credit cost falling from about 11% to 5.5%–6%.","Excess liquidity deployment: The capital raise and FCNR deposits added 35% to the balance sheet. Funds went to prepaying high-cost borrowings, wholesale and secured retail lending, and short-term investments, to be fully deployed \"over the medium term\".","FY28 targets declined: Asked about FY28 ROA, the MD said \"let us wait for some more time\" and pointed to the current 0.92. Board targets: \"Nothing on that chart now.\" Details will come \"over a period of next few months\".","Bancassurance hit expected: The MD put banca income at about INR300 crore a year and expects \"anywhere between 50% to 60% impact\" at minimum if the IRDA draft rules are implemented as written.","ENBD-linked strategy: Priorities include large corporates previously out of reach, trade corridors where ENBD is present, NRI flows starting with the Middle East, and wealth. The ENBD India business acquisition awaits RBI approval, \"definitely in this financial year\"."],"revenue":["Wholesale-led growth: Wholesale advances grew 56% YoY (corporate 60%, commercial banking 47%) and retail 32%. Secured retail includes about INR10,300 crores of FCNR leverage to customers. The retail-wholesale mix is 56/44.","Deposits ex-FCNR: Deposit growth excluding FCNR was 6% YoY as the bank optimised deposit cost. Retail term deposits grew around 16%. The CASA dip reflects SA rate cuts, with outflows typically recouped in four to five months.","Yield mix shift: Yield on advances fell from 11.2% to 10.8% on mix, as wholesale grew and unsecured retail fell, plus FCNR leverage. The ED said individual segment yields have not fallen.","Loan rate benchmarks: About 58% of loans are linked to external benchmarks (32% repo, 11% FBIL, 12% other) and about 3.5%–4% to MCLR. Repo resets are 90-day, so the full benefit comes next quarter.","Fee one-off: The CFO said fees included a one-off from FCNR currency and fixed-to-floating swaps: \"You cannot expect that to happen on an ongoing basis.\""],"profit":["Retail asset quality stable: Rural collections have been stable \"with no disruption so far\" and no material Middle East impact, but the MD is \"very watchful\" of deficient monsoons. JLG bucket X efficiency is close to 99.5%.","No standard asset buffer: The CFO said standard asset provisioning covered FCNR and gross loan growth, with \"no buffers on the standard asset side\". Card provisioning was about INR575 crores.","Opex outlook split: The MD guided opex growth \"around the same range of 7%, 8%\". The CFO said it \"will probably inch up to low single digits, maybe a little higher\". Card collection costs are now normalised.","Deposit rate path: The CFO said SA rate cuts will be gradual, and TD rates will move \"relative to what we see in the market\". The MD targets narrowing the deposit-cost gap with larger banks.","Liquidity and duration: Average LCR was 148%, with a normalised target of 120%–125%. The CFO said the investment book is \"light on duration\", so there is no mark-to-market concern."],"pipeline":["Branch expansion: The MD plans approximately 30 to 50 branches a quarter, with around 38 opening in a couple of weeks. The earlier 1,000-branch goal is now \"around two and a half, three years\".","Wealth proposition: The CFO said the bank is taking \"early step into investing in a wealth proposition… don't expect something to be launched next week\".","NRI and corridor build-out: The ED said the bank is building an NRI proposition to capture the UAE corridor, leveraging FCNR relationships, with MNC and IT-sector liability relationships also strengthening.","Card and MFI growth: The CFO expects cards to \"stop de-growing in a quarter\", then grow 10% to 12% next year. Microfinance grows only \"to the extent what we wanted as a PSL\".","Name change undecided: On a possible name change, the MD said \"we'll take a call at the appropriate time\" based on market feedback."],"commitments":[{"c":"Loan growth baseline","s":"22% to 25%, \"that's the minimum\"; model 5% to 6% quarterly","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"NIM trajectory","s":"\"Slightly lower next quarter and then probably inch up in Q4\"","t":"Q3; Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Card slippages","s":"\"Around the 400 plus minus number\" per quarter","t":"Q3, Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Card credit cost","s":"From 11% plus minus to 5.5% to 6% range","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Opex growth","s":"MD \"7%, 8%\"; CFO \"low single digits, maybe a little higher\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Branch additions","s":"30 to 50 per quarter; 1,000 branches","t":"\"two and a half, three years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ENBD India acquisition","s":"Awaiting RBI approval","t":"\"Definitely in this financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Strategy details","s":"Building blocks to be articulated","t":"\"over the coming months\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"LCR normalisation","s":"120% to 125% zone","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Card book growth","s":"Stop de-growing; 10% to 12% growth next year","t":"\"in a quarter\"; next year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY28 mix","s":"Overall growth mid-20%s; unsecured high single or low double digits","t":"FY28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Banca income impact","s":"50% to 60% impact \"should be minimum to be penciled in\" if draft implemented","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The MD cited heightened geopolitical tensions, elevated crude prices, tighter global financial conditions, inflation risks from energy, freight and food, and possible further rate increases.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 12 Oct 2026). Guidance p4–6, p16; K1 p6; K2 p1–2; K3 p9, p12–13; K4 p5; K5 p2, p15; R1 p3; R2 p3–4; R3 p7; R4 p4; R5 p15; F1 p2; F2 p8; F3 p5–6; F4 p2, p18; F5 p6, p17; P1 p4, p15; P2 p6; P3 p4; P4 p16, p19; P5 p13; C1 p16; C2 p5; C3 p6; C4 p6; C5 p5–6; C6 p4, p15; C7 p15; C8 p2, p9; C9 p17; C10 p16, p19; C11 p20; C12 p5; M1–M3 p3; M4 p2; M5 p3; M6 p2; M7–M9 p2–3; M10 p3; M11–M14 p3; M15 p3; M16–M17 p2; M18 p8; M19 p2–3; M20 p17; Risks p1."},
  {"name":"Sejal Glass Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"FY27 revenue growth of 25% to 40% maintained (\"Minimum 25%, maximum 40%\"). EBITDA margin around 18%, which the CFO expects \"above 18%\" for the full year, and PAT margin approximately 10%.","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated total income","v":"INR139.86 crore vs INR105.04 crore, +33.14% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR25.66 crore vs INR17.89 crore, +43.44%"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"18.35% vs 17.03%, +132 bps"},{"m":"Net profit","v":"INR14.42 crore vs INR8.11 crore, +77.61%"},{"m":"Net profit margin","v":"10.31% vs 7.73%, +258 bps"},{"m":"EPS","v":"INR12.52 vs INR7.95"},{"m":"H1 FY27 total income","v":"INR259.29 crore vs INR182.81 crore, +41.84%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"INR43.68 crore, +43.79%; margin 16.85% vs 16.62%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 net profit","v":"INR21.63 crore vs INR12.53 crore, +72.6%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EPS","v":"INR18.79 vs INR12.28, +53%"},{"m":"Taloja utilisation","v":"Tempering 69% vs 55%; IG almost 40% vs 24%"},{"m":"Erode utilisation","v":"Tempering over 24% vs 13%; IG over 18% vs under 5%"},{"m":"Erode EBITDA margin","v":"Around 10%; Taloja near breakeven"},{"m":"UAE order book (Sep)","v":"Approximately AED78 million"},{"m":"India order book","v":"Around INR85 crore"},{"m":"July UAE order wins","v":"AED27.6 million, around INR71 crore"},{"m":"Annual production capacity","v":"Approximately 61.94 lakh square meters"},{"m":"UAE capex requirement","v":"Around AED20 million"}],"takeaways":["Growth guidance held: The promoter reiterated 25% to 40% YoY revenue growth for FY27. Asked about the annual report's 35% outlook, the CFO said \"Minimum 25%, maximum 40%\".","Fire-rated glass launch: The manufacturing line has been imported and is being installed. The promoter targets commercial production by end of Q3 FY27, while the CFO said trial runs come in Q3 and commercial sales from Q4.","Specialty mix target: The CFO expects value-added products at about 5% of revenue this year and \"15% or 20%\" next year. Fire-rated EBITDA margins are \"more than 30%\", with guidance only \"in the first quarter of the '28\".","Acquisitions under evaluation: To raise India's revenue share, the company is looking at \"acquisitions or capacity addition in the coming quarter, in the coming year\". The CFO said \"Nothing as such concrete to disclose as of now.\"","Peak revenue timing: An analyst cited peak revenue of INR600–650 crore from existing infrastructure. The CFO said \"I think so we'll be able to reach in '28\" but did not confirm the figure."],"revenue":["India plants ramping: Taloja tempering utilisation rose from 55% to 69% QoQ and IG from 24% to almost 40%. Erode tempering went from 13% to more than 24% and IG from under 5% to over 18%.","UAE near capacity: The CFO said UAE runs at \"about 90% in capacity\" on IG glass. A third tempering line is due in Q3. The UAE order book was approximately AED78 million in September, covering six to nine months.","Geographic mix: The CFO said the India-UAE revenue mix \"will be 60%-40%. 60% is from the UI, 40% from India.\" Exports to Europe and Africa are \"less than 5%\", with Africa still developing.","Railway glass slow build: The company is an approved vendor at three railway coach factories. Over INR100 crores of railway tenders come each quarter, with about 5% conversion (\"Less than 5%\") as a new, category C vendor.","India project reach: The CFO said India is shifting from plain tempered to IG and laminate glass, supplying projects in Bangalore, Hyderabad, Mumbai and Gujarat. Project deliveries run six to eight months."],"profit":["COGS-led margin gain: The CFO attributed margin improvement to raw material changes (silicon, PVB from new vendors), better input cost negotiation, manpower and machine efficiency in UAE, and the UAE facade division. He called the gross margin \"very much sustainable\".","Plant-level profitability: The CFO said Erode runs at a positive EBITDA of around 10%, while Taloja is \"more or less on the still on the breakeven side\". Higher utilisation will absorb fixed costs.","Cost pass-through in UAE: The CFO said Middle East tensions raised logistics and shipment costs, which are \"still passing on to the end users\". Developer and facade activity continues.","Working capital constraints: UAE runs on post-dated cheques, but 25% of business is on LCs that are discounted. India requires buying coated glass in advance. Working capital is funded from internal resources.","No debt prepayment: The CFO said debt is long-term bank borrowing repaid by EMI, with \"nothing on that front to reduce that or making prepayments\"."],"pipeline":["UAE capex funding: The third tempering line, fire-rated line and related facilities need around AED20 million, funded roughly 60% from internal accruals and 40% from bank finance.","Order wins: The UAE subsidiary won five orders of about AED27.6 million (around INR71 crore) in July 2026. Domestic projects worth about INR150 crore were secured, and the India order book is around INR85 crore.","Specialty product pipeline: Digital printing has expanded the decorative range, and bullet-resistant glass has progressed through testing in India. The CFO estimates the fire-rated market at \"above INR500 crore including India and UAE\".","Export market build: The company began exporting from India to Israel and Cyprus last year, and to Sri Lanka this year. One salesperson is developing Africa.","Capacity and long-term view: Total capacity is approximately 61.94 lakh square meters across Silvassa, Taloja, Erode and Ras Al Khaimah. The CFO expects \"more 25%\" YoY growth on the same capacities, more with additions."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 revenue growth","s":"25% to 40% YoY; \"Minimum 25%, maximum 40%\"; maintained","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EBITDA margin","s":"Around 18%; CFO \"above 18% as a whole year\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"PAT margin","s":"Approximately 10%","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Fire-rated glass production","s":"Trial in Q3; commercial production end of Q3 (promoter) / commercial sale from Q4 (CFO)","t":"Q3–Q4 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Value-added product share","s":"About 5% this year; \"15% or 20%\" next year","t":"FY27; FY28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"UAE third tempering line","s":"\"More or less we are on track\"","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Fire-rated guidance","s":"Market, margin and revenue guidance to be given","t":"\"first quarter of the '28\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Peak revenue","s":"Reach peak revenue on existing infrastructure; analyst's INR600–650 crore not confirmed","t":"\"'28\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Acquisitions or capacity addition","s":"Evaluating; \"Nothing as such concrete to disclose\"","t":"\"coming quarter, in the coming year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"UAE capex funding","s":"Around AED20 million; 60% internal accruals, 40% bank finance","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Plant visit","s":"Investor plant visit to be arranged","t":"\"very soon\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Middle East geopolitical tensions raising logistics and input costs; working capital tied up in UAE credit terms and advance glass purchases in India.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p2, p4–5; K1 p2, p4; K2 p2–3; K3 p6, p11; K4 p10; K5 p5–6; R1 p3–4; R2 p1–2, p8; R3 p10; R4 p2, p11, p14–15; R5 p8–9, p13; P1 p4–5, p12; P2 p3; P3 p12; P4 p14; P5 p12; I1 p10; I2 p2, p5; I3 p2, p6; I4 p10, p13; I5 p1, p9; C1 p2, p4; C2 p2, p5; C3 p2; C4 p2–3; C5 p11; C6 p5, p8; C7 p6; C8 p5–6; C9 p10; C10 p10; C11 p15; M1–M6 p1; M7–M10 p1; M11–M13 p3–4; M14 p2; M15 p5; M16 p2; M17 p1; M18 p10; Risks p12, p14. Omitted as garbled or unconfirmed: UAE order book stated as \"INR78 million\" (vs AED78 million), \"COGS has been improved to 3%\", and analyst-cited Silvassa IGU utilisation of 43% / 50% target."},
  {"name":"E2E Networks Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"The MD declined to give guidance: \"We don't give any guidance\". He sees \"70% to 70% plus\" EBITDA margin over the medium and long term.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue","v":"INR2,238 million, +411% YoY, +43% QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR1,715 million, +35% QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"76.6%, +141 bps vs Q1 FY27"},{"m":"Profit before tax","v":"INR971 million vs INR586 million in Q1, +66%"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR727 million vs INR439 million in Q1"},{"m":"PAT margin","v":"32.5% vs 28%"},{"m":"Diluted EPS","v":"INR3.48 vs INR2.1 in Q1"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"3805 million, +376% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"2894 million at 76%; 2.3 times full-year FY26 EBITDA"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR1,166 million"},{"m":"IndiaAI Mission revenue share","v":"Roughly around 13 to 15%"},{"m":"International revenue share","v":"Roughly around 20 to 25%"},{"m":"Blackwell contract value","v":"Nearly INR1,000 crore, up to June 2029"},{"m":"Next B200 lot","v":"1,024 instances, go-live expected this quarter"}],"takeaways":["Blackwell cluster moved to contract: The existing B200s moved from pay-as-you-go to a multi-year contract of nearly INR1,000 crore with a sovereign AI company, running to June 2029. The MD said that contract fully uses the flagship capacity.","No guidance, by policy: The MD declined to guide on MRR exit rate, capex, GPU additions or contracted revenue share. He said \"look back at what we have done rather than giving you future guidance.\"","GPUs kept for own R&D: The MD said some GPUs are used by the company's own team rather than sold to customers, to build out the platform: \"that's also an investment into the future.\"","Pricing secondary to adoption: Asked about pricing for the next B200 tranche, the MD said \"our goal would be to make somewhat higher amount of money\". He added that strategy is \"not dependent on\" a \"few percentage points more\".","Segment metrics withheld: Management declined to break out inference, contracted revenue or utilisation. It called these point-in-time figures \"subject to like wild swings\", not \"really trackable metrics\"."],"revenue":["Quarter's growth drivers: The CFO attributed the performance to contribution from Blackwell GPUs and higher average GPU utilisation.","Customer mix: The CFO said IndiaAI Mission customers were roughly 13 to 15% of quarterly revenue, and international customers roughly 20 to 25%.","Platform vs bare metal: The MD said \"Majority of our GPUs like today run on top of our platform\", with \"very, very little bare metal\".","IndiaAI Mission relationships: The MD said the company uses IndiaAI Mission to build relationships with customers who may continue to work with it \"beyond the remit of IndiaAI mission\"."],"profit":["Gross margin not committed: An analyst cited an 88% gross margin; management did not confirm it. The MD called the numbers \"moving targets\", affected by capex timing and capacity built before it goes live.","Debt stance: The MD said there is no upper limit on debt, \"as long as we have the ability to repay it\" and cash flow supports it, subject to lender covenants.","Funding options open: On off-balance sheet GPU funding the MD said \"never say never\". On equity, \"we'll keep everyone informed in case like there is any future equity raise.\"","GPU advances: The MD said whether an advance is paid \"depends on like the vendor\" and changes \"week on week or month on month\"."],"pipeline":["Next B200 lot: Deployment of the already announced 1,024 B200 instances is expected this quarter. The MD said \"We have not decided anything\" on contracting this lot.","Leadership additions: A new Chief Revenue Officer was hired, a founder's office team was added, and Anand Roy joined as a strategic advisor.","Inference products soft-released: Blaze and Endpoint in Jarvis Labs were \"soft released\", not launched. The MD said this is \"the general direction we are taking\".","Capex disclosure approach: The MD said capex plans are announced only once \"sufficiently justified and solidified\", and called the company \"aggressive as well as prudential\" on capex deployment.","Market view: The MD put the global AI infrastructure market at \"anywhere close to like a trillion dollars\" over the medium term. He called it \"a vast blue ocean\".","Partnerships and chips: The MD described an arm's-length relationship with L&T and said the company is open to partnerships with all players. It will add support for new chipmakers if customers require it."],"commitments":[{"c":"EBITDA margin target","s":"\"70% to 70% plus kind of like an EBITDA margin business\"","t":"Medium term and long term","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Next B200 lot go-live","s":"1,024 instances; \"we expect the deployment to go through\"","t":"\"this quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Next B200 contracting","s":"\"We have not decided anything around the next lot of B200\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Blackwell contract term","s":"Nearly INR1,000 crore multi-year contract with a sovereign AI company","t":"Up to June 2029","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Earnings from existing assets","s":"For uncontracted or short-term capacity, \"we obviously expect the earnings to grow from the same assets\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Capex and deployment plan","s":"Will inform everyone \"at the same time\" once plans clarify; no speculative guidance","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"B300 / Vera Rubin plans","s":"\"We will obviously kind of like keep everyone informed\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Equity raise","s":"Will inform \"in case like there is any future equity raise\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gross margin at full utilisation","s":"Asked if above 70%: \"I don't see any, uh, change in that, uh, Overall target\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The MD said GPU procurement and advance terms are \"subject to massive changes like week on week or month on month\", depending on vendors' order books.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026). Guidance p3; K1 p1, p3, p13; K2 p3, p6, p8; K3 p1, p4; K4 p5; K5 p4–5, p7–8; R1 p2; R2 p6–7; R3 p15; R4 p6; F1 p3, p18; F2 p6–7; F3 p6, p11; F4 p17; P1 p2, p14, p18; P2 p1; P3 p9–10; P4 p1–2; P5 p10–11; P6 p13, p16–17; C1 p3; C2 p2, p14; C3 p18; C4 p1; C5 p4; C6 p6; C7 p14; C8 p6; C9 p18; M1–M9 p2; M10 p6; M11 p7; M12 p1; M13 p14; Risks p17."},
  {"name":"360 ONE WAM Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Positive","guidance":"Cost-to-operating-income: \"100 to 150 basis points of improvement\" year on year, \"well on course\" (CFO); MD aims to move \"48 towards the 46-47\" over 12 to 18 months.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR896 crores, +17.5% YoY"},{"m":"Total revenue","v":"INR853 crores, +4.8% YoY"},{"m":"Total costs","v":"INR462 crores, +15.5% YoY"},{"m":"Operating PBT","v":"INR434 crores, +19.7% YoY"},{"m":"PBT","v":"INR391 crores"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR302 crores vs INR316 crores in Q2 FY26"},{"m":"Cost-to-operating-income ratio","v":"51.5% vs 54.3% in Q1"},{"m":"Tangible ROE","v":"16.9%"},{"m":"Total AUM","v":"About INR9 lakh crores, +31% YoY"},{"m":"ARR AUM","v":"INR3,53,000 crores, +19.7% YoY"},{"m":"ARR net flows","v":"INR12,482 crores vs INR10,815 crores in Q1"},{"m":"ARR revenue","v":"INR706 crores, +27.5% YoY; 79% of operating revenues"},{"m":"ARR retention","v":"79 bps vs 74 bps in Q1; 67 bps excluding carry"},{"m":"Third-party fund carry","v":"Approximately INR60 crores"},{"m":"Transaction and brokerage income","v":"INR190 crores, -9.1% YoY"},{"m":"Other income","v":"Negative INR44 crores"},{"m":"Wealth ARR AUM","v":"INR2,52,627 crores, +24.3% YoY"},{"m":"Wealth net flows","v":"INR10,990 crores"},{"m":"AIF AUM","v":"INR58,139 crores, +27.9% YoY"},{"m":"Second interim dividend","v":"INR7.50 per share"}],"takeaways":["Treasury MTM hit profit: Other income was negative INR44 crores on mark-to-market of treasury investments; MD said NSE-related fair value change was \"close to roundabout 110-120 crores\", largely invested through its funds.","ROE target set at 20%: Tangible ROE 16.9%; MD aims for \"around that 20% zip code\" over 3 to 3.5 years by doubling profits, and does not \"see any need to raise capital.\"","UBS flows building: MD set a target of \"$600-odd million\" of flows exchanged this year; about \"$300, $325 million\" happened over the last 3 months; expects 40 to 50 basis points retention.","Capital markets ramp timeline: MD said the full team will be ready \"31st March, first week of April\"; banking to come in \"a big way\" in Q1 of next financial year; appointed in 8 to 10 issues.","Flow target not quantified: An analyst asked about beating a 12–15% flows-to-opening-AUM range; MD gave no number, saying the flow mix gives \"a fair degree of confidence for us to keep achieving our net flow targets.\""],"revenue":["Carry lifted retention: ARR retention improved to 79 bps from 74 bps \"driven by higher carry income\"; excluding carry, stable at 67 bps; third-party fund carry approximately INR60 crores; carry reversals more than offset by NSE-listing carry.","Flow source split differs: MD said this quarter 50% of flows came from wallet-share enhancement and 50% from new mandates, against a typical 65–70% from new mandates; Wealth CEO cited a general 1/3 new, 2/3 existing split.","TBR now brokerage-led: MD said TBR \"has been soft\"; upfront income components have disappeared; expects TBR growth in \"late single digits to early double digits\" versus ARR.","Advisory retention bands: MD said core advisory retentions are 35–40 bps, very large mandates 20–25 bps, and overall relationship retention comes back to \"65 to 72-73 basis points\".","Captive brokerage synergy: Equity brokerage from HNI/UHNI clients moved from 4.5–5 crores a month to 8–9 crores a month; MD said it \"should be doing around about 15-20 crores a month.\""],"profit":["One-time restructuring cost: Employee costs include a one-time impact of about INR9 crores from restructuring one of its businesses.","Hiring cost ceiling: MD said productivity gains remain \"especially on the employee cost side\"; a big build could raise cost-to-income ratios by \"100, 150, 200 basis points\" at most.","Capital base: MD cited tangible net worth of \"7,400 crores\", close to 1,600 crores coming from UBS, and \"1,000-1,200 crores\" of post-dividend profit for the next 2–3 years.","Capital needs cap ROE: MD said alternates need 3 to 4% of AUM as sponsor capital plus NBFC lending capital, so he is \"comfortable with the 18-20% ROE\" rather than 25%.","Dividend: Board approved a second interim dividend of INR7.50 per share."],"pipeline":["Fund launches: Launched and mobilised Multistage AI Fund, Defence and Space Fund, and Arise Credit Fund; Dyna SIF AUM crossed INR1,000 crores.","Alternates flow cycle: Alternates saw distributions of about INR2,500 crore plus, gross inflows \"closer to the INR4,800-4,900 crore\", and net inflows close to INR2,500 crore.","Large fund redemption ahead: MD said fund P1A, about INR3,000–3,500 crores current value, will go back over 6 to 18 months; launches will be planned to reflect that redemption.","Institutional mandates: MD said the institutional redemption is \"behind us\"; won 2 institutional mandates late in the quarter and its first European institutional mandate.","Senior leadership hires: Ashish joins in a senior leadership role with 2 others; MD said the first 6 to 8 months are about integrating; talent search is at the \"top quartile\".","Segment build-outs: HNI business manages over INR8,000 crores for 1,500 clients with about 60 RMs, expected to break even on direct costs \"soon\"; ETMoney has 5.5 lakh plus active users."],"commitments":[{"c":"Cost-to-operating-income improvement","s":"\"100 to 150 basis points of improvement\"; \"well on course to achieve that\"","t":"Year on year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Cost-to-income further reduction","s":"\"Move that 48 towards the, uh, towards the 46-47 number\"","t":"\"next 12 to 18 months\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"UBS flow exchange","s":"Target of \"$600-odd million\"; MD later cited \"$600 to $700-odd million\"","t":"Current financial year, \"by March end\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Asset management flow targets","s":"\"Should be in a position to meet our targets\"; no number given","t":"Current year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"HNI business break-even","s":"Expected to \"break even on direct costs soon\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Capital markets team","s":"Full-stack team and talent in place","t":"\"31st March, first week of April\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Banking / ECM revenue","s":"\"We see really ourselves coming in a big way\"","t":"\"Q2 of next calendar year, or effectively Q1 of next financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"P1A fund redemption","s":"About INR3,000–3,500 crores to go back; launches to reflect redemption","t":"\"6 to 18 months\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Profit doubling","s":"\"Double our profits\"; no need to raise capital","t":"\"next 3.5 years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Tangible ROE","s":"\"Around that 20% zip code on the tangible\" net worth","t":"\"3, 3.5 years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"TBR size","s":"\"Zip code of… INR800-1,000 crores\" when revenue reaches INR5,000–5,500 crores","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"CFO cited near-term challenges: market volatility, competition, evolving regulation, and pressure on costs.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026). Guidance p3–4; K1 p1, p3; K2 p1, p16–17; K3 p4, p12; K4 p11; K5 p12–14; R1 p1, p6; R2 p8, p14; R3 p13–14; R4 p15–16; R5 p11; F1 p1; F2 p4, p6; F3 p16; F4 p16–17; F5 p1; P1 p2; P2 p15; P3 p12; P4 p2, p6; P5 p4–6; P6 p2; C1 p3; C2 p4; C3 p4; C4 p6; C5 p2; C6 p11; C7 p11; C8 p12; C9 p16; C10 p16–17; C11 p14; M1–M16 p1; M17–M19 p1–2; M20 p1; Risks p1."},
  {"name":"Axis Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"17 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"Through-the-cycle NIM of 3.8% maintained, timeframe not given; loan growth 300 bps above industry over the medium term, maintained; \"we do not need equity capital\".","metrics":[{"m":"Net interest income","v":"15,371 crore, +12% YoY, +5% QoQ"},{"m":"Fee income","v":"6,381 crores, +6% YoY, +4% QoQ"},{"m":"Operating expenses","v":"10,819 crores, +9% YoY, +11% QoQ"},{"m":"Operating profit","v":"11,083 crores, +6% YoY"},{"m":"Core operating profit","v":"10,998 crores, +11% YoY"},{"m":"Profit after tax","v":"7,003 crores, +38% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 net interest income","v":"30,018 crore, +10% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 profit after tax","v":"14,117 crore, +30% YoY"},{"m":"NIM","v":"3.36%, -37 bps YoY, -10 bps QoQ; 3.47% ex-FCNRB"},{"m":"Cost-to-assets","v":"2.14%, -24 bps YoY, -6 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Net credit cost","v":"1,564 crores at 0.45%, -28 bps YoY, -18 bps QoQ"},{"m":"GNPA","v":"1.16%, -30 bps YoY, -12 bps QoQ"},{"m":"NNPA","v":"0.35%, -9 bps YoY, -4 bps QoQ"},{"m":"PCR","v":"70%"},{"m":"Gross slippages","v":"4,762 crores"},{"m":"Total advances","v":"+23% YoY, +9% QoQ; +19% YoY ex-FCNRB loans"},{"m":"Total deposits (QAB)","v":"+23% YoY"},{"m":"CASA deposits (QAB)","v":"+14% YoY"},{"m":"Consolidated ROA / ROE","v":"1.49% / 13.96%"},{"m":"CET-1 (including H1 profit)","v":"14.61%"}],"takeaways":["FCNRB weighed on NIM: NIM 3.36%, down 10 bps QoQ; CFO attributed 11 bps to FCNRB grossing-up and liquidity carry; excluding it, NIM 3.47%, up 1 bp; benefit expected \"in Q3 and Q4\".","NIM timeline not given: CFO said the 3.8% guidance is unchanged but \"The time frame of this is difficult to sort of ascertain\"; declined to give a September exit NIM.","Insurance fee risk pending: ED said the IRDAI consultation impact cannot yet be specified; expects \"about 6 to 9 months\" before it comes through; bank does not give fee breakup by product.","Retail mix to rebuild: Retail is 54% of advances; ED said the effort is to \"get back to the above 55% retail mix\" while corporate opportunities continue.","UPI MDR uncertain: Implementation postponed, so no guidance; MD said any revenue would be reinvested, and \"it's very difficult to predict as to where the penny will drop.\""],"revenue":["Growth ex-FCNRB: Advances grew 23% YoY, 19% excluding international branch loans against FCNR-B deposits; retail book 16%, or 10% excluding FCNRB leverage; SME 25%; wholesale 34%.","Retail disbursements: Retail disbursements grew 22% YoY: home loans 24%, vehicle loans 25%, SBB 33%, retail agri 19%, consumer loans 20%.","Selective corporate growth: Wholesale growth spans capex, project finance, refinancing and working capital; \"we are not chasing assets\", and assets failing transfer pricing, RAROC and RWA filters are let go.","Fee growth soft: Fees grew 6% YoY; ED cited lower asset-linked fees on corporate growth, weak card spends, and festive season shifting to October; no fees charged for raising FCNRB deposits.","Deposit franchise: FCNRB market share 8% this quarter; NTB average balances up 20%; NTB salary balances up 40%; Burgundy AUM up 27% YoY."],"profit":["One-time costs: Opex includes 254 crores one-time, slightly more than half Labour Code-related, the rest FCNRB mobilisation; Q1 had 271 crores staff cost reversals; adjusted QoQ opex rise 572 crores, about 6%.","Credit cost improving: Gross slippages 4,762 crores, retail 4,286 crores; written-off recoveries 971 crores, up 51%; CFO said no unusual recovery and expects further improvement, without specific guidance.","Provision buffer: Provisions of 8,006 crores not reckoned in regulatory capital, about 49 bps cushion; CFO does not expect material impact from ECL transition.","Capital and funding: CET-1 14.61% including H1 profit; raised US$600 million AT-1 to call back the earlier AT-1, and US$600 million senior notes in Q2.","Repricing lag: Repricing happens in the last week of the quarter; CFO said the latest repo rate increase benefits the book in Q4 rather than Q3; 72% of loans floating."],"pipeline":["Branch expansion: Net 119 new branches in the quarter and 139 in H1; H1 period-end headcount down 375 versus 31 March 2026.","Technology spend: Technology and digital were 10% of opex; AXIOM enterprise AI model with DocAI and GenAI-powered collections in use.","Payments launches: First Indian bank to enable Apple Pay for credit card customers; launched UPI limit management feature in the mobile app.","ECLGS lending: Around 12,500 loans worth around 10,700 crores sanctioned; around 80% disbursed.","Self-employed lending: Personal loans only to salaried customers; self-employed served through business loans, where disbursals are growing \"quite sharply\".","FCNRB runway: ED said \"we have not used the full FCNRB liquidity to grow our asset\"; CFO said full optimisation will take \"the next couple of quarters\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"Through-cycle NIM","s":"3.8%, \"remains unchanged\"; timeframe not specified","t":"\"through the cycle\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Sequential NIM improvement","s":"\"Sequentially we should be able to show an improvement\" from FCNR optimisation","t":"\"Q3 and Q4\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FCNRB optimisation","s":"Full benefit of deployment and optimisation","t":"\"the next couple of quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Loan growth","s":"\"300 basis points higher than the industry average\"","t":"\"over the medium term\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Equity capital","s":"\"We do not need equity capital for either the growth or protection pillars\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Retail mix","s":"\"Get back to the above 55% retail mix\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Cost-to-assets","s":"Steady decline; \"we do expect that journey to continue\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Credit cost","s":"\"Further improvement\" expected; \"we won't be able to give a specific guidance\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Repo repricing benefit","s":"Benefit of latest repo increase on book","t":"Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ECL transition","s":"No material impact expected on transition or flow","t":"\"as we come closer to the date\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"LCR band","s":"Managed within 115% to 120%","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Insurance fee mitigation","s":"Mitigate through volumes, product mix and other fee sources","t":"\"about 6 to 9 months\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"MD cited geopolitical tensions, rising energy and food prices, tariff and weather risks; CFO is monitoring inflation, liquidity and cost of funds.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 17 Oct 2026). Guidance p4, p7, p16; K1 p4, p7; K2 p7, p10–11, p14; K3 p10–12; K4 p5–7; K5 p12; R1 p1; R2 p5; R3 p8; R4 p3, p10–11; R5 p2; F1 p4, p9; F2 p5, p8, p14; F3 p4, p16; F4 p4; F5 p5, p15; P1 p4; P2 p2, p4; P3 p2–3; P4 p9; P5 p16; P6 p7, p14; C1 p7; C2 p7, p11; C3 p7; C4 p16; C5 p4; C6 p7; C7 p13; C8 p8; C9 p15; C10 p16–17; C11 p15; C12 p10; M1–M8 p3; M9 p4; M10–M14 p3; M15 p5; M16 p1; M17–M18 p2; M19 p3; M20 p4; Risks p1, p6."},
  {"name":"Bajaj Healthcare Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27: 10%–15% revenue and profit growth with 18%–20% margin (analyst-cited; CFO: \"we are on track\"); FY29 target Rs 900–1,000 crores revenue at 18%–21% EBITDA margin.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"Rs 175.9 crores, +18.9% YoY, +6.2% QoQ"},{"m":"Gross profit","v":"Rs 85.4 crores, +12.4% YoY; gross margin 48.3%"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"Rs 32.1 crores, +12.2% YoY, +8.6% QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"18.2% vs 17.8% in Q1"},{"m":"PAT","v":"Rs 14.1 crores, +26.9% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"Rs 341.5 crores, +15.1% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"Rs 61.7 crores; margin about 18.1%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"Rs 27.8 crores, +21.1% YoY"},{"m":"Domestic API revenue","v":"Rs 71.6 crores vs Rs 76.3 crores YoY, Rs 92 crores in Q1"},{"m":"H1 FY27 domestic API revenue","v":"Rs 163 crores, +10.1% YoY"},{"m":"Export API revenue","v":"Rs 62.6 crores, +28.5% YoY, +25.1% QoQ"},{"m":"Formulations revenue","v":"Rs 41.7 crores vs Rs 22.9 crores in Sep 2025 quarter"},{"m":"H1 FY27 formulations revenue","v":"Rs 65 crores, +38.4% YoY"},{"m":"API exports plus formulations share","v":"Close to 60% of revenue"},{"m":"Net worth (30 Sep)","v":"About Rs 557 crores vs Rs 533 crores in March 2026"},{"m":"Debt to equity","v":"About 0.5x"},{"m":"Receivable days","v":"135 days, from 145"},{"m":"FY27 capex","v":"Rs 65 to Rs 80 crores"},{"m":"R&D spend","v":"Approximately 2.5% to 3%"}],"takeaways":["Exports and formulations lead: API exports and formulations crossed Rs 100 crores in quarterly revenue, close to 60% of revenue; domestic API fell to Rs 71.6 crores from Rs 76.3 crores YoY.","Inventory built for West Asia: MD raised inventory so facilities are not stopped by the Gulf dispute; CFO called it \"a policy and a strategic decision\", holding back EBITDA-to-cash conversion.","First anti-seizure launch: First DCGI approval in India for a next-generation anti-seizure tablet, launched in September through 8 partners; repeat orders received; MD said revenue is \"very difficult\" to quantify for 2 years.","GenRx acquisition approved: NCLT approved the GenRx Pharmaceutical acquisition; MD expects 200 to 250 crores from that unit in the next 2 to 3 years.","Domestic API pricing flat: MD said API prices are \"more or less stabilized\" and, over time, \"there will not be much improvement in the API pricing. It will be a straight line.\""],"revenue":["Formulation surge: MD said formulations grew 82% YoY; CFO cited Rs 41.7 crores versus Rs 22.9 crores, \"nearly 79% growth\"; H1 formulations Rs 65 crores, up 38.4%.","API export growth: Export API Rs 62.6 crores, up 28.5% YoY and 25.1% QoQ; MD attributes it to approvals on DMFs filed over the last 1.5 to 2 years.","Product concentration: Magnesium L-Threonate, supplied to the US patent holder, contributes \"around 11 to 12% of the overall turnover\"; company makes more than 50 APIs.","Formulation mix target: MD expects formulations to rise from 20%–24% of revenue now to 45%–50% over the next 3 to 4 years.","Regulatory approvals: Europe 12 CEPs filed, 8 approved; South Korea total 17 products; Egypt 3 registered, 10 more planned; 17 products identified for Brazil submission."],"profit":["Mix held EBITDA: CFO said EBITDA margin held at 18.2% despite a gross margin dip, as higher-margin export and formulation sales offset weaker domestic API.","Balance sheet: Net worth about Rs 557 crores; debt to equity about 0.5x; India Ratings upgraded long-term rating to IND A Stable.","Cash flow: CFO expects operating cash flow above Rs 30 crores for the year; receivable days improved from 145 to 135.","Integration margin gain: MD expects 60%–70% of oncology products to be backward integrated in 2 to 3 years, with a 10%–15% margin increase.","Talent retention: Senior hires retained through incentives and ESOPs; MD said \"All these incentives are on performance basis.\""],"pipeline":["Peptide plant: 250 kg per annum peptide plant under construction; 6 peptide APIs ready at pilot scale; customer approvals take 6 to 10 months after commercial start.","Oncology API plant: For CDMO and own brand; MD expects about 200 crores revenue within 2 to 3 years of operation.","Nashik formulation plant: Facility for oral solid dosage, nasal, oral oncology, nutraceutical and ointment products.","R&D build-out: Over 80 professionals in the new R&D centre with room for about 120; R&D spend raised to about 2.5%–3%; API, peptide, oncology and biotech sections.","Formulation exports: Dossiers submitted in Africa, Myanmar, Philippines, Vietnam and Latin America; MD expects business \"in next 2 years\".","Leadership hires: COO with more than 40 years' experience, plus heads for institutional sales, global API, R&D, ROW formulations and oncology sales."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 growth and margin","s":"10%–15% revenue and profit growth, 18%–20% margin; CFO: \"we are on track to achieve this\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY29 target","s":"Rs 900 to 1,000 crore revenue, EBITDA margin 18% to 21%","t":"FY29","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 capex","s":"Rs 65 to Rs 80 crores","t":"FY2027","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ongoing capex","s":"\"Every year we'll be infusing around 50 to 60 crores\"","t":"Per year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Operating cash flow","s":"\"More than Rs 30 crores\"","t":"\"for the financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Peptide plant","s":"Completion, installed capacity 250 kgs per annum","t":"\"by 31st March 2027\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Peptide revenue","s":"\"First year to be around 70 to 100 crores\"; next year \"around 300 crores\" cumulative","t":"\"'28-'29\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Oncology API plant","s":"Commencement; about 200 crores revenue in 2 to 3 years","t":"\"2nd quarter of financial year 2028\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Nashik formulation plant","s":"Expected operational","t":"\"first quarter of 20 FY28\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"GenRx unit revenue","s":"Around 200 to 250 crores","t":"\"next 2 to 3 years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Formulation share","s":"45% to 50% of revenue","t":"\"next 3 to 4 years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 margins","s":"\"Pretty confident of maintaining this gross margin and EBITDA margins\"","t":"Remaining fiscal","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"MD said the Gulf dispute, rising container freight and higher interest rates make the market \"unpredictable right now\"; API faces continuing price and demand pressure.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026). Guidance p2, p5; K1 p1–2; K2 p4, p8; K3 p1, p5, p13; K4 p1, p4; K5 p5, p9; R1 p1–2; R2 p2, p11; R3 p10; R4 p11; R5 p1; F1 p2, p10; F2 p3; F3 p8; F4 p7; F5 p13; P1 p1, p6; P2 p4, p12; P3 p2; P4 p2, p6; P5 p2, p11; P6 p2; C1 p5; C2 p2; C3 p5, p11; C4 p12; C5 p8; C6 p1; C7 p6; C8 p1–2, p4; C9 p2; C10 p4; C11 p11; C12 p10; M1–M6 p2; M7 p2–3; M8 p3; M9–M13 p2; M14 p1; M15–M16 p3; M17 p8; M18 p5; M19 p2; Risks p5, p9."},
  {"name":"Bajaj Finance Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"19 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 AUM growth raised to \"between 26%, 27%\"; medium-term 23%–25% unchanged; long-term 20%–25% profit growth and 19%–21% ROE maintained.","metrics":[{"m":"PBT","v":"INR8,537 crores, +29% YoY"},{"m":"PBT before macro overlay","v":"INR8,874 crores, +34% YoY"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR6,367 crores, +29% YoY"},{"m":"PAT before macro overlay","v":"INR6,619 crores, +34% YoY"},{"m":"ROA / ROE","v":"4.6% / 20.6%"},{"m":"Opex to total income","v":"33%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 non-interest income","v":"+20% vs 16%–18% guidance"},{"m":"AUM","v":"INR5,84,624 crores, +26.5% YoY"},{"m":"Net AUM addition","v":"Close to INR38,000 crores in the quarter"},{"m":"New loans booked","v":"13.5 million, +11% YoY"},{"m":"New customers","v":"4.42 million"},{"m":"Customer franchise","v":"Just below 129 million; cross-sell franchise 82 million"},{"m":"Cost of funds","v":"7.31%, down 9 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Deposits share of book","v":"14%"},{"m":"Loan loss to average AUF","v":"1.56% vs 1.88% last year; 1.32% ex-overlay"},{"m":"GNPA / NNPA","v":"1.06% / 0.42%"},{"m":"PCR","v":"60%"},{"m":"Gold loan book","v":"Just above INR24,000 crores; 4.1% of balance sheet"},{"m":"Gold loan branches","v":"2,022; 320-odd added in the quarter"},{"m":"Liquidity buffer","v":"Just below INR20,000 crores"}],"takeaways":["FY27 growth upgraded: VCMD expects 26%–27% growth for FY27 after two quarters of INR38,000–39,000 crores book addition; he confirmed this includes drought-related pruning.","Capital raise for growth: Board approved INR11,700 crores QIP and INR5,800 crores preferential warrants to Bajaj Finserv; VCMD said it is \"principally for growth\"; leverage corridor lowered from 5.5–6 to 5–5.5.","Overlay for resilience: Macro overlay stated as INR337 crores in opening remarks and INR327 crores in Q&A; CFO cited El Nino, 87% monsoon and $100–110 oil; \"not because we see stress building\".","Gold short-tenor exit: Stopped short-tenor gold schemes since August; gold Stage 2 rose about INR1,100 crores, of which INR550 crores already returned to Stage 1; VCMD sees no credit cost impact.","Revolver loan contingency: If RBI bans revolvers, VCMD expects \"transient impact on AUM and a marginal compression in NIM and profitability\"; new term loan variants launching 1 November."],"revenue":["Segment growth: Urban consumer finance 43%, rural consumer finance 63%, MFI 54%, CV and tractor 89%, LAS 42%; MSME 3%; captive book down to INR2,400 odd crores.","Fee income ahead: H1 non-interest income grew 20% against 16%–18% guidance, due to stronger B2B growth; VCMD said 15%–18% fee growth is sustainable longer term.","Loan volumes: New loans 13.5 million, up 11%, with year-on-year noise as the festive season came later this year.","Gold loan profile: Book just above INR24,000 crores, 4.1% of balance sheet; ticket size INR2.2–2.5 lakhs unchanged; LTV 75% inclusive of interest; 99% bullet repayment.","Customer franchise: Added 4.42 million new customers; franchise just below 129 million; cross-sell franchise 82 million."],"profit":["NIM moderating: Q2 NIM 10–12 bps below Q1; CFO now carries \"a marginal headwind\" to the 10–15 bps compression guided; two more rate increases baked in.","Funding: Cost of funds 7.31%, down 9 bps, helped by interest rate hedging; deposits 14% of the book, rates raised in Q2; liquidity buffer just below INR20,000 crores.","Credit cost: Loan loss to average AUF 1.56% versus 1.88%; 1.32% excluding overlay; GNPA 1.06%, NNPA 0.42%; CFO attributed the Q2 GNPA rise to more days in the quarter.","Provision buffers: CFO said macro provision is about INR226 crores, management overlay INR512 crores, and LGD flooring carries INR350–500 crores additional provision.","Opex efficiency: Opex to total income 33% despite investment in gold loan branches, MFI branches and technology."],"pipeline":["Gold branch rollout: Added 320-odd gold loan branches in the quarter, taking the total to 2,022.","FinAI scale-up: AI unit of 328 people; AI bots handle 75% of DIY customer service volume; 129 agents and 65 agentic applications live.","New customer risk model: First model in testing on 500,000 B2B accounts; modelled 15%–20% better risk outcomes; B2C model to launch only if B2B performance holds.","Leverage tightening: 3PL share of origination cut from 14% to 7%; pruning business, including MFI, in drought-declared districts based on 3MOB signals.","BHFL affordable loans: Monthly disbursement over INR500 crores; BFL holds 87% of BHFL and intends to remain a 75% holder, with dilution possible within three years."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 AUM growth","s":"\"Between 26%, 27% growth\" on a full-year basis","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Medium-term growth","s":"23% to 25%, \"I am not changing\"","t":"Medium term","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"New loans","s":"60 million to 62 million","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"New customers","s":"18 million to 20 million","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gold loan branches","s":"\"Close to 2,800-odd standalone gold loan branches\"","t":"\"ending the year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Opex to NTI","s":"25 bps to 40 bps improvement","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"NIM compression","s":"10–15 bps guided, plus \"a marginal headwind to that number\"","t":"Current year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Credit cost","s":"\"Same corridor… A similar corridor\"","t":"Rest of the year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"3PL origination","s":"Bring down from 7% to 3.5%","t":"\"in the six months' time\"; \"by Feb\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MSME growth","s":"\"A double-digit kind of growth momentum\"","t":"\"as we get into second half\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Agentic AI","s":"118 agentic applications and 600 agents","t":"\"for the year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"BHFL affordable disbursement","s":"INR800 crores a month exiting the year; INR1,300–1,400 crores a month","t":"\"by third quarter of next year\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"VCMD said drought-declared districts, with Maharashtra most impacted, are being tracked, and \"we remain in a VUCA world, so we'll take quarter at a time.\"","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 19 Oct 2026). Guidance p9, p12, p15; K1 p1, p9, p16; K2 p2, p12–13; K3 p1–2, p7–8; K4 p2, p4; K5 p13; R1 p1; R2 p10; R3 p1; R4 p1, p4, p14; R5 p1–2; F1 p9–10; F2 p2, p9; F3 p2, p6; F4 p8; F5 p2; P1 p1; P2 p2–3; P3 p3; P4 p6, p15; P5 p3, p12; C1 p9; C2 p9, p12; C3 p1–2; C4 p2; C5 p1; C6 p2; C7 p10; C8 p11; C9 p6; C10 p1; C11 p3; C12 p12; M1–M6 p1; M7 p10; M8–M10 p1; M11–M14 p2; M15–M17 p2; M18–M19 p1; M20 p2; Risks p2, p16."},
  {"name":"Elecon Engineering Company Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"H2 FY27 consolidated revenue growth \"mid-double-digit… somewhere around 15%\" (CFO), versus 11.1% in H1. Minimum EBITDA margin at FY26's level. INR5,000 crore top line by FY30 reiterated.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR639 crore, +10.5% YoY, +22.7% QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR148 crores, +17.7% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"23.1%, +140 bps"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR96 crores, +9.8%; PAT margin 15.1%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"+11.1% YoY vs adjusted H1 FY26"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"INR257 crores, +11.4% adjusted; margin 22.2% vs 22.1%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR167 crores; adjusted PAT +6.5%"},{"m":"Gear revenue","v":"INR488 crores, +10.6% YoY; approximately 76% of revenue"},{"m":"Gear EBIT","v":"INR108 crores; margin 22.1%, +290 bps"},{"m":"H1 FY27 gear","v":"Revenue INR903 crores, +13.1%; EBIT INR182 crores, margin 20.2%"},{"m":"Gear order intake","v":"INR493 crores, stable YoY; H1 INR1,063 crores, +8.8%"},{"m":"Gear order book (30 Sep)","v":"INR1,056 crores, +37% YoY"},{"m":"MHE revenue","v":"INR151 crores, +10.4% YoY; EBIT margin about 20.2%"},{"m":"MHE order intake","v":"INR253 crores, +32.5%; overseas INR68 crores; H1 INR438 crores"},{"m":"MHE order book (30 Sep)","v":"INR579 crores, +27.3% YoY"},{"m":"Overseas revenue","v":"INR159 crores, +30.6% YoY; H1 INR309 crores, +26.2%"},{"m":"Export order inflow","v":"INR230 crores vs INR172 crore; H1 INR420 vs INR291 crore"},{"m":"International order book","v":"INR326 crores"},{"m":"Net cash and working capital","v":"Net cash approximately INR700 crores; cycle 89 days from 98"},{"m":"Interim dividend","v":"INR0.50 per equity share of face value INR1"}],"takeaways":["H2 guide above H1 pace: The CFO guided around 15% consolidated growth for H2, based on improved execution in recent months. He still flagged macro, geopolitical and inflation risks, including rupee depreciation.","Margin guide kept conservative: The CFO said H1 EBITDA margin of 22.2% should hold. \"Out of the abundant caution\", the guide is only FY26's level, because CT business (about 50% of revenue) lacks back-to-back pricing.","Domestic delay, orders firm: The CMD blamed the slower domestic execution on steel price volatility and a weak monsoon. He said \"the orders are all firm and fixed\". Momentum is expected after Diwali, \"especially till March\".","FY30 targets reaffirmed: The CMD said \"we will reach 50% of our turnover from overseas markets\" by FY30. He is \"quite confident of achieving our INR5,000 crore top line\", including inorganic growth.","Defense order deferred: The P-17 Bravo final RFP is expected in Q2 or Q3 of next financial year. As follow-on to P-17 Alpha, \"we will be the single bidder\". The award timeline in the transcript is garbled."],"revenue":["Overseas drives gear growth: Gear growth was led by overseas markets. Demand is moving from standard gearboxes to customised and condition-monitoring solutions, including data center cooling towers and oil and gas.","Export geographies: Overseas orders rose 35%, mainly from the Middle East and the Americas. The CMD said the US is \"just got the tip of the iceberg\". Europe and the UK are improving \"only slight right now\".","MHE execution recovery: MHE execution improved after project clearances in Q2. The two delayed power orders from Q1 are now being executed, with \"good amount of invoicing\" in Q2.","Gear product mix: Gear revenue is about 53% catalog and 47% engineered. The CFO said highly engineered solutions within that have \"improved a lot\".","MHE international entry: MHE won orders worth approximately $9 million overseas in H1. The CMD said \"this is just the beginning\", with large inquiries coming from abroad."],"profit":["Gear margin drivers: The CFO cited price hikes, a better product mix and export orders carrying higher margins. Export orders slipped from Q1 into Q2, which concentrated the margin in Q2.","MHE margin dip reasons: The CFO cited order quality, early-stage scale-up after Q2 clearances, and weaker raw material price-locking on project business. Gear-style cost pass-on \"has not happened in MHE\".","Steel volatility managed: The CFO said Q1's abnormal raw material price rise did not recur in Q2. The advantage of peers holding low-cost inventory has \"softened\" and the market has normalised.","Balance sheet and rating: The long-term rating outlook was upgraded from AA Stable to AA Positive. The lead banker released collateral on working capital limits. There are no conventional borrowings; debt is mainly lease liabilities.","Capacity utilisation: The CFO said capacity utilisation is \"almost 60%\" on a consistent basis. Constrained machines are being debottlenecked and reassessed quarter on quarter."],"pipeline":["Inorganic growth abroad: The CMD said the company is looking at small to medium-sized companies in America and Europe for acquisition or strategic tie-ups. Size \"will depend upon what opportunities we get\".","Three-year capex program: The CFO said the three-year capital expenditure program \"remains on track\".","Thermal power opportunity: The CMD said thermal power plants bring business in both material handling and gears, with new applications that have \"great potential\".","High-speed gearboxes: The CMD said high-speed gearboxes for industrial and oil and gas uses have potential, with \"a reasonable good reference list\"; the company needs to \"catch more momentum\".","MHE competitive landscape: MHE management said TRF has closed its material handling operations. L&T and Tical focus on EPC projects and need OEMs like Elecon. Takraf and Ammon compete in the private sector.","Market growth estimate: The CMD cited industry research estimating India's heavy-duty industrial gearbox market will grow at approximately 8% CAGR through FY29."],"commitments":[{"c":"H2 revenue growth","s":"Consolidated \"mid-double-digit… somewhere around 15%\"","t":"H2 FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EBITDA margin floor","s":"\"Minimum EBITDA margin at last year's level\"; CFO expects to sustain H1's 22.2%","t":"Remaining part of FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Three-year capex","s":"Committed to capital expenditure program; \"remains on track\"","t":"Three years","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MHE EBIT margin","s":"22% to 24% guidance \"stays committed\"; improvement expected with scale","t":"Q3, Q4; \"throughout the year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gear EBIT margin","s":"\"We will maintain 20% to 22% EBIT margin for gear\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"P-17 Bravo RFP","s":"Final offer RFP expected; single bidder; execution two to three years","t":"\"Q2 or Q3 of the next financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Overseas share target","s":"50% of turnover from overseas markets","t":"By FY30","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Revenue target","s":"INR5,000 crore top line, including possible inorganic growth","t":"By FY30","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Domestic pickup","s":"Momentum expected after Diwali; \"sizable amount before March\"","t":"\"especially till March\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Overseas acquisitions","s":"Small to medium companies in America and Europe; \"pursue in a very strong way\"","t":"\"in the near future\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 order inflow","s":"CMD declined to quantify: \"it is difficult to mention\"","t":"H2","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The CMD cited \"global uncertainty, geopolitical developments, and raw material volatility\", plus \"delays in project execution clearances\" and an \"uneven macroeconomic environment\".","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026). Guidance p4–5, p10, p12; K1 p4–5, p12; K2 p5; K3 p4; K4 p10; K5 p6–7; R1 p9–10; R2 p11; R3 p2, p6; R4 p7; R5 p1, p10; F1 p7; F2 p15; F3 p8–9; F4 p2–3; F5 p8; P1 p10, p14; P2 p3; P3 p14; P4 p14; P5 p13; P6 p1; C1 p3–5, p12; C2 p4–5; C3 p3; C4 p6, p15; C5 p7; C6 p6–7; C7 p10; C8 p10; C9 p4; C10 p14; C11 p11; M1–M4 p2–3; M5–M7 p3; M8 p2–3; M9–M16 p3; M13 also p15; M17–M18 p12; M19–M20 p3; Risks p1–2."},
  {"name":"Jio Financial Services Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated total income ex-dividend","v":"INR 1,699 crores, +132% YoY, +14% QoQ"},{"m":"Pre-provisioning operating profit","v":"INR 574 crores, +85% YoY, +14% QoQ"},{"m":"PBT ex-dividend","v":"INR 489 crores, +51% YoY, around +6% QoQ"},{"m":"Reported PAT","v":"INR 340 crores vs INR 695 crores in Q2 FY26"},{"m":"H1 FY27 total income ex-dividend","v":"INR 3,195 crores, +136% YoY; H1 PAT INR 1,171 crores"},{"m":"Jio Credit gross AUM","v":"INR 37,320 crores, +154% YoY; INR 30,667 crores in Q1"},{"m":"Jio Credit disbursements","v":"INR 13,135 crores, +98% YoY; INR 11,252 crores in Q1"},{"m":"Jio Credit NII","v":"INR 302 crores, +116% YoY, +17% QoQ"},{"m":"Jio Credit PAT","v":"INR 120 crores, +140% YoY, +24% QoQ"},{"m":"Jio Credit AUM mix","v":"Mortgages 44.5%, corporate and SME 44.2%, LAS 11.3%"},{"m":"Jio Credit cost of borrowing","v":"7.08% ex-TREPS vs 7.07% in Q1"},{"m":"Jio Credit capital adequacy","v":"18.5%; equity INR 7,379 crores"},{"m":"Jio Payments Bank deposits","v":"INR 643 crores, +53% YoY; 40 lakh CASA customers"},{"m":"Jio Payment Solutions TPV","v":"INR 14,682 crores; net processing margin 14 bps"},{"m":"Jio BlackRock AMC AUM","v":"INR 22,746 crores, +57% YoY, +24% QoQ"},{"m":"Reinsurance gross written premium","v":"INR 244 crores; first six months INR 510 crores"},{"m":"Insurance broking","v":"Premium facilitated INR 346 crores; fee income INR 54 crores, +46%"},{"m":"JioFinance users","v":"3.2 crores unique users; about 80 lakhs average MAU"},{"m":"Consolidated balance sheet","v":"Net worth close to INR 1.3 lakh crores; assets INR 1.81 lakh crores"},{"m":"Standalone PAT","v":"INR 575 crores, +26% YoY, +447% QoQ"}],"takeaways":["Bank of America deal pending: Bank of America agreed to acquire a 49.9% stake in Jio Credit, bringing around INR 18,000 crores of growth capital. A first tranche of around INR 9,500 crores is subject to regulatory approvals.","Dividend timing distorts comparisons: The RIL-linked dividend was received in Q2 last year but in Q1 this year. Management reported growth excluding it; reported PAT was INR 340 crores versus INR 695 crores in Q2 FY26.","RSHL consolidation effect: RSHL is fully consolidated line-by-line from April 29, 2026. The CFO said this adds to total income and PPOP \"without impacting our profit after tax\".","Deliberate TPV pullback: Payment volume fell QoQ because of a \"strategic pivot towards margin-accretive merchant segments\". The company \"chose to let go of certain high-volume, low-margin engagements\".","Insurance distribution norms: The CFO said the IRDAI draft rules are \"a significant regulatory shift\" that \"may lead to consolidation\". The company will \"recalibrate our business strategy as appropriate based on the final regulations\"."],"revenue":["Lending growth fully organic: The CTO said Jio Credit AUM growth was \"driven entirely by robust organic originations\". Q2 disbursements were 26.1% mortgages, 20% loans against securities and 53.9% corporate and SME.","Income drivers: The CFO attributed interest income growth to lending traction and higher treasury scale. Fee income nearly doubled from payments and insurance broking.","Reinsurance moderation: Reinsurance GWP fell sequentially as primary insurers recalibrated crop insurance exposure \"to align with current seasonal and environmental factors\".","App engagement mixed: Average monthly active users were about 80 lakhs, reflecting \"a temporary moderation\" in price-sensitive transactions like digital gold. About 36,000 products were purchased daily.","AMC reach: 18.6% of Jio BlackRock retail customers are new to mutual funds, and over 40% of retail AUM comes from beyond the top 30 cities. The AMC has over 15,000 distributors."],"profit":["Jio Credit funding mix: Bank loans are around 50% of borrowings, down from 60% in Q2 FY26, and lower-cost TREPS borrowing was introduced. Over 70% of AUM is floating-rate.","Payments margin expansion: Net processing margin rose to 14 bps, from 9 bps in Q2 FY26 and 12 bps in Q1 FY27, from customer mix changes and portfolio rationalisation.","Gestation losses in JVs: The share of associates and JVs was a loss of INR 44 crores. This reflects gestation costs at the BlackRock ventures and the general insurance JV with Allianz.","Staff cost and treasury: Staff expenses fell sequentially because FY26 increments and bonuses were paid in Q1 FY27. Net gain on fair value changes was INR 165 crores versus INR 210 crores in Q1 FY27.","Standalone capital raise: Standalone net worth rose to INR 35,521 crores from INR 29,305 crores in March. This was \"primarily due to\" the second tranche of INR 5,934 crores from promoter preferential warrants."],"pipeline":["New app features: Launched JioFinance+, which shares platform commission with customers and is complimentary for 365 days. Also launched JioFinance360, a Financial Health Score across five pillars.","Payments bank approvals: Jio Payments Bank received approvals for a 3-in-1 savings account and to act as banker to issue for IPOs. It now operates at 20 highway toll plazas and two barrierless plazas.","Insurance JVs: Jio Allianz General Insurance is \"in advanced stages for securing regulatory approvals\". Work on a life insurance JV with Allianz continues under a non-binding agreement.","Investment products: Jio BlackRock Broking launched with introductory zero brokerage. The maiden SIF reached approximately INR 480 crores in three months. IFSCA approved a GIFT City global equity fund.","AI and distribution: The app runs 50 AI agents and 10 ML models; personalisation lifted conversion 59%. The company has 26 Jio Credit offices in 19 cities and over 633,000 banking touchpoints."],"commitments":[{"c":"Bank of America stake","s":"49.9% of Jio Credit; around INR 18,000 crores growth capital; \"subject to regulatory approvals\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"First BofA tranche","s":"Around INR 9,500 crores into Jio Credit, subject to approvals","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Global equity fund launch","s":"Jio BlackRock Advantage World Equity Fund from GIFT City","t":"\"during the current quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"General insurance JV","s":"Jio Allianz General Insurance to commence operations; \"advanced stages\" of approvals","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Life insurance JV","s":"Strategize launch with Allianz under non-binding agreement","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Insurance broking strategy","s":"\"Will recalibrate our business strategy\" based on final IRDAI regulations","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Payments bank new streams","s":"Launch 3-in-1 savings account; banker to issue for IPOs","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"JioFinance+ offer","s":"Complimentary introductory membership","t":"First 365 days","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The CFO cited a \"volatile macro environment\" for treasury. Seasonal and environmental factors in crop insurance moderated reinsurance volume.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026; prepared remarks only, no Q&A in transcript). K1 p3, p5; K2 p2, p9; K3 p2, p8; K4 p1, p5; K5 p8; R1 p4; R2 p8; R3 p2, p7; R4 p6; R5 p7; F1 p4–5; F2 p6; F3 p9; F4 p8; F5 p9; P1 p2, p6; P2 p5; P3 p7; P4 p3, p7; P5 p3–4, p6; C1 p3; C2 p5, p10; C3 p7; C4 p7; C5 p7; C6 p8; C7 p5; C8 p6; M1 p2, p8; M2 p2, p9; M3 p2, p9; M4 p9; M5 p2; M6 p4; M7 p4; M8 p5, p9; M9 p5, p10; M10 p4; M11 p5; M12 p5; M13 p5; M14 p5–6; M15 p1–2; M16 p7; M17 p7; M18 p2, p6; M19 p9; M20 p9; Risks p2, p8."},
  {"name":"Onward Technologies Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Positive","guidance":"FY27 outlook raised: revenue growth 15%–18% and EBITDA margin 13.8%–14.3%, up from an earlier \"double-digit EBITDA\". The MD prefers to \"remain prudent\" on further upside.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue","v":"INR170 crores, +22.4% YoY, +13.2% QoQ"},{"m":"Total income","v":"INR171.2 crores"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR26.5 crores, +33.2% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"15.6%, all-time high"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR16.8 crores, +41.2% YoY"},{"m":"Constant currency growth","v":"13.5% QoQ, 17.7% YoY"},{"m":"$1 million+ customers","v":"18, from 16"},{"m":"Contract renewal value","v":"Approximately INR44.5 crores, up over 70%"},{"m":"DSO","v":"About 80 days; target 65 to 70 days"},{"m":"Delivery mix","v":"10% onsite, 90% offshore"},{"m":"Employee base","v":"Almost 3,000 plus people"},{"m":"Customer count","v":"70 plus customers"},{"m":"In-house recruiters","v":"90 plus"}],"takeaways":["Guidance upgraded, upside not committed: The MD raised FY27 guidance on H1 execution and North America visibility. Asked about more upside: \"We obviously prefer to remain prudent and execute rather than get ahead of ourselves.\"","Execution is the constraint: The MD said \"revenue is not the challenge… execution is the challenge\". Limits are ready capacity in people and infrastructure, and capability to run large programs.","Large contract renewal: A 3-year digital engineering renewal worth approximately INR44.5 crores was won with a global construction and mining equipment leader. Contract value rose over 70%, and the full contract was awarded upfront instead of periodic POs.","UK R&D credit in Q2: A UK R&D credit for FY26 was booked in Q2 after September approval, net against employee expenses. The MD said it \"happens once a year\". An analyst cited INR2.9 crores; management did not confirm.","Visa rules factored in: The MD is \"definitely a bit cautious\" about new US regulation and hiring locally. He said guidance is given \"post the new regulatory… external environment\"."],"revenue":["Growth quality drivers: The MD cited deeper engagement with strategic customers, higher utilisation, productivity and offshore execution. The North America ramp-up began in Q4 last year with larger programs: \"it's not a one-off thing\".","Customer mining: Customers billing $1 million annually rose from 16 to 18 among 70+ customers. The MD's vision is close to $10 million per year per customer.","Vertical outlook: Rail transportation has grown for many quarters, and automotive \"bounced back\" after European furloughs. The MD expects T&M to \"grow much faster\", with HCLS ramp-up possibly faster than other verticals.","Demand areas: The MD sees opportunities in digital engineering, AI-led transformation and data center infrastructure engineering, including power management and cooling. All customers are in the US and Europe; \"we have no India customers\".","AI deflation risk: The MD said customers want AI efficiency but \"it's not reducing the workload\" because the work is engineering-heavy. Guidance includes \"some impact from that\"."],"profit":["Margin expansion drivers: The MD cited higher offshore utilisation, more offshore leverage as customers shifted work offshore before the new US policy, and delivery discipline focused on few customers.","DSO stretched: DSO rose to about 80 days because of fast ramp-up, delayed POs and customer payments. The MD targets 65 to 70 days, normalising \"in the next two quarters\".","Depreciation rise: Depreciation rose because of a purchased office in Bombay and hardware for new design centres.","Structural margin levers: The MD said utilisation, offshore delivery, productivity and account mining are structural, with \"a lot of opportunity\" over time. The delivery mix is 10% onsite and 90% offshore.","Cash for investment: The MD said \"we have substantial cash reserves\" to invest proactively behind customer engagements."],"pipeline":["Hiring plan: The MD expects Q3 hiring to be \"a bit slow\" because of holidays, focused on subject matter experts. He expects another few hundred net additions in Q4 and Q1. There are about 90 in-house recruiters and no fresher hiring.","Capacity build-out: A fourth design centre opened in Pune and is expected to be full by end November or December. A lease for more Pune seats is signed; delivery centres are in Pune, Chennai, Bangalore and Hyderabad.","Chennai AI lab: The Chennai AI lab will train staff, showcase work and serve customers testing products. The MD expects it running \"by Q4 or early 2027\", with first benefit in 2027.","Large RFP pipeline: The MD said the company is bidding for \"a number of large RFPs\" with existing customers, especially in AI data center work. Rail investment includes attending InnoTrans."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 revenue growth","s":"15% to 18%; \"increased our FY27 outlook\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 EBITDA margin","s":"13.8% to 14.3%; revised from \"double-digit EBITDA\"","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"DSO normalisation","s":"From about 80 days to 65 to 70 days","t":"\"next two quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Chennai AI lab","s":"Up and running","t":"\"by Q4 or early 2027\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q4 hiring","s":"\"Another few hundred people\", net","t":"Q4; Q1","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Pune design centre","s":"Fourth centre \"expected to get full\"; lease signed for more seats","t":"End of November, December","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Data center opportunities","s":"\"Will be very happy to share more\"","t":"\"subsequent quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"T&M vertical growth","s":"\"Will continue to grow and grow much faster\"","t":"\"next couple of quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Customer revenue aspiration","s":"Each customer close to $10 million per year","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Segment constant currency data","s":"Team to share segment-wise constant currency growth","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The MD cited a more competitive hiring market, US regulatory changes affecting onsite delivery, and external factors that \"can delay the projects\".","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026). Guidance p1, p5, p10; K1 p2, p5; K2 p5–6; K3 p1; K4 p4, p7; K5 p3; R1 p1, p7; R2 p1, p8, p12; R3 p8–9; R4 p1, p3; R5 p9; F1 p4; F2 p3, p13; F3 p14; F4 p10, p12; F5 p6; P1 p6, p13; P2 p1, p6; P3 p5; P4 p1, p8; C1 p1; C2 p1, p10; C3 p13; C4 p5; C5 p6; C6 p6; C7 p1; C8 p9; C9 p12; C10 p5; M1–M5 p1; M6 p5, p11; M7–M8 p1; M9 p3, p13; M10 p10; M11 p3; M12 p8; M13 p13; Risks p3, p6, p13."},
  {"name":"Orient Electric Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"15 Oct 2026","tone":"Positive","guidance":"Revenue CAGR guidance hinted up: \"more than 15%… slightly more aggressive number\", not formalised (H1 growth 24.3%). Long-run gross margin about 32%; double-digit EBITDA aspiration in \"four to six quarters\".","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue","v":"INR879 crores, +25.2% YoY"},{"m":"Gross margin","v":"29%"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR57 crores, +49.7% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"6.5%, +106 bps YoY"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR27 crores, more than doubled"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR1,829 crores, +24.3% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 margins","v":"Gross 29.4%; EBITDA 6.7%, +104 bps; EBITDA +46.9%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR59 crores, close to 100% growth"},{"m":"ECD revenue","v":"INR550 crores, +24.9% YoY"},{"m":"Lighting and switchgear revenue","v":"INR329 crores, +25.7% YoY"},{"m":"BLDC fans growth","v":"Approximately 26% YoY; almost 30% of ceiling fans"},{"m":"New products share of fans","v":"More than 30% of fan revenue"},{"m":"Premium share of fans","v":"Approximately 40%"},{"m":"Quick commerce","v":"About 15% of digital revenue; grew almost 100%"},{"m":"Price increases","v":"3% to 6% in Q2; about 3% in October"},{"m":"Project Sanchay savings","v":"About INR13 crores in Q2; about INR23 crores in H1"},{"m":"Working capital","v":"34 days"},{"m":"R&D spend","v":"About 0.8% to 0.9% of turnover"},{"m":"Hyderabad plant utilisation","v":"About 40% on single shift"},{"m":"BLDC price index","v":"1.11 vs industry 1, from 1.04"}],"takeaways":["Growth guidance upgrade hinted: Against the 15%+ CAGR guide and 24.3% H1 growth, the MD said \"we would be more than 15% of CAGR. How much more? We don't give a future…\" He added \"slightly more aggressive number than what we had given earlier\".","Gross margin hit by inflation: The MD confirmed \"a sharp drop of over 250 basis points\" in gross margin over two quarters. He said this was inflation outpacing price hikes, not under-pricing.","Price hikes cover most inflation: Price hikes of 3% to 6% were taken in Q2, plus about 3% in October in some categories. Coverage is about 80% in fans, close to 90% in appliances and closer to 100% in wires.","Double-digit EBITDA timing hedged: The MD said that without \"this unprecedented inflation\", he \"would have had a very different confident timeline\". He expects trending \"far closer\" to double digits \"in the next four to six quarters, at least in H2 onwards\".","No channel upstocking: The MD said price hikes are \"not leading to any abnormal primary upstocking\". He said \"there is no abnormal one-off\", with festive stocking split between September and October."],"revenue":["Volume and price both: The MD said each category had double-digit volume growth, not just price. In all categories, both volume and value grew.","Fans premiumisation: New products were more than 30% of fan revenue, with a 90%+ success rate. Premium was about 40% of the fan mix, and BLDC was almost 30% of ceiling fans vs about 17%–18% for the industry.","Lighting and switchgear mix: Consumer lighting grew double digits and professional lighting high single digits. High-value lumens are 66% of the lumen mix, and B2B is about 25% of lighting.","Channel mix: E-commerce grew high double digits. Quick commerce is about 15% of digital revenue and grew almost 100%. B2B grew slower than B2C, with less ability to pass on pricing.","Wires selective rollout: Wires more than doubled YoY but are not launched pan-India; focus is North, East and parts of South. The MD said 45% of fan-selling outlets sell wires."],"profit":["EBITDA expansion drivers: EBITDA margin rose 106 bps despite gross margin pressure, from operating leverage and Project Sanchay cost savings of about INR13 crores in Q2 and INR23 crores in H1.","Working capital rise: Working capital is at 34 days because the move from master distributors to direct-to-market raised receivables. The MD expects about 20 days \"in the next four or six quarters\" through channel financing.","DTM margin accretive: The MD said direct-to-market markets are margin accretive, with market share gains, faster BLDC growth and new products contributing 500 to 600 bps more.","Hyderabad plant utilisation: The Hyderabad plant is at about 40% single-shift utilisation, about 75% PPW. The MD expects about 20 to 30 bps margin benefit at steady state.","Commodity inflation breadth: The MD said steel moved from about 65 to about 80 plus, copper is \"touching a new high every two weeks\" and aluminium is volatile. Unorganised players gain share as prices rise."],"pipeline":["DTM expansion: Direct-to-market expanded to Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand, and service was taken over in Punjab and Nagpur. The MD said the model will stay \"a balanced approach\" with master distributors.","New launches: Launched NIXA Shield 6kA MCB in a phased rollout (Delhi, UP West, more in coming months), plus BLDC fans: Aerosilent, Ecotech Volt, Aero O2 and Harrier gesture-control fan.","R&D and insight programs: R&D spend is about 0.8% to 0.9% of turnover. Project Samvad uses AI for consumer insights and faster service. About 70% to 80% of BLDC PCBs are made in-house.","Distribution reach: About 3,000 to 4,000 outlets were added on a fan base of about 1 lakh plus direct and indirect outlets."],"commitments":[{"c":"Revenue CAGR","s":"\"More than 15% of CAGR\"; \"slightly more aggressive number than what we had given earlier\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Double-digit EBITDA margin","s":"Trend \"far closer\" to double digits","t":"\"next four to six quarters, at least in H2 onwards\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gross margin","s":"\"My guidance in the long run stays at about 32%\"","t":"Long run","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Working capital days","s":"From 34 days to about 20 days via channel financing","t":"\"next four or six quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Project Sanchay savings","s":"Exceed last year's close to INR70 crores; accruals mostly in H2","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"October price hike","s":"About 3% in some categories (fans 3rd of October; lighting about 2%)","t":"October","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Hyderabad margin benefit","s":"About 20 bps to 30 bps at steady state","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MCB geographic rollout","s":"UP and rest of places","t":"\"next few months\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Wires expansion","s":"Expand into white-space states","t":"\"next six to eight quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 EBITDA seasonality","s":"\"Q3, Q4 being much better in terms of EBITDA percentages\"","t":"H2","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"The MD cited persistent copper, aluminium, steel and plastic inflation, crude price swings, geopolitical supply chain uncertainty and unorganised players gaining share.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 15 Oct 2026). Guidance p5, p7; K1 p6–7; K2 p12; K3 p1, p8; K4 p5; K5 p3; R1 p6–7, p9; R2 p2–3; R3 p1, p9; R4 p2, p9; R5 p1, p11; F1 p2; F2 p5–6; F3 p8–9; F4 p10–11; F5 p9–10; P1 p2, p8; P2 p4–5, p11; P3 p4, p11; P4 p9; C1 p7; C2 p5; C3 p7; C4 p6; C5 p6; C6 p1, p7–8; C7 p11; C8 p5; C9 p11; C10 p5; M1–M5 p2; M6–M8 p2; M9–M10 p1; M11 p2–3; M12–M13 p2; M14 p2; M15 p1; M16 p2; M17 p2; M18 p4; M19 p10; M20 p12; Risks p1, p9–10."},
  {"name":"ICICI Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"17 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"NIM was 4.18% in Q2 FY27; management expects further moderation in Q3 and a “pretty healthy pickup from Q4 onwards.” Management declined to quantify the FCNR impact separately.","metrics":[{"m":"Profit before tax excluding treasury","v":"INR199.54 billion; grew 23.4% year-on-year"},{"m":"Core operating profit","v":"INR208.05 billion; increased 21.8% year-on-year"},{"m":"Core operating profit excluding subsidiary dividends","v":"INR203.42 billion; increased 25% year-on-year"},{"m":"Profit after tax","v":"INR148.43 billion; grew 20.1% year-on-year"},{"m":"Net interest income","v":"INR253.39 billion; increased 17.7% year-on-year and 3.9% sequentially"},{"m":"Net interest margin","v":"4.18%; 4.36% in the previous quarter and 4.30% in Q2 of last year"},{"m":"Non-interest income excluding treasury","v":"INR82.77 billion; grew 12.5% year-on-year"},{"m":"Fee income","v":"INR77.95 billion; increased 20.1% year-on-year"},{"m":"Operating expenses","v":"Increased 8.5% year-on-year and 1.9% sequentially"},{"m":"Total provisions","v":"INR8.51 billion; compared with INR9.14 billion in Q2 of last year"},{"m":"Consolidated profit after tax","v":"INR163.33 billion; grew 22.3% year-on-year"},{"m":"Total deposits","v":"Grew 27.1% year-on-year and 11.8% sequentially at September 30, 2026"},{"m":"Deposits excluding FCNR (B)","v":"Period-end deposits grew 17% year-on-year and 3.1% sequentially at September 30, 2026"},{"m":"Average liquidity coverage ratio","v":"About 126%"},{"m":"Overall loan portfolio","v":"Grew 30% year-on-year and 12% sequentially at September 30, 2026"},{"m":"Loan portfolio excluding specified international FCNR loans","v":"Grew 24.1% year-on-year and 7.3% sequentially"},{"m":"Retail and rural portfolio","v":"Grew 14.8% year-on-year and 3.2% sequentially"},{"m":"Net NPA ratio","v":"0.3% at September 30, 2026; 0.35% at June 30, 2026 and 0.39% at September 30, 2025"},{"m":"Provisioning coverage ratio","v":"75% at September 30, 2026"},{"m":"CET1 ratio","v":"16.01%; total capital adequacy was 16.68% at September 30, 2026"}],"takeaways":["[Margin pressure ahead]: Management expects the full impact of FCNR (B) deposits and the larger overseas book to moderate margins further in Q3, followed by a “pretty healthy pickup from Q4 onwards.” (p7–8)","[Agriculture provision review]: Management said the agricultural portfolio standard-asset provisioning review remains “work in process” and expects an update “over the next two quarters.” (p5)","[FCNR opportunity rationale]: Management described FCNR as a “one-off opportunity” intended to support business growth over time; it declined to provide a separate quantified margin impact. (p8–9)","[Growth allocation remains flexible]: Management gave no particular view on the future retail-corporate mix, saying it would pursue opportunities meeting its risk/reward threshold. (p5)","[Credit card decline explained]: Management attributed the quarterly credit-card book decline mainly to a timing and base effect, saying the underlying growth was “maybe marginally positive.” (p6)"],"revenue":["[Corporate lending drivers]: Management attributed corporate growth to client demand and pricing, including working-capital requirements, shifts from bond and foreign-currency borrowing markets, and investment activity. (p5)","[Fee income composition]: Retail, rural, and business banking customers constituted about 75% of total fees; fee income included processing fees for lending facilities against FCNR (B) deposits. (p3, p9)","[Savings customer balances]: Management said retail savings growth remained “quite okay” and described retail “money in bank,” including fixed deposits and savings accounts, as stable to slightly better versus past quarters. (p10)","[Jewel loan operating changes]: Management said sequential jewel-loan growth moderated while it aligned processes, distribution, and operational risk management with new RBI guidelines; it expected volumes to pick up. (p6)"],"profit":["[Margin drivers]: Management attributed margin moderation to a higher share of overseas-branch assets and the large sequential increase in retail term deposits, which reduced the average CASA ratio. (p3, p7)","[Operating expense drivers]: Management said the Q1-to-Q2 operating-expense increase was largely explained by costs of wholesale resources, bonds, and loans raised during the quarter to fund FCNR-related lending. (p9–10)","[Credit cost explanation]: Management said lower NPL formation in personal loans and cards contributed to provisioning; after adjustments for specified one-offs, it said credit cost would be “up to 50 basis points.” (p5)","[Corporate credit assessment]: Management said corporate credit costs should sustainably be lower than retail, citing its focus on return of capital and ancillary revenue streams. (p11)","[Capital and contingency provisions]: Management reported a CET1 ratio of 16.01%, total capital adequacy of 16.68%, and contingency provisions of INR131 billion at September 30, 2026. (p1)"],"pipeline":["[Branch network expansion]: The bank's branch count increased by 145 in H1 FY27, reaching 7,656 branches as of September 30, 2026. (p3)","[Technology expense]: Technology expenses were about 11.2% of operating expenses in H1 of the current year. (p3)","[ICICI Life shareholding]: The bank purchased 2% additional shareholding in ICICI Life Insurance Limited during the quarter for consideration of INR14.7 billion. (p1)","[FCNR liquidity deployment]: Management said excess liquidity was expected to be deployed “over the next two quarters,” a period it described as traditionally supportive of credit growth. (p8)"],"commitments":[{"c":"Agriculture provision review","s":"Management said the review process was underway and expected an update “over the next two quarters”","t":"Over the next two quarters","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Normalized credit cost","s":"“Up to 50 basis points” after adjusting for specified one-offs","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"NIM outlook","s":"Further moderation expected in Q3; “pretty healthy pickup from Q4 onwards”","t":"Q3; Q4 onwards","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Excess liquidity deployment","s":"Expected to be deployed “over the next two quarters”","t":"Over the next two quarters","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Jewel loan volumes","s":"Management said it would expect volumes “to pick up” after operational alignment with new RBI guidelines","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FCNR margin impact","s":"Management declined to provide a separate quantified impact, calling it a theoretical number","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management cited further Q3 margin moderation, ongoing agricultural portfolio provisioning review, changes in institutional savings deposits, and the operational adjustments needed to comply with new RBI jewel-loan guidelines.","source":"Earnings call transcript; K1 p7–8, K2 p5, K3 p8–9, K4 p5, K5 p6; R1 p5, R2 p3 and p9, R3 p10, R4 p6; P1 p3 and p7, P2 p9–10, P3 p5, P4 p11, P5 p1; I1 p3, I2 p3, I3 p1, I4 p8; C1 p5, C2 p5, C3 p3 and p7–8, C4 p8, C5 p6, C6 p8–9; M1–M4 p1, M5–M6 p3, M7–M10 p3, M11 p4, M12–M17 p1, M18–M20 p1."},
  {"name":"Leela Palaces Hotels & Resorts Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"14 Oct 2026","tone":"Positive","guidance":"FY27 EBITDA growth is expected to be significantly higher than initially guided; H2 growth remains anchored at “high teens,” with the original mid-teens FY27 EBITDA guidance maintained.","metrics":[{"m":"Operating revenue","v":"INR4,119 million; increased 33% year-on-year"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA","v":"INR1,877 million; increased 36%"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA margin","v":"46%; 118 basis point improvement over the previous year"},{"m":"Profit after tax (Q2 FY27)","v":"INR946 million; increased 27% year-on-year"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR1,433 million; grew by 72%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 operating revenue","v":"Grew 30%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 operating EBITDA","v":"Grew 38%; margin expanded by 246 basis points"},{"m":"Room revenue","v":"INR2,041 million; grew 39%"},{"m":"F&B revenue","v":"INR1,456 million; grew 21%"},{"m":"Management fees","v":"INR289 million; grew 76%"},{"m":"Other operation services revenue","v":"INR333 million; grew 25%"},{"m":"ADR","v":"INR25,046; grew 30%"},{"m":"India headline occupancy","v":"69%"},{"m":"Net promoter score","v":"87 for the first half"},{"m":"Brand.com room revenue share","v":"15.1% in H1 FY '27, versus 10.5% last year and 5.5% two years ago"},{"m":"Net debt","v":"INR12,150 million as of September 2026"},{"m":"Net debt to LTM EBITDA","v":"1.4 times; 1.6 times in March 2026 and 3.7 times in March 2025"},{"m":"Finance cost","v":"INR384 million for the quarter; H1 finance cost fell 37% to INR777 million"},{"m":"Five-project pipeline capex","v":"Around INR1,432 crores; around INR150 crores spent"},{"m":"F&B outlet and banquet mix","v":"Outlet around 56%; banquet around 44%"}],"takeaways":["[FY27 outlook improved]: Management said H1 performance significantly exceeded the original guidance and that FY27 should be “much better” than initially guided, while maintaining a “high teens” growth outlook for H2. (p17)","[BKC funding approval pending]: Management said it would seek shareholder approval for investment in buying the full FSI required for the Mumbai BKC mixed-use project; the overall committed capex remains unchanged. (p4, p10)","[Dubai renovation timeline]: Management expects to take over the Dubai hotel early next year, renovate it for 12 months, and communicate fuller operating visibility after renovation and branding. (p14)","[Luxury residences launch]: Management said Leela Luxury Residences in Mumbai was ready for show-arounds, with opening teams in place and first residents expected in the last quarter of the financial year. (p6)","[Acquisitions still under evaluation]: Management said it was evaluating opportunities across owned and managed assets, including acquisitions and brownfield acquisitions, in addition to its already contracted pipeline. (p9)"],"revenue":["[Room revenue drivers]: Room revenue growth was driven by ADR and occupancy gains and contribution from Coorg; management also cited broad-based demand across corporate travel, leisure, celebrations and events. (p3, p1)","[Non-room revenue mix]: For H1, 52% of revenue came from beyond rooms, including F&B, management fees, club revenues, wellness, transport and retail. (p2)","[Managed property fees]: Management attributed fee growth to ramp-up of managed properties, including performance-linked contracts and past key-money investments; it said the trajectory was supported by the expanded portfolio and upcoming openings. (p7)","[Direct booking contribution]: H1 FY27 contribution from the Leela brand.com website to room revenue rose to 15.1% from 10.5% last year and 5.5% two years ago. (p4)","[Corporate pricing negotiations]: Management said it was pitching for “mid to high double-digit growth rates” in corporate ask rates for CY '27; negotiations were ongoing and were expected to continue towards year-end. (p11)"],"profit":["[Payroll and Coorg costs]: Management attributed cost increases to additional headcount for new value drivers, ESOP expense recorded from this quarter, and Coorg’s ramp-up; excluding these impacts, flow-through was said to be close to 60%. (p10)","[Finance cost reduction]: H1 finance cost fell 37% to INR777 million, reflecting debt reduction through the IPO. (p4)","[Effective tax rate outlook]: Management said the H1 tax rate was 16% of PBT before accounting for joint-venture profit and anticipated an effective tax rate “in 20s” in FY '27. (p4)","[Debt and borrowing costs]: Net debt stood at INR12,150 million as of September 2026; management said borrowing costs had reduced by a further 30 basis points from mid-October after lender renegotiations. (p4)","[Dubai operating performance]: Management said the Dubai property achieved EBITDA break-even in Q1 and positive EBITDA in Q2; interest and depreciation continued to weigh on PAT. (p14)"],"pipeline":["[Five-project capex]: For the five contracted pipeline projects—Agra, Srinagar, Ayodhya, Ranthambore and Bandhavgarh—management stated capex of around INR1,432 crores, with around INR150 crores spent. (p20)","[Upcoming project openings]: Management said Srinagar and Bandhavgarh were expected towards the end of calendar year '27, while Agra, Ayodhya, Ranthambore and Sikkim were planned for '28. (p16)","[FY27 openings on track]: Management said The Leela Jaisalmer and Leela Luxury Residences were on track to open in the current financial year, with timing described as later this year or early next year. (p16)","[Wellness and renewable energy]: Earth by The Leela in New Delhi was operational; Earth Chennai was scheduled to open this quarter, while Ojasya in Bengaluru and renewable energy initiatives in Chennai and Coorg were progressing. (p3)","[Development pipeline scale]: Management said more than 1,000 keys were under development across marquee destinations and that over 5,200 keys were contracted or at various development stages by FY '30, including around 2,600 owned keys. (p3, p9)","[Training and talent development]: The Leela Center of Excellence had delivered over 4,800 man-hours of training and engaged more than 280 participants since launch. (p3)"],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 EBITDA growth","s":"Management maintained its mid-teens FY27 EBITDA guidance, while saying H1 had significantly exceeded the guidance and FY27 should be “much better” than initially guided","t":"FY '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 EBITDA growth","s":"Management said it remained anchored towards “high teens” growth for the balance of the year","t":"H2 FY '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Q3 RevPAR growth","s":"Management said it was “very much on track for high double-digit growth” in RevPAR","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Mumbai residences opening","s":"Management said the first set of residents was expected in the last quarter of the financial year, subject to last-mile approvals","t":"Last quarter of this financial year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"BKC FSI approval","s":"Management said it would reach out to shareholders for approval of investment to buy the entire FSI required for the project","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Dubai handover and renovation","s":"Management said handover was expected early next year, followed by 12 months for renovation before full visibility on EBITDA and other numbers","t":"Early next year; 12 months for renovation","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Resort occupancy","s":"Management said resorts were operating at mid-fifties, expected to move closer to 60, with a runway to “late sixties” in the next few years","t":"Next few years","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Srinagar and Bandhavgarh","s":"Management expected both openings towards the end of calendar year '27","t":"End of calendar year '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Four FY28 openings","s":"Management identified Agra, Ayodhya, Ranthambore and Sikkim as planned openings","t":"'28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Tadoba opening","s":"Management said Tadoba was opening towards CY '30; survey, architect and consultant appointments, and design development were underway","t":"CY '30","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Pipeline capex spending","s":"Management said it expected to spend around INR450 crores by end of this year and around INR600 crores next year on the five contracted pipeline projects","t":"By end of this year; next year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Effective tax rate","s":"Management anticipated an effective tax rate “in 20s” in FY '27","t":"FY '27","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management mentioned geopolitical uncertainty affecting Dubai international demand, Dubai ADRs lagging, Coorg still ramping up, and pending shareholder and other approvals for BKC.","source":"Earnings call transcript; K1 p17, K2 p4 and p10, K3 p14, K4 p6, K5 p9; R1 p1 and p3, R2 p2, R3 p7, R4 p4, R5 p11; P1 p10, P2 p4, P3 p4, P4 p14, P5 p20; I1 p20, I2 p16, I3 p16, I4 p3, I5 p3 and p9, I6 p3; C1 p17, C2 p17, C3 p11, C4 p6, C5 p4, C6 p14, C7 p16, C8 p16, C9 p16, C10 p16, C11 p20, C12 p4; M1–M4 p3, M5–M7 p4, M8–M11 p3, M12–M14 p2, M15–M18 p4, M19–M20 p20."},
  {"name":"Federal Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"All maintained: loan growth mid-teens; NIM improvement 5–6 basis points per quarter for the year; ROA improvement 3–4 basis points per quarter.","metrics":[],"takeaways":["Net profit ₹1,380 crore, highest in the bank's history, up 45.1% YoY and 17.7% QoQ.","Net interest income ₹3,153.4 crore, up 7% QoQ, 26.38% YoY; NIM 3.32%, down one basis point QoQ.","Gross advances ₹3,01,687 crore, up 21% YoY; total deposits ₹3,42,693 crore, up 18.6% YoY.","GNPA 1.32%, down 20 basis points QoQ; NNPA 0.17%; both at decade lows."],"revenue":[],"profit":[],"pipeline":[],"commitments":[],"margin":"Lending yields bottomed last quarter; externally benchmarked loans reprice ahead of deposits after October rate increase; FCNR impacted NIM.","risks":"Higher inflation and tighter policy; intense deposit competition; El Niño, geopolitics, interest-rate scenario; PSU bank competition in gold loans.","source":"Earnings call transcript (13 pages). Call date/quarter p1–2; guidance p6, p8, p11–12; profit and NII/NIM p3; advances/deposits p4; asset quality p5; margin outlook p3–4, p8; risks p4–5, p9."},
  {"name":"Gallantt Ispat Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"21 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"No revenue or margin number given. Expanded Gorakhpur capacity is expected at \"85% to 90%\" utilisation by December end. The medium-term growth plan has moved from this quarter to Q4 FY27.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR1,146 crores, +13% YoY; in line QoQ"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR111 crore vs INR185 crores Q1, INR135 crores Q2 FY26"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"9.64% vs 16.19% Q1, 12.91% Q2 FY26"},{"m":"EBITDA per tonne","v":"INR6,150 vs INR8,010 in Q1"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR61 crore vs INR124 crore Q1, INR89 crore Q2 FY26"},{"m":"PAT margin","v":"5.3% vs 10.8% Q1, 8.8% Q2 FY26"},{"m":"EPS","v":"INR2.51"},{"m":"Profit before tax","v":"INR83 crores vs INR165 crore Q1, INR103 crores Q2 FY26"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR2,292 crores, +7% vs INR2,141 crores"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"INR296 crore, 12.91% margin vs INR382 crores, 17.85%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT and EPS","v":"INR184 crores vs INR263 crores; EPS INR7.63"},{"m":"TMT sales volume","v":"1,65,000 tonnes vs about 1,92,000 tonnes in Q1"},{"m":"Kutch rolling mill utilisation","v":"68% vs 66% in Q1"},{"m":"Other income","v":"INR18 crores"},{"m":"Finance cost","v":"INR9 crores, stable"},{"m":"Net worth (30 Sep)","v":"INR3,487 crores vs INR3,316 crore in March"},{"m":"Borrowings (30 Sep)","v":"INR592 crore, mostly working capital"},{"m":"Inventory (30 Sep)","v":"INR669 crores vs INR551 crore in March"},{"m":"H1 FY27 capex","v":"INR278 crore; about INR141 crore in Q2"},{"m":"FY26 final dividend","v":"INR2 per share"}],"takeaways":["Growth plan deferred: The Vice Chairman had promised to share the medium-term growth plan this quarter. He deferred it to Q4 FY27 because the coal block award opens a larger greenfield option that depends on land acquisition and state incentives.","Shutdowns hit margins: A planned Gorakhpur shutdown to integrate the expansion, plus an unplanned power plant shutdown extended by a turbine issue, compressed margins. The Vice Chairman called it \"all one-time effect\".","Coal block decision pending: The Singrauli coal block was awarded in August 2026, and the company has \"another one-month time\" to finish due diligence. The CEO said becoming operational would take \"about two to three years\".","Price-cost lag: The CEO said coal costs rose in Q2, but the finished-steel price increase came only in September: \"the effect of the risen prices will come into Q3\".","Funding mix undecided: For the larger growth plan, the Vice Chairman said the company \"largely rely on internal accruals\". It will \"decide the mix of the funding\" based on project size."],"revenue":["Volumes down on shutdown: TMT sales were 1,65,000 tonnes vs about 1,92,000 tonnes in Q1. The Vice Chairman said better realisations offset the volume impact, keeping revenue in line QoQ.","Steel prices firming: The CEO said prices firmed from August as the monsoon receded, with September TMT prices about 21% higher YoY. He expects prices \"would remain strong\" as construction peaks from December.","Brand premium: The CEO cited a 5% to 6% premium over unbranded TMT in UP and 3% to 4% in Gujarat. He said the company holds more than 25% of the serviceable market in UP.","Kutch utilisation slow: Kutch rolling mill utilisation was 68% vs 66% in Q1. The CEO said distribution work showed \"not much result in seen in Q2\". He expects it to \"structurally further improve\" in Q3 and Q4.","Domestic demand: The CEO said finished steel consumption grew 7.5% to about 85 million tonnes in H1 and construction steel demand rose 7%. India remained a net importer of finished steel."],"profit":["Coal cost impact: The CEO said higher coal prices hit raw material cost by 4.5% to 5% of sales, about INR2,500 per tonne on TMT, with a further 1% increase on iron ore.","Captive power shortfall: The CEO said lower captive power availability during the extended power plant shutdown raised power costs. He called it \"one of the main reasons margins were compressed\". The plant resumes \"within a week\".","Inventory build: The CEO said inventory rose because of the prolonged shutdowns and stocking for added capacity. It will \"normalize in Q3\" once the plant is operational \"within the next 15, 20 days\".","Net cash, no term loans: Borrowings are mostly working capital, with no term loans for projects. The CFO cited about INR426 crores of bank balances and liquid investments plus INR266 crores of treasury deployment: \"net cash positive\".","Digital cost initiatives: The CEO said SAP integrations and AI-linked gate and weighbridge systems are plugging leakages, but \"it is difficult to comment on any number\"."],"pipeline":["Gorakhpur expansion: Capacity is expanding from 1 million to 1.23 million tonnes, adding every stage except sponge iron and 22 MW of captive power. Operations are proposed from 10 November.","Renewable power build-out: The 18 MW Sidhpur solar plant is completed. The 60 MW Prayagraj plant for Gorakhpur targets Q4 FY27. After that, about 5 to 7 MW, \"hardly… 5% to 6%\" of requirement, stays outsourced.","Captive iron ore mines: Exploration and environment and forest clearances are underway for the UP and Rajasthan blocks, targeting FY28. Full feed to the plants comes \"in a year or two, post-FY '28\".","Gujarat backward integration: Captive power debottlenecking was completed last month. A sponge iron (DRI) plant commissions in Q4, with its benefit \"from the next financial year\". Syncing it needs a Gujarat shutdown.","Capex programme progress: INR650 crores of the INR3,000 crore programme has been spent up to September 2026. About INR500 crore remains this year, of which INR400 crores is for Gorakhpur, all from internal accruals.","Coal replaces imports: The CEO said Singrauli coal would let Gorakhpur stop using imported South African coal entirely. He said it \"seems to be much cheaper\" than market purchases."],"commitments":[{"c":"Gorakhpur capacity commissioning","s":"Expansion to 1.23 million tonnes operational; start proposed 10th of November","t":"November '26","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Sidhpur solar restart","s":"18 MW plant \"set to resume operations\"","t":"November '26","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Prayagraj solar plant","s":"60 MW for Gorakhpur; solar savings INR30 crores to INR40 crores annually in power cost","t":"Q4 '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gujarat debottlenecking","s":"Upstream debottlenecking \"on track\"; sponge iron plant commissioning","t":"This financial year; Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Iron ore mines operational","s":"About INR2,000 per tonne EBITDA addition once fully feeding plants","t":"FY '28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Medium-term growth plan","s":"Deferred from this quarter; greenfield plan around raw material base","t":"Q4 '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Capex funding","s":"INR3,000 crore programme funded entirely through internal accruals; no term loans","t":"FY '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Inventory normalisation","s":"Working capital to normalise once expanded plant is operational","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Balance FY27 capex","s":"Around INR500 crore; INR400 crores for Gorakhpur","t":"\"during the year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gorakhpur utilisation ramp","s":"85% to 90% capacity utilisation, \"provided there is no major issues\"","t":"\"by December end\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Coal block due diligence","s":"Decide whether to proceed; operational in about two to three years thereafter","t":"\"another one-month\"; \"two to three years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Utilisation improvement","s":"Utilisation expected to improve 3% to 5% once capacities are synced","t":"\"next year\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Middle East conflict keeping energy, freight and coal costs elevated; surplus overseas steel shifting trade flows; sharp rises in sponge iron and pig iron prices.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 21 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p1–2, p9; K1 p1–2; K2 p1–2; K3 p1, p10; K4 p5; K5 p4; R1 p1–2; R2 p2, p5; R3 p2, p8; R4 p2, p10; R5 p2, p6; P1 p5; P2 p2; P3 p6; P4 p3; P5 p8; I1 p1–2, p9; I2 p2, p9; I3 p3, p5–6; I4 p2, p11–12; I5 p3, p7; I6 p11; C1 p1–2, p9; C2 p2; C3 p2, p9; C4 p2, p12; C5 p3, p5–6; C6 p2; C7 p1, p3; C8 p6; C9 p7; C10 p9; C11 p10; C12 p11; M1–M8 p3; M9–M11 p3; M12–M13 p2; M14–M15 p3; M16–M20 p3; Risks p1–2. Omitted for inconsistency: the expansion stated once as \"1.3 million tonnes\" (vs 1.23 million twice), the Gujarat solar stated as \"22 megawatt\" (vs 18 MW Sidhpur), the \"INR3,000 [Phonetic]\" per tonne raw material figure, the finished product price \"20%\" (vs about 21%), and the garbled \"Implied costs INR42 crores\"."},
  {"name":"Waaree Renewable Technologies Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"21 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"FY27 consolidated EBITDA margin maintained: \"more than 15% should be maintaining our margin… what we estimate at this stage\". Management declined to give an execution or revenue target for FY27.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR1,057.84 crores, +36.53% YoY"},{"m":"EBITDA","v":"INR159.98 crores"},{"m":"EBITDA margin","v":"15.12%"},{"m":"PAT","v":"INR81.48 crores"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR1,982.09 crores, +43.84% YoY"},{"m":"H1 FY27 EBITDA","v":"INR333.46 crores; margin 16.82%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 PAT","v":"INR200.44 crores"},{"m":"Solar EPC executed","v":"456 MWp"},{"m":"H1 FY27 solar EPC executed","v":"1,345 MWp"},{"m":"Q2 order additions","v":"1.7 GWp"},{"m":"Unexecuted order book (30 Sep)","v":"More than INR5,500 crores"},{"m":"Solar EPC and BESS book","v":"More than INR3,300 crores"},{"m":"T&D order book","v":"More than INR2,200 crores"},{"m":"Operational IPP portfolio","v":"82.82 MWp"},{"m":"O&M portfolio","v":"1.5 GWp"},{"m":"T&D margin","v":"Around 9% to 10%"},{"m":"T&D order pipeline","v":"More than INR20,000 crores"},{"m":"FY26 revenue","v":"INR3,300 crores"},{"m":"FY26 T&D revenue (APSPL)","v":"Around INR1,995 crores"},{"m":"Jamnagar facility capacity","v":"1 lakh metric ton"}],"takeaways":["Execution slowed on land: Solar EPC execution fell to 456 MWp. The CFO blamed \"some land portion handover delay\" at the developer's end, said this happened \"this quarter only\", and declined to quantify the delayed orders.","No FY27 execution target: Asked twice for a FY27 execution target, the CFO cited the order book, inflows and FY26 revenue of INR3,300 crores, but gave no number.","Acquisition raises costs: The CFO said depreciation is \"mostly contributed on account of the acquisition\", and the debt-funded finance cost \"will remain\". An analyst cited 75% debt funding of the INR1,225 crores APSPL deal; management did not confirm the figures.","Debt reduction, no timeline: The CFO confirmed the debt was taken for the acquisition. He said \"we'll see how we can reduce the debt\" and on interest \"will try to reduce as much as possible in the coming quarters\".","Mix guidance declined: The CFO said it is \"difficult to give any kind of number\" on the solar EPC vs T&D mix or margin impact. He said solar EPC will remain \"the key driver for the growth\"."],"revenue":["T&D now consolidated: The CFO said the T&D business achieved around INR1,995 crores last year and is \"in line with the guidance, whatever they internally set\". It is following an order pipeline of more than INR20,000 crores.","Recurring revenue base: The operational IPP portfolio is 82.82 MWp and the O&M portfolio has reached 1.5 GWp. The CFO said completed projects carry in-built O&M contracts \"for the next few years\".","BESS add-on demand: The CFO said customers with existing solar plants are asking to add BESS, and new orders also come with BESS. He cited regulatory requirements and grid stability.","Utility-scale and repeat customers: The CFO said the majority of orders are utility-scale, with some C&I. \"A lot of orders\" come from repeat customers through bilateral discussions, and Q2 inflows included new customers."],"profit":["T&D dilutes margin: The CFO said T&D margin is \"around 9% to 10%\", lower than solar EPC. He expects improvement \"in the coming quarters\" through scale and synergies with Associated Power Structures.","Opex rise from consolidation: The CFO said the multifold rise in other and employee expenses reflects consolidating both companies, \"in line with our budget estimates\".","No guarantee constraints: The CFO said working capital is mainly non-fund-based limits, and \"We are not facing any kind of constraints with respect to the banks\" for bidding. H1 cash flow movement reflected operations plus acquisition financing.","BESS margin approach: The CFO said BESS EPC margins depend on the project and site, and the company keeps \"a certain margin as a threshold\" in quotations, then expands it through operational efficiency."],"pipeline":["Order inflow and book: Q2 order additions were 1.7 GWp. The unexecuted order book is more than INR5,500 crores: more than INR3,300 crores solar EPC and BESS, and more than INR2,200 crores T&D.","Solar EPC pipeline: The CFO cited around 25 GW of domestic and around 9 GW of international pipeline, with \"active discussion with the customers on a bilateral basis\".","Jamnagar T&D facility: The company is expanding T&D manufacturing with an additional Jamnagar facility of 1 lakh metric ton capacity.","Policy tailwinds cited: The CFO cited PM Surya Sarovar Yojana (5 GW floating solar with storage) and Green Energy Corridor Phase 3 (around INR1.86 lakh crores outlay). He also cited the CEA's estimated battery storage need of around 208 GWh by 2030."],"commitments":[{"c":"Consolidated EBITDA margin","s":"\"More than 15% should be maintaining our margin… what we estimate at this stage\"","t":"FY '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Order book execution","s":"INR5,500 crores book \"likely to be converted in the next three to four quarters\"","t":"\"next three to four quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"T&D margin improvement","s":"From \"around 9% to 10%\"; improvement in subsidiary margin profile","t":"\"coming quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Interest cost reduction","s":"Acquisition financing \"will remain the constant\"; will \"try to reduce as much as possible\"","t":"\"coming quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Acquisition debt reduction","s":"\"We'll see how we can reduce the debt\"; no number given","t":"\"upcoming quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"T&D revenue contribution","s":"T&D order book to contribute to Waaree Renewable revenue; Q3 T&D wins to count in FY27","t":"\"coming quarters\"; FY '27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Execution guidance","s":"Declined to give FY27 execution or revenue target","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Land and connectivity readiness at the developer's end, including land procured gradually on larger projects, can delay project execution.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 21 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p6–7, p3–5; K1 p3, p9; K2 p3–5; K3 p7; K4 p5, p15; K5 p8, p12; R1 p5; R2 p1, p12; R3 p10, p12; R4 p11, p14; P1 p3–4, p6; P2 p10; P3 p13; P4 p11; I1 p1, p3; I2 p8; I3 p1–2; I4 p2; C1 p7; C2 p5, p9; C3 p4, p6; C4 p5; C5 p15; C6 p5, p7; C7 p3–5; M1–M7 p1; M8–M10 p1; M11–M13 p1; M14–M15 p1; M16 p6; M17 p5; M18 p5; M19 p5; M20 p1–2; Risks p3–4. Omitted for inconsistency or garbling: T&D order book \"INR2,002 crore\" (vs more than INR2,200 crores), the INR3,300 crores executed \"in the next two quarters\" (vs three to four quarters), and interest and depreciation stabilising over \"three years [Phonetic]\". Not used as management-confirmed: analyst-cited 60/40 mix, ~30% PAT fall, ~40% solar EPC degrowth, and finance cost/D&A figures."},
  {"name":"Vishal Mega Mart Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"21 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 goal of double-digit same-store sales growth, with Q3 \"higher than 10%\". The company is \"quite confident\" of opening more than 100 stores in FY27 and expects gross margin stable YoY in H2.","metrics":[{"m":"Revenue from operations","v":"INR3,399 crores, +14% YoY"},{"m":"Same-store sales growth","v":"5.4%; around 9% adjusted for festive shift"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA","v":"INR262 crores, +3.7% YoY"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA margin","v":"7.7% vs 8.5% last year"},{"m":"PAT adjusted for ESOP","v":"INR172 crores, +6.4% YoY; margin 5.1% vs 5.4%"},{"m":"Gross margin change","v":"Down 30 bps YoY; 20 bps from apparel shift"},{"m":"H1 FY27 revenue","v":"INR7,126 crores, +16.4%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 SSSG","v":"7.7%; about 9.5% adjusted"},{"m":"H1 FY27 operating EBITDA","v":"INR649 crores, +12.5%; margin 9.1% vs 9.4%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 adjusted PAT","v":"INR437 crores, +15.8%; margin 6.1% vs 6.4%"},{"m":"New stores","v":"26 in Q2; 53 in H1"},{"m":"Total stores (Sep end)","v":"844; presence in 582 cities"},{"m":"Trading area","v":"1.41 crore square feet"},{"m":"H1 FY27 private brand share","v":"74% of revenue"},{"m":"Small format stores","v":"18"},{"m":"Kerala store count","v":"35"},{"m":"Quick Commerce registered users","v":"Crossed 1.5 crore"},{"m":"Revenue by price point","v":"Opening 18 to 20%; highest 24 to 25%; middle about 50%"}],"takeaways":["Festive shift distorts quarter: Durga Puja, Dussehra and Navratri moved from September to October. The MD said this cut Q2 same-store sales growth by 3% to 4%, putting adjusted SSSG at around 9%.","Q3 must beat 10%: Confirming an analyst's arithmetic, the MD said \"Q3 will have to be higher than 10%\" for \"the first 9 months to look like double-digit growth\".","No further price increases: The MD said there is \"absolutely no plan to tinker with the pricing even beyond the festive season\". Opening price points are untouched, mid-price points saw \"very, very few\" increases, and higher price points carried most of them.","Board reviewing cash reserves: The MD said the board is \"engaged with this entire issue of the cash that we have on the balance sheet\" and will report \"as and when there's a conclusion\".","Growth from share gains: The MD said \"almost all our growth has come through market share gain and not through our existing customers buying more\". He said \"there is price elasticity of demand\" in this quarter's numbers."],"revenue":["Apparel shifted to Q3: The CFO said apparel mix was lower in Q2 because the clothing festivals of Puja and Dussehra fell in Q3. The MD expects \"a significantly higher bias towards apparel in Q3\".","Demand steady across regions: The MD said \"We are not seeing any moderation\" in demand, which is \"quite consistent across the country\". Uneven rainfall causes only \"a very temporary 1 or 2 day\" revenue disruption.","Price-point revenue mix: The MD said opening price points are about 18 to 20% of revenue, the highest price points about 24 to 25%, and the middle about 50%.","Private brand share: Private brands were 74% of H1 FY27 revenue (prepared remarks). In Q&A, the MD said \"75% of our revenue is through our private brands\".","Belong & Co. pilot: The new apparel format targets young, affluent, metro customers. In the first month, the MD said footfall-to-purchase conversion is \"north of 50%\", with repeat visits."],"profit":["Operating deleverage hit margins: Management said lower fixed-cost absorption from the festive shift weighed on Q2 and H1 EBITDA and PAT margins.","Gross margin dip explained: The CFO said gross margin fell 30 bps YoY, of which 20 bps came from the apparel shift. The MD said cost re-engineering offsets vendor price increases, with no material impact expected in Q3 and Q4.","Employee cost pressure: The CFO attributed the QoQ rise to ESOP cost and new stores. The minimum wage increase came in Q1. He said SSSG \"anywhere between 8 to 10 is good enough to offset this impact\".","Margin philosophy: The MD said gross margin gains will be reinvested in product quality or price. He expects EBITDA margin to rise about 30 bps a year, \"generally speaking\", with growth outpacing 6% to 7% store cost inflation.","Rent and inventory: The MD said there was \"no significant element\" of rental renegotiation, and lower rent growth reflects Tier 2 and 3 stores. The CFO said the inventory build shifted later with the 3-week festival shift."],"pipeline":["Store openings: 26 gross new stores in Q2 (15 South, including 6 in Kerala, now 35 stores) and 53 in H1. 24 of the 26 are in Tier 2 and 3 cities, adding 23 new cities.","Store size by location: The MD said \"I will firstly solve for stores\", with size set by catchment. Kerala stores are smaller and more numerous, and the Gujarat/Maharashtra pilot uses smaller stores; \"too early\" to conclude.","Small format scope: 18 small format stores exist, two added in Q2, relevant \"only for UP and Haryana at the moment\". The MD sees a national opportunity \"upwards of 3,000\" and an identified universe of 2,000 large format stores.","Automated warehouse network: A fully automated large warehouse is commissioned in North India. The East comes next, then the South, then the West.","Quick Commerce advertising: Quick Commerce registered users crossed 1.5 crore. The MD has decided to \"invest in advertising and digital media aggressively\", starting in Delhi and extending nationwide \"very soon\".","Belong & Co. rollout pending: The MD said \"we are still learning\", with \"a few stores\" this year. \"I will at a future date come back to you on the Belong and Co question.\""],"commitments":[{"c":"Full-year SSSG","s":"\"Our goal is to achieve double digit for the full year\"; Q3 \"higher than 10%\"","t":"FY27; Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gross margin stability","s":"\"Do not see any material impact\"; stable YoY in second half","t":"Q3, Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"No further price increases","s":"Opening price points protected; \"no plan to tinker with the pricing even beyond the festive season\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Store openings","s":"\"Quite confident of our guidance of opening more than 100 stores\"","t":"This year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Belong & Co. plans","s":"\"A few stores\" this year; will \"come back to you\" on rollout","t":"This year; \"at a future date\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Annual store additions","s":"100 plus stores, rising every year over the previous year","t":"Every year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"EBITDA margin improvement","s":"\"Probably improve our EBITDA margin by 30 bps every year\"","t":"Every year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Employee cost offset","s":"SSSG \"anywhere between 8 to 10\" offsets elevated employee costs","t":"\"subsequent quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Cash deployment decision","s":"Board reviewing cash reserves; will report back on conclusion","t":"\"as and when\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Automated warehouses","s":"East India next, then South, then West","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Quick Commerce advertising","s":"Aggressive advertising, started in Delhi, extended to all parts of the country","t":"\"Very soon\"","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Renewed inflationary pressure across key raw material categories, an uneven monsoon, and an evolving geopolitical environment.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 21 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p3, p8, p16; K1 p1–2; K2 p3; K3 p5, p15–16; K4 p14–15; K5 p13–14; R1 p7; R2 p4–5; R3 p15; R4 p2, p11; R5 p2, p7; P1 p1–2; P2 p3, p7; P3 p4, p14; P4 p9, p11; P5 p6; I1 p2, p10; I2 p11–12; I3 p4, p9; I4 p15; I5 p2, p15; I6 p8; C1 p3; C2 p3, p16; C3 p5, p15–16; C4 p8; C5 p8; C6 p9; C7 p9; C8 p14; C9 p14–15; C10 p15; C11 p15; M1–M5 p1; M6 p7; M7–M8 p1; M9–M10 p1–2; M11–M14 p2; M15 p4; M16 p10; M17 p2; M18 p15; Risks p1–2. Omitted as garbled: large format count \"at this stage we are at 50-odd\" and \"AgriCommerce… 781 stores across 532 cities\"."},
  {"name":"Mahindra & Mahindra Financial Services Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"21 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"Medium-to-long-term targets maintained: AUM growth 16%–18%, credit cost 1.3%–1.7%, ROA 2.2%–2.5%. The MD said \"we never give quarter or yearly commitments\". The CFO guided cost of funds upward.","metrics":[{"m":"PAT","v":"INR882 crores, +55%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 consolidated PAT growth","v":"+68%"},{"m":"Disbursement growth","v":"+22%"},{"m":"AUM growth","v":"+15%"},{"m":"Lending AUM","v":"INR1,56,000 crores; 80% wheels, 20% non-wheels"},{"m":"Core vs non-wheels growth","v":"Core +21%; non-wheels +52%"},{"m":"GS2 + GS3","v":"8.1%; down 17 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Credit cost","v":"1.6%"},{"m":"ROA","v":"2.2 vs 1.6 last year; down sequentially"},{"m":"Capital adequacy","v":"18%; Tier 1 16.2%"},{"m":"Cost-to-income","v":"Under 37%, from above 40%"},{"m":"Fee-based income","v":"Increased from 1% to 1.6%"},{"m":"Overlay buffer","v":"INR852 crores"},{"m":"End losses","v":"1.2"},{"m":"AI-orchestrated disbursements","v":"INR1,663 crores"},{"m":"MRHFL PAT","v":"INR35 crores"},{"m":"Floating rate borrowings","v":"Around 45%–48%"},{"m":"Branches","v":"MFSL 1,400; MRHFL 400"},{"m":"Employees","v":"25,000; close to 9,000 collection officers"}],"takeaways":["Festive start weaker: The MD said underlying commerce and unit disbursements in the first 10 days of Navratri were \"a fall from last year\", with tractor \"a little more decline\". Last year's base included GST-related pent-up demand.","Rising cost of funds: The CFO said a 25 bps hike reprices about 10 bps of existing floating borrowing within a quarter. Cost of funds \"will be towards the upward trajectory\". He declined to quantify the effect of 75 bps of hikes.","Insurance draft risk: The MD said the insurance consultation paper \"can have a material impact if it is implemented… as is\", cutting fee income at MFSL and MIBL. It is \"too early to quantify\".","PAT growth not sustainable: The MD said PAT growth \"is not sustainable… at the 70% levels\". Credit cost \"has been optimized\", and loan growth of 16%–18% is the proxy for income growth.","Drought watch on tractors: In 282 drought-prone districts across 3 states, the MD said it is \"early days\" because tractor installments are half-yearly. He would not be \"surprised\" by delays, but \"Delay is not equal to default\"."],"revenue":["Broad-based disbursement growth: The MD cited growth in passenger vehicles 28%, used 15%, CV 16%, tractor 13%, three-wheeler 32%, SME 13% and other businesses 71%. Core wheels grew 21% and non-wheels 52%.","Lending mix shift: AUM is 80% wheels and 20% non-wheels, including trade advances. The MD said the \"alpha\" in growth is diversification. Personal loans to existing customers and prime housing (started August) are building.","Trade advance elevated: The MD said this year's trade advance is \"clearly a clip above the normal\" to drive Q3 festive growth. As a non-earning asset, it compressed loan income in Q2, along with the liquidity buffer.","H2 growth hedged: The MD said last year's H2 base is \"significant\", and \"we're just hoping it's not a reverse of the last year\". The company \"will not chase any growth at the cost of either price or risk\".","Rural demand intact: The MD said vehicle registration data showed rural and semi-urban growing \"at a marginally higher clip than urban and metro\" through September. \"We are not discounting rural sentiment as a whole.\""],"profit":["Inter-quarter volatility reduced: The MD said GS2 plus GS3 fell 17 bps between Q1 and Q2, against past seasonal spikes, and the credit cost increase was only 5 bps vs 23 bps last year.","Overlay buffer held: The MD said the INR852 crores overlay, created for El Nino and the West Asia crisis, may be used \"if those disruptions become material\". \"I don't see any need to create additional ones.\"","Liquidity buffer drag: The MD said the liquidity buffer has \"pretty much doubled\" in rupee terms, causing a returns drag. Both he and the CFO see \"optionalities of a reduction\" but \"not to a very large extent\".","NIM pressure: The MD said the 2.2%–2.5% ROA target needs NIM above 7%, and \"we are right now… at the lower end\". NIM expansion will not be \"extremely easy\". Incremental wheels pricing has started rising since the repo hike.","Cost-to-income and end losses: Cost-to-income fell from above 40% to under 37%. The MD called current end losses of 1.2 \"reasonable\" and said \"we will see… how that number can even slide down further\"."],"pipeline":["MRHFL merger timeline: Lender approval has been received, with NCLT and regulatory approvals pending. The CFO gave the appointed date as 1st April 2027, and the MD targets June–July of next fiscal as the effective date.","Mortgage strategy: The plan covers affordable housing, LAP and \"some segments of prime housing\". The MD said mortgage \"might create a bit of a drag\" initially, and the merger removes duplication across 400 MRHFL and 1,400 MFSL branches.","AI-orchestrated disbursements: AI-orchestrated disbursements reached INR1,663 crores, mainly top-up, used vehicle and personal loans, at \"a lower cost of acquisition\". 75% of the back office is on enabled workflows. The MD said it is too early for asset-quality insight.","Co-lending restart pending: The MD said co-lending under the new regulation \"is in the kitchen right now\", with system integration underway with a couple of banks. He will give commentary when live.","Headcount for growth: The MD said headcount additions are \"essential\" for growth in wheels, SME and mortgage, including moving consistently performing off-roll staff to on-roll. He expects the income-to-expense gap to be maintained."],"commitments":[{"c":"AUM growth","s":"16%–18%, \"medium to long-term\"; \"I'm not changing our posture\"","t":"Medium to long term; FY31","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Credit cost range","s":"1.3%–1.7%; \"I don't want to kind of change any guidance\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ROA aspiration","s":"2.2%–2.5% ROA; requires NIM above 7%","t":"Medium term; FY31","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Cost of funds","s":"\"Will be towards the upward trajectory\"; declined to quantify","t":"Coming quarters","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Liquidity buffer","s":"\"Possibly optionalities of a reduction\"; \"not to a very large extent\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Overlay buffer","s":"Hold INR852 crores; no additional overlays seen as needed","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Prime housing disclosure","s":"Will call out prime housing separately in coming quarters","t":"\"quarters that unlock from here on\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Co-lending relaunch","s":"Integrating systems with a couple of banks; commentary when live","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"MRHFL merger","s":"Appointed date 1st April 2027; effective \"June-July of next fiscal\"","t":"June–July next fiscal","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Insurance fee impact","s":"Quantification after final regulation; \"wait and watch\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Book maturity disclosure","s":"Will consider disclosing asset maturity; connect offline","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Repo rate hikes with more expected, El Nino-linked drought risk, and the West Asia crisis.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 21 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p7, p8, p14, p16–17, p22; K1 p13; K2 p5, p14, p18; K3 p3–4, p17; K4 p10; K5 p4–5, p13; R1 p1–2; R2 p1, p9; R3 p3, p9; R4 p7, p16; R5 p21; P1 p2; P2 p8; P3 p2, p5; P4 p18–20; P5 p3, p21; I1 p15–16; I2 p11–12; I3 p3, p6, p14; I4 p12; I5 p15; C1 p7, p16, p22; C2 p8, p21; C3 p3, p19–20, p22; C4 p14, p18; C5 p5; C6 p8; C7 p9; C8 p12; C9 p15–16; C10 p17; C11 p18–19; M1 p1; M2 p3; M3–M4 p1; M5–M6 p1; M7 p1–2; M8 p1; M9 p3; M10 p2; M11 p3; M12 p3–4; M13 p8; M14 p21; M15 p3; M16 p2; M17 p19; M18 p12; M19 p4, p6; Risks p2, p4. Omitted as garbled: \"FY21 overall goals\" (vs FY31 in closing remarks), \"ROEs are at 2.3%\", and the closing \"ROE profile… 2.2%-2.5%\" (ROA stated elsewhere)."},
  {"name":"Mahindra Holidays & Resorts India Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"21 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"No financial guidance (\"we don't give guidance\"). The target of 10,000 keys by FY30 is maintained as \"very much on track\". About 1,000 gross keys are expected over the next 6 months.","metrics":[{"m":"Standalone total income","v":"Rs 395 crores, +4% YoY"},{"m":"Standalone EBITDA","v":"Rs 124 crores vs Rs 141 crores, down 12%"},{"m":"Standalone PAT","v":"Rs 43 crores vs Rs 52 crores, down 16%"},{"m":"Consolidated total income","v":"Rs 819 crores, +9%"},{"m":"Consolidated EBITDA","v":"Rs 194.8 crores, +5%"},{"m":"Consolidated PAT","v":"Rs 19.7 crores, +17%"},{"m":"Total sales value incl. upgrades","v":"Rs 141 crores, +6% YoY"},{"m":"Upgrade value","v":"Rs 83 crores, +26%"},{"m":"Average unit realisation incl. upgrades","v":"Rs 13.4 lakhs, +43%"},{"m":"Member base","v":"About 3,03,000"},{"m":"Occupancy","v":"75.6%, +220 bps"},{"m":"Resort revenue growth","v":"+15%, highest in last 8 quarters"},{"m":"TRevPAR (Sep end)","v":"Rs 8,777 per room, +6% YoY"},{"m":"Network (Sep)","v":"5,762 keys across 106 resorts; 6 resorts exited"},{"m":"Keys under renovation","v":"442"},{"m":"HCRO revenue and EBITDA","v":"€36.5 million, +8%; EBITDA €1.7 million vs €1.3 million"},{"m":"HCRO loss in Indian books","v":"Rs 18 crores vs Rs 27 crores; net loss €0.3 million"},{"m":"Deferred revenue (Sep end)","v":"Rs 5,828 crores"},{"m":"Cash balance (Sep end)","v":"Rs 1,329 crores"},{"m":"H1 FY27 capex","v":"Rs 312 crores vs Rs 166 crores a year ago"}],"takeaways":["Standalone profit down on investments: Standalone EBITDA and PAT fell on \"group-linked costs\". The MD also cited growth investments, renovation shutdowns, new resorts \"not profitable initially\", and changes in GST and solar subsidies.","Portfolio exits near completion: Six resorts were exited in Q2 (net reduction of 103 keys). The MD expects about 650 key exits this year after about 400 last year, after which \"a large part of the journey\" will be \"largely done\".","HCRO strategic review pending: The MD said the HCRO strategic review \"is in progress\" and \"as soon as we… reach any kind of conclusions on that, we will come back\".","Project costs and delays: The MD said project delivery costs rose \"in some cases\", with delays \"impacting openings a bit\". He added \"we are not pulling back on any of our plans\".","Deferred revenue query deferred: Asked about VO revenue recognition versus the FY26 deferred revenue schedule, the MD said \"I don't have the exact details\" and offered to clarify offline."],"revenue":["Keystone mix and upgrades: The MD said the 10-year (Ivory-led) and 5-year products make up about 80% of the portfolio. Sales moved from a familiarisation phase to acceleration, and he expects net member additions to rise \"over the next 1 or 2 quarters\".","Referral-led member additions: Organic and digital made up 66% of member additions. The MD said referrals are usually \"between 40 and 50%\" of new sales, and cost of acquisition as a percentage has fallen YoY.","Driving holidays offset airfares: The MD said West Asia-driven airfare increases had a \"limited impact on occupancy\" at fly-in destinations, offset by driving holidays. He does not expect a major impact in Q3.","Seasonality: Membership and ASF income are non-seasonal and make up about 2/3 of revenue. The MD put the split at \"probably 35 to 40% in the first half followed by 60 to 65% in the second half\". Q2 is \"usually our worst quarter\".","Occupancy and non-members: The MD said Q2 occupancy \"is obviously not representative\", with full-year occupancy usually \"between 80% and 81%\". Corporate and MICE are growing but \"still a very small fraction\"."],"profit":["Renovation drag: The MD said 442 keys under renovation contribute nothing to revenue. \"The majority of them should come back in Q3 and Q4\", but he gave no revenue impact figure.","HCRO loss drivers: The MD said forex impact this year is \"minimal\", against euro-loan forex losses last year. Interest cost, the standalone HCRO result and Ind-AS lease accounting drive the international loss.","Internally funded capex: The CFO said H1 cash generation matched last year while capex nearly doubled. With almost Rs 1,300 crores of cash, \"we are going to rely on the internal accruals\".","Marketing spend ahead: The MD said the sales force will stay \"roughly constant\". Marketing may rise with new resorts and the Club M rebranding, with spends over Q3 and Q4."],"pipeline":["Key additions and 10,000 target: About 1,000 gross keys are due in the next 6 months from own and partner-led projects. 8 greenfield and brownfield projects give 85% visibility on 10,000 keys by FY30, with \"the remaining 1,500 keys\" still to be found.","Project opening timelines: The own-build Ganpatipule resort opens \"around Q3 end, maybe Q4 beginning\". Kumbhalgarh and Jaisalmer open in Q3, Poovar has just opened, and Gir opens \"potentially Q4 end, Q1 early\".","Capex and transformations: H1 capex was Rs 312 crores vs Rs 166 crores a year ago, with \"almost an equal amount in the next 6 months\". Two properties were transformed in Q2, and about 5 transformations are underway.","Capital allocation split: The MD said that over the longer term, about 70% of capital goes to new resorts and about 30% to improving existing resorts.","Luxury Signature Resorts: The luxury offering is positioned for \"family and tribe\", is experience-forward and discovery-based, and uses local design, F&B and experiences. The targeted ARR is about 20,000.","AI in guest operations: AI is used for paperless check-ins, WhatsApp billing and anticipating guest needs. An AI tool ranks resorts by customer sentiment to guide exits."],"commitments":[{"c":"Gross key additions","s":"About 1,000 gross keys, including own and partner-led projects","t":"\"next 6 months\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"10,000 keys target","s":"\"Very much on track\"; 85% visibility from funnel; net of exits","t":"FY30","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Renovated keys return","s":"442 keys under renovation; \"majority of them should come back\"","t":"Q3 and Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"H2 capex","s":"\"Almost an equal amount\" to H1's Rs 312 crores, funded internally","t":"\"next 6 months\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ganpatipule resort opening","s":"Own-build resort","t":"\"Q3 end, maybe Q4 beginning\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Kumbhalgarh and Jaisalmer reopening","s":"Renovation projects back online","t":"Q3","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gir resort opening","s":"New resort","t":"\"potentially Q4 end, Q1 early\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Key exits","s":"About 650 keys exited this year; rationalisation then \"largely done\"","t":"This year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"HCRO strategic review","s":"\"In progress\"; will come back on conclusion","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Club M rebranding","s":"Brand and marketing spends","t":"Q3 and Q4","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Capital allocation split","s":"About 70% to new resorts, 30% to existing resorts","t":"\"longer-term\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Deferred revenue clarification","s":"CFO's team to clarify deferred revenue recognition with analysts offline","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"A challenging Finnish economic environment for HCRO, West Asia-linked airfare increases, and new properties that \"don't make money immediately\".","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 21 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p1–4; K1 p2–3, p14; K2 p1, p9; K3 p10; K4 p4; K5 p5; R1 p3; R2 p1, p11, p14; R3 p4; R4 p6, p14; R5 p7, p12; P1 p2, p4; P2 p10; P3 p14; P4 p6–7; I1 p1–2, p10–11; I2 p8; I3 p1–2, p8; I4 p5; I5 p8–9; I6 p2, p9; C1 p1, p11; C2 p2, p10; C3 p4; C4 p8; C5 p8; C6 p8; C7 p8; C8 p9; C9 p10; C10 p7; C11 p5, p13; C12 p5, p15; M1–M3 p2–3; M4–M6 p3; M7–M10 p1; M11–M13 p2; M14 p1; M15 p2; M16–M17 p3; M18–M19 p3; M20 p2; Risks p2, p13. Omitted as garbled: 12-month retention \"up from about 11%\" and quarterly growth investment \"9 to 15 crores\"."},
  {"name":"The Indian Hotels Company Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"21 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"FY27 consolidated revenue growth raised from 12%–14% to 15%–17%, with hotel segment revenue growth of 17%–19%. The raised guidance excludes the OHL merger.","metrics":[{"m":"Consolidated operating revenue","v":"Rs 2,484 crores, +22% YoY"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA","v":"Rs 706 crores, +24% YoY"},{"m":"Operating EBITDA margin","v":"28.4%, +50 bps"},{"m":"PAT","v":"Rs 349 crores, +23%"},{"m":"Hotel segment growth","v":"Revenue +25%; EBITDA +30%"},{"m":"Standalone operating revenue","v":"Rs 1,336 crores, +26% YoY"},{"m":"Standalone operating EBITDA","v":"Rs 520 crores, +40%; margin expansion 400 bps"},{"m":"Standalone PAT margin","v":"27%"},{"m":"Occupancy","v":"82% vs 75% in Q2 last year"},{"m":"Standalone RevPAR growth","v":"+28%"},{"m":"Management fee income","v":"Rs 156 crores vs Rs 125 crores, +25%"},{"m":"Ginger consolidated revenue","v":"Rs 193 crores, +24%; EBITDA margin 40%"},{"m":"H1 FY27 signings and openings","v":"47 signed; 20 opened"},{"m":"Portfolio (Sep)","v":"670 hotels; over 69,000 keys"},{"m":"Operational hotels","v":"390 (Sep); 392 as of 20 Oct"},{"m":"Pipeline keys","v":"Over 34,800; 94% capital light"},{"m":"Gross cash reserves","v":"Over Rs 4,350 crores"},{"m":"Taj Palace New Delhi room revenue","v":"+132% YoY"},{"m":"Renewable energy share","v":"41%"},{"m":"Wedding nights next year","v":"95 vs normal 60–65"}],"takeaways":["Guidance raised 300 bps: The MD expects to exceed the earlier 12%–14% guidance by 300 basis points. He said a few hundred bps slip on existing hotels \"will be compensated through the not-like-for-like growth\" from 30 to 34 openings.","700-hotel target to reset: The portfolio has crossed 675 hotels against the Accelerate 2030 target of 700, which should be reached \"definitely before the end of this financial year\". A revised development target comes by mid-November.","OHL merger: The all-stock merger at 25 IHCL shares per 117 OHL shares has an appointed date of 1st April 2027. The MD expects a 6%–7% upside to FY28 standalone operating revenue.","FY28 better: Asked whether FY28 can match FY27, the MD said \"it will be better\", citing another \"50 or 70\" hotels, a higher wedding count and an international recovery, barring \"war or pandemic\".","Pierre solution pending: The MD said of the Pierre, New York, \"we are getting very close to finding a more sustainable solution\". It runs a positive EBITDA margin but \"is not the one which will do 20% or 25%\"."],"revenue":["Balanced occupancy and rate growth: The CFO said Q2 growth was \"balanced… between occupancy and ARR\", with occupancy up roughly 6%–7% in both standalone and domestic consolidated hotels. Standalone RevPAR grew 28% and RevPAR grew double digits across all brands.","MICE and weddings: The CFO said MICE grew faster in Q2, with the BRICS Summit among the drivers. Without BRICS, Delhi growth would have been \"late 20s\". Next year has about 95 wedding nights vs a normal 60–65.","Asset management pricing: Upgrades at marquee hotels are lifting pricing power. Taj Palace New Delhi room revenue grew 132% in Q2, and the hotel crossed Rs 100 crore monthly revenue in September.","Foreign tourist upside: The CFO said foreign room nights were \"pretty much flat\" YoY, but revenue contribution rose on higher spending. The MD sees foreign arrivals CAGR rising from 3.9% to 5%–6% as a \"hidden upside\".","City-level recovery expected: The MD said Chennai and Kolkata have lagged other metros and should \"move up significantly\" by \"next quarter or the quarter after\". London saw \"at least\" a double-digit increase in October after works were completed."],"profit":["No one-off in standalone margin: The CFO said there was \"no one-off\". Operating leverage, MICE activity and management fees booked in standalone drove the expansion despite normal payroll increases, the PF rules and the wage code. \"Positive outlook on margins without… specific guidance.\"","Taj SATS consolidation year: The CFO said airline schedule cuts from June hurt Q2 margins, while non-flight catering preserved the top line. The MD expects Taj SATS to return to prior levels and improve in H2.","London cost pressures: The CFO said London and US costs rose on fuel and power from the West Asia crisis. A London property tax relief was removed this quarter, which \"will get baked into the base going forward\".","Cash and capex: Gross cash reserves exceed Rs 4,350 crores. The MD said H1 capex was 20%–30% higher than the annual average of the preceding 7 to 8 years, because \"something is always under renovation\".","Management fee mix: Management fee growth came from a 25% rise in managed inventory, higher RevPAR, technical fees, and brand fees from branded residences and villas. The MD noted Ras Al Khaimah brand fees \"stops\" with the West Asia crisis."],"pipeline":["Portfolio expansion: 47 hotels were signed and 20 opened in H1, taking the portfolio to 670 hotels and over 69,000 keys. 392 hotels were operational \"as of yesterday\", and the 400 mark comes in November or before Q3 end.","Capital-light pipeline: Of over 34,800 pipeline keys, 94% are capital light (81% management contracts, 13% capital-light leases). The CFO cited guidance of 5,000 key openings every year.","Select service scaling: Ginger crossed 100 operational hotels and targets 150 by March 31st FY27. Cumin has over 130 outlets, AmaStays and Trails over 400 villas, and Tree of Life over 50 resorts.","Acquisition integration: Clarks has close to 75 signings, 30 already on board as Ginger. The CFO said Brij and Atmantan grew after marketing and sales integration, with \"upside\" in the next financial year.","International stance: The MD said \"we will not be aggressive on international growth but opportunistic\". Frankfurt is ramping up after startup challenges, and San Francisco is \"almost 90% there\".","ESG progress: Renewable sources supply 41% of energy, with over 361 EV charging stations across 170 locations. The company has trained over 56,000 youth toward its goal of 100,000+ by 2030."],"commitments":[{"c":"FY27 consolidated revenue growth","s":"Raised from 12%–14% to 15%–17%; excludes OHL","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"FY27 hotel segment revenue","s":"17%–19% growth","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"400 operational hotels","s":"Cross 400-hotel milestone","t":"November, \"latest before the end of Q3\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Revised development target","s":"Reset 700-hotel Accelerate 2030 target; revert to investors","t":"\"by middle of November\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Management fee growth","s":"High teens CAGR; \"disappointed if we don't cross Rs 1,000 crores in 2 years\"","t":"2 years","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Ginger hotel count","s":"150 operational hotels","t":"March 31st, FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"OHL merger","s":"Appointed date 1st April 2027; 6%–7% upside to standalone operating revenue","t":"FY28","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Hotel openings","s":"50 openings this year; 30 in H2, \"maybe 32 or 34\"","t":"FY27; H2","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Management fee guidance","s":"Separate revised guidance on fees and pipeline value","t":"\"end of next quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Key openings","s":"5,000 keys every year","t":"Per year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"OHL benefit details","s":"Revenue and margin benefits to be shared","t":"\"end of the financial year\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Skilling target","s":"100,000+ youth skilled","t":"2030","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"West Asia geopolitical tensions raising fuel prices and airfares and cutting airline capacity, moderating international and long-haul travel demand. Management said it has \"no control\" over further war developments.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 21 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p1, p3; K1 p1, p7; K2 p2, p7; K3 p3, p18; K4 p10; K5 p12; R1 p1, p5; R2 p5–6; R3 p1–2; R4 p13; R5 p8, p19; P1 p14–15; P2 p4–5; P3 p8; P4 p2–3, p9; P5 p2, p11; I1 p2, p7; I2 p2, p11; I3 p2; I4 p18; I5 p4, p9, p12; I6 p3; C1 p1, p3; C2 p1; C3 p2; C4 p2, p7; C5 p2, p11; C6 p2; C7 p3; C8 p3, p7; C9 p11; C10 p11; C11 p18–19; C12 p3; M1–M4 p1; M5 p1; M6–M8 p1; M9–M10 p1; M11 p2; M12 p2; M13–M14 p2; M15 p2, p7; M16 p2; M17 p2; M18 p2; M19 p3; M20 p6; Risks p1, p4. Omitted as garbled: standalone EBITDA margin \"3 38.9%\", the \"FY26\" label on upgraded guidance (FY27 elsewhere), \"25% growth in consolidated domestic hotel supply\", and \"margins of 35% consolidated\" (conflicts with 28.4%)."},
  {"name":"The South Indian Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Optimistic","guidance":"Loan and deposit growth of 15%–16% maintained, \"preferably… in the higher bucket\", around 16%. NIM guidance of 3.25% already beaten. NIM target 3.45%–3.50% over 12 months.","metrics":[{"m":"Net profit","v":"INR426 crores vs INR351 crore, +21% YoY"},{"m":"Net interest income","v":"INR1,116 crores vs INR808 crores, +38%"},{"m":"NIM","v":"3.3% (opening), 3.33% (Q&A); +10 bps QoQ, +53 bps YoY"},{"m":"ROA and ROE","v":"ROA 1.08% (opening), \"1.10%\" (Q&A); ROE 12%"},{"m":"Total deposits","v":"INR137,279 crore, +19% YoY"},{"m":"Gross advances","v":"INR109,517 crore, +19% YoY; 20% ex-write-off"},{"m":"CASA","v":"INR43,310 crore, +18% YoY"},{"m":"Gross NPA","v":"1.24%, down 169 bps YoY"},{"m":"Net NPA","v":"0.09%, down 47 bps YoY"},{"m":"PCR","v":"97.84% incl. write-off; 92.42% excl. write-off"},{"m":"Slippage ratio","v":"0.09%"},{"m":"Credit cost","v":"0.14%"},{"m":"Capital adequacy","v":"19.40%; Tier 1 18.71%"},{"m":"Gold loan book","v":"Around INR15,000 crore retail; INR10,000 crore agri; +41%"},{"m":"Loan mix","v":"Corporate 39%; retail, agri and MSME 61%"},{"m":"EBLR-linked loans","v":"Around 43%; MCLR around 5%; fixed around 47%"},{"m":"TWO recovery","v":"INR73 crores"},{"m":"AFS reserve (30 Sep)","v":"Debit balance INR43 crores; quarter impact INR52–53 crores"},{"m":"FCNR deposits","v":"Around $239 million; 95%–96% direct"},{"m":"Total business","v":"INR246,796 crores, +19%"}],"takeaways":["NIM ahead of guidance: The MD said NIM of 3.33% \"comfortably beaten\" the 3.25% guidance. He expects 3.36%–3.38% in the short term and 3.45%–3.50% over \"12 months or four quarter\", helped by repo hikes and portfolio churn.","ROA trajectory: The MD said ROA should rise from 1.1% to \"1.20% and 1.25% levels very shortly… in a year itself\". The medium-term target is 1.25%–1.30% and long-term 1.5%.","Conservative provisioning: The MD said higher provisions were \"being conservative\", account-based and not floating or one-off, with \"no cause of concern\". The ECL impact is \"not very substantial\", and the glide path decision is \"too early\".","Current account one-off: The MD said the QoQ current account jump included \"one-off flows… due to some of the IPOs\" at September end. \"There could be some minor slippage in that current account number.\"","Leadership continuity: The new MD took over from 1st October. He said \"Absolutely no change in the strategy… whenever new MD comes, and if you start changing the system, it changes the whole ecosystem.\""],"revenue":["Mix shift lagging: The corporate-to-retail mix is 39:61 against a 35:65 target. The MD acknowledged \"the segment change is not happening\" fast enough. The ED said the shift \"will be gradual\" while corporate is not being marginalised.","Gold loan push: The gold portfolio grew 41% and is 25% of loans. The MD targets 30%–32% \"immediately\" and 35% as the first phase. The split is 60% retail and 40% agri, with blended yield \"closer to 10%\".","Retail disbursements leading: The MD said retail disbursements, including MSME, are outpacing wholesale. Priority order: gold loans, business loans and MSME, with personal loans retained.","Corporate repricing: The MD said corporate loans priced to repo or T-bill have seen upticks of \"25 to 30 basis points\" in the last three months. Repo hikes are passed to new gold loans, with no spread increase yet.","Fee income sustainable: The MD said fee momentum will sustain with \"no one-offs\". Third-party income is \"5% to 6% or maybe 7%\" of fees, limiting bancassurance circular impact. The CFO put banca fees at \"INR30 crores to INR35 crores\"."],"profit":["Technical write-off recovery: The CFO said recovery from technically written-off accounts was INR73 crores this quarter. He expects INR50 crores to INR55 crores to continue, declining after \"four to five quarters or six quarters\" as the stock exhausts.","Deposit cost tailwind: The MD said lower deposit costs reflect repricing of last year's high-rate contracts. Bulk deposits rose as post-FCNR-B liquidity made bulk rates lower than retail. Repricing over the next two quarters will be at lower rates.","Rate sensitivity: About 43% of loans are EBLR-linked and reprice immediately, MCLR is around 5% and fixed around 47%. The MD expects \"a positive impact for every rate hike\". Each basis point of yield equals about INR1 to INR1.1 crore of MTM.","Treasury impact: The CFO said the AFS reserve had a INR43 crores debit balance at 30 September, with a quarter impact of around INR52 crores to INR53 crores.","Capital position: Capital adequacy is 19.40%. The Board permitted INR500 crores of Tier 2 bonds. The MD said \"we do not foresee any major capital requirements at this point of time.\""],"pipeline":["Branch and hiring expansion: The MD said around 11 branches opened in the last two quarters, with hiring additions. Opex growth \"will be lower than the loan growth\", and cost-to-income should come down toward 50%.","Technology and AI: The MD said no \"major technological overhaul\" is needed, only refinement under the \"next-gen bank\" tagline. Artificial intelligence will get more focus to improve process efficiency.","Three-year roadmap: Asked for a three-year roadmap, the MD said \"We'll give you shortly or maybe it should be available very shortly.\"","MSME and mid-corporate verticals: The MD said verticals created earlier, mid-corporate and MSME credit segmentation, \"should fire up going forward\"."],"commitments":[{"c":"ROA target","s":"Medium term 1.25%–1.30%; long term 1.5%","t":"Medium term; long term","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"NIM trajectory","s":"3.36%–3.38% short term; 3.45%–3.50% over 12 months","t":"Short term; \"12 months or four quarter\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Cost-to-income ratio","s":"Coming down to 50% \"going forward\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Tier 2 bonds","s":"Board permitted INR500 crores; no equity raise planned","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Loan and deposit growth","s":"15%–16% maintained; \"higher bucket\", around 16%","t":"FY27","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ROA near-term step-up","s":"To \"1.20% and 1.25% levels very shortly\"","t":"\"in a year itself\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Opex growth","s":"Lower than loan growth","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Gold loan share","s":"From 25% to 30%–32% immediately; 35% first phase","t":"\"immediately\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Disclosure additions","s":"Loan book classification and fee detail to be provided","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"TWO recovery run-rate","s":"INR50 crores to INR55 crores to continue; then decline","t":"Coming quarters; \"four to five quarters or six quarters\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Three-year roadmap","s":"To be provided","t":"\"very shortly\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Corporate mix target","s":"Corporate around 35%, RAM around 65%","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Hardening bond yields create MTM losses of about INR1 to INR1.1 crore per basis point. Rate-cycle effects on deposit repricing and the ECL transition also flagged.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026, p1 header). Guidance p4, p8, p17; K1 p4, p17; K2 p3, p9, p17; K3 p2, p19; K4 p3; K5 p1, p5, p18; R1 p8–9, p18, p20; R2 p8, p10; R3 p7, p8; R4 p6; R5 p3, p12; P1 p12, p15; P2 p15, p17; P3 p7, p16–17; P4 p19; P5 p5; I1 p5, p9; I2 p11; I3 p12; I4 p18; C1 p3, p17; C2 p4, p17; C3 p5; C4 p5; C5 p8; C6 p9; C7 p9; C8 p10; C9 p11; C10 p12, p15; C11 p12; C12 p9; M1–M2 p1; M3 p1, p4; M4 p1, p3; M5–M7 p1; M8–M12 p1; M13 p1; M14 p8, p10; M15 p9, p20; M16 p7; M17 p12; M18 p19; M19 p4; M20 p1; Risks p16–17, p19. Omitted as garbled: credit cost \"slightly higher than 0.09% to 0.12%\", yields \"towards… 10.40% [Phonetic]\", and the EBLR T-bill/repo split \"20% to 23%\". Not used: the analyst-cited 60 bps calculated credit cost and the INR592 crore to INR732 crore operating profit."},
  {"name":"The Federal Bank Limited","q":"Q2 FY27","date":"16 Oct 2026","tone":"Neutral","guidance":"Maintained: mid-teens loan growth; NIM up 5–6 bps per quarter on average; ROA up 3–4 bps per quarter. Cost-to-income about 52% (ED Venkat) or \"54-ish\" (MD).","metrics":[{"m":"Net profit","v":"₹1,380 crore, +45.1% YoY, +17.7% QoQ"},{"m":"EPS","v":"₹22.26, +44.34% YoY, +16.25% QoQ"},{"m":"Operating profit","v":"₹2,225.37 crore, +35.35% YoY, +17.29% QoQ"},{"m":"Net interest income","v":"₹3,153.4 crore, +26.38% YoY, +7% QoQ"},{"m":"NIM","v":"3.32%, down 1 bp QoQ, up 26 bps YoY"},{"m":"Fee income","v":"₹1,167 crore, +31.86% YoY; 1.11% of average assets"},{"m":"Cost-to-income","v":"52%, down 196 bps YoY, 42 bps QoQ"},{"m":"Total deposits","v":"₹3,42,693 crore, +18.6% YoY, +7% QoQ"},{"m":"CASA","v":"₹1,09,803 crore, +22.56% YoY; ratio up 103 bps YoY"},{"m":"NRE and NRO deposits","v":"₹1,19,055 crore, +26.6% YoY, +13.25% QoQ"},{"m":"Gross advances","v":"₹3,01,687 crore, +21% YoY, +7.27% QoQ"},{"m":"Cost of deposits","v":"Down 3 bps QoQ, 39 bps YoY"},{"m":"Average CD ratio","v":"86.22%"},{"m":"GNPA","v":"1.32%, down 20 bps QoQ, 31 bps YoY"},{"m":"NNPA","v":"0.17%"},{"m":"PCR","v":"86.78% excluding technical write-off; 94.4% including"},{"m":"Credit cost","v":"0.31%, down 10 bps QoQ, 19 bps YoY"},{"m":"ROA","v":"1.32%, up 10 bps QoQ (ED); MD: \"about 1.33%\""},{"m":"ROE","v":"13.57%"},{"m":"Book value and gearing","v":"₹266.32 per share; gearing 16.8%"}],"takeaways":["FCNR window weighed on NIM: The MD said the special-window FCNR raise \"of course\" impacted NIM. He declined to quantify it: \"That is a slippery slope of calculation.\" Deployment details are for one-on-one discussion.","MFI acquisition question declined: Asked about acquiring an MFI, the MD said \"there is nothing to discuss right now, so we do not discuss\". Acquisitions must be accretive, and he prefers a \"single-pull strategy\" over \"big-bang\".","One-off ESOP accounting change: From April 1, 2026, all ESOPs moved to fair-value accounting. This added ₹32.7 crore to employee cost, a ₹24.5 crore PAT impact (ED Venkat). The MD called it a one-time impact.","Rate hike reprices assets first: After the October repo hike, the externally benchmarked loan book will reprice ahead of deposits. Management expects deposit competition to intensify once liquidity normalises.","Lending yields have bottomed: The MD said \"last quarter we saw the bottom of that cycle\" for lending yields. This quarter's dip was \"more of the FCNR impact\"."],"revenue":["Segment loan growth: Gold loans grew 37.13% YoY, personal loans 32.21%, credit cards 28.27%, commercial banking 25.46%, CV/CE 21.74%, corporate 17.19%, business banking 12.39% and retail 17.92%.","Current accounts rebound: Current-account deposits grew 14.04% QoQ and 33% YoY after Q1's seasonal dip. Savings grew close to 4% QoQ. Average CASA crossed one lakh crore during the quarter.","Fee income broad-based: The ED said fee growth came from \"almost every line\" of fee income. The FCNR component \"is not that significant\". Last quarter's fees were seasonally low against a strong March base.","Gold loan pricing and LTV: Gold loan LTV rose from about 55 to about 60–61. Rate actions were taken, but PSU banks charge less than 9%. A higher-yield income-generating gold loan launched in Q2.","Loan book rate mix: The MD said 33% of the book is fixed, 45% floating and the rest MCLR-linked. Gold loans are \"typically a one-year kind of product\" that reprices as it matures."],"profit":["Deposit cost decline slowing: Cost of deposits fell 3 bps QoQ, against 22 bps in Q1. The ED said the easing-cycle benefit \"has now passed through to our liability book\".","Opex investment continues: The MD said opex rose from branches, people, a new sales channel and technology. \"Do not bake in benefits on our OPEX\"; investment continues \"for a couple of years\".","Asset quality, no stress seen: GNPA and NNPA are at decade lows. The MD said \"we have not seen any impact on our asset quality\" from inflation. Agriculture and microfinance books were held back.","Liquidity and CD bands: The ED said LCR target band is 110 to 120, and the CD ratio stays within the bank's own band.","ECL impact unchanged: An analyst cited the earlier ECL transition impact of less than 2% of net worth. The MD said the expected credit-loss impact \"does not change\" with higher G-Sec yields."],"pipeline":["Board changes: Virat Diwanji joins the board. Rajesh Varrier joins as Independent Director, bringing technology and AI experience. Ramanand Mundkur steps down after five years.","NRI franchise and wealth: The MD said the bank remains committed to covering its NRI franchise \"holistically\", including building its wealth business.","Branches, technology, sales channel: The ED said the bank will invest in technology, new branch openings and other initiatives. The MD cited a new sales channel and additional people.","Medium-yield asset focus: The MD said medium-yielding assets remain the focus. Personal loans and business banking now show traction, while risk-adjusted margins improved every quarter for four to six quarters."],"commitments":[{"c":"Loan growth","s":"\"Mid-teens\" growth rate guidance; \"We continue to maintain that guidance\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"NIM improvement","s":"5–6 bps per quarter on average, not linear; \"We are still striving to get that number\"","t":"For the year","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"ROA improvement","s":"3–4 bps per quarter, \"may not be linear every quarter\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Cost-to-income","s":"ED: \"operate in the range of 52%\"; MD: \"about 54-ish… around that\"","t":"\"for the time being\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Opex investment phase","s":"Invest \"for a couple of years\"; \"do not bake in benefits on our OPEX\"","t":"\"a couple of years\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"CASA guidance","s":"\"There is no change to our guidance on CASA\"","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"LCR band","s":"Operate within 110 to 120","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Retirement cost estimate","s":"Better estimate of actuarial and retirement cost impact","t":"\"over the next quarter or so\"","st":"Not tracked"},{"c":"Wealth business build","s":"Committed to covering NRI franchise holistically, including wealth","t":"No date","st":"Not tracked"}],"risks":"Management flagged higher inflation and tighter policy, and said it is watching El Niño, geopolitics and interest-rate outcomes.","source":"Earnings call transcript (Q2 FY27, 16 Oct 2026). Guidance p4, p6, p8, p11–13; K1 p6, p8; K2 p5–6; K3 p4, p9; K4 p3–4; K5 p8; R1 p4; R2 p4; R3 p7; R4 p9–10; R5 p8–9; F1 p3; F2 p7; F3 p5; F4 p4, p8; F5 p9; P1 p2; P2 p11; P3 p4, p7; P4 p11–12; C1 p6; C2 p8, p11; C3 p5, p12; C4 p4, p13; C5 p7; C6 p4; C7 p8; C8 p9; C9 p11; M1–M3 p3; M4–M5 p3; M6–M7 p4; M8–M10 p4; M11 p4; M12 p3; M13 p4; M14–M19 p5; M18 also p12; M20 p5; Risks p5."}
];
